      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>2 f;:g mcDOUGALL'S Pate lit SELF—RAISING FLOUR unrlied. The Examiner, Saturday, September 18, 1909,</head>
        <p>2 f;:g mcDOUGALL'S Pate lit SELF—RAISING FLOUR unrlied. The Examiner, Saturday, September 18, 1909,</p>
        <p>ALL ABOUT THE IG OLD UDGET which puts the IGGEST URDENS on the ROTIDEST ReKS.</p>
        <p>their opinion upon the way in which he proposes to raise an enormous sum of money, every penny of which is to be devoted for the good of the people. (Cheers.) In past years you and I can remember Chancellors of the Exchequer who came to us inviting a contribution of large sums in order to enable them to pay for wars— wars which, after the fever had passed away and we had resumed our normal temperature, we sll saw to be acts of folly and midness—fcheers)—if not something worse. In past timfs Chancellors of the Exchequer have come to us and invited a contribution because of some shrinkage in the receipts of the treasury due, perhaps, to some great fluctuation in trade, and they have invited us to fill up the sap which this fluctuation of business had probably brought in our public revenue. Now, for the flrst time, a Chancellor of the Exchequer comes and tells you that he wants a large sum of money from you to supply not merely a passing need but to supply a permanent and ever-growing need—(chcors)—which has been deliberately crcatecl by the members of the Government. (Cheerb). He asks you for a vast sum of money in order to enable us to relieve some of the</p>
        <p>of their neighbours? If you think you would like to invite a modest contribution from these happy and fortunate fellow mortals of yours—(laughter)—so says the Chancellor of tha Exchequer. (Ch:ere). And that is the whole Budget. ( Laughter and Cheers). I tome- times hear people talk of</p>
        <p>ALTERNATIVE METHODS of getting the money. They say there is some other way. There may be, but I don't know it. (Laughter). I do know that no responsible politician,great or small, wise or foolish, well-informed or ill-informed has ever put forward any alternative method of getting this money. (Cheats). Irresponsible politicians frequently have. (Laughter). They are doing it to-night— (laughter)—on the sands at your watering places— (laughter)—at the street corners of your great towns, from barrows and from caravans. (Laughter). How do they propose to get the money? I hey do not dare—it would be as much as their lives are worth—to say that you should tax the food of the people in order to get the money. What these irresponsible gentlemen say— aye, and mind you gain many votes by saying—is that they will procure the whole of the money by Casing the unsophisticated but malevolent foreigner—(laneht er)— by laying a 10 per cent. duty upon all manufactured articles which reach your shores from beyond the sets. That is their method of securing the £13,000,000 which are necessary to satisfy the demands of the Chancellor of the Exchequer. Shall we look at this the only alternative method suggested of securing the necessary millions? What will be the effect of putting</p>
        <p>A 10 PER CENT DUTY. - upon. the whole of the manufactured articles which reach your shores? The gentleman at the street corner and upon the sands will tell you the effect will be this —you don't know it, I don't know it, but he does— (laughter)—that you will keep out one half of these manufactured articles, and you will make the goods yourselves, and that the other half will come in and yield you a revenue sufficient to supply all your needs. Last year we bought from across the seas £150,000,000 worth of manufactured articles,Divide this by two and you will find that the answer is £75,000,000. £75,000,000 worth of those articles will be kept out. You will make the goods yourselves and the other half will come in and yield a revenue su3cient to satisfy the wants of the Chancellor of the Exchequer. Shall we for a moment or two endeavour to follow the fortunes.of each of the £75,000,000 worth of manufactured articles? I will bike first the £75,000,000 worth that reach our shores and 111 • taxed. AlO per cent duty will yield you ,£7,500,000. But these gentlemen always conveniently forget the</p>
        <p>great deal better for Scotland.(Cheers.)But I know as well as you do that the customs and the habits of a great people cannot be changed in the twinkling of an eye. If the customs and habits of a people are to change, it must be by a slow, steady andgradual process, if the change is t o be permanent and abiding, and until that change takes place, I think there ii no fairer source from which the Chancellor should secure a portion of his money than from those who can afford to drink whi:ky. (Cheers.) I turn to another luxury in widespread vse—</p>
        <p>TOBACCO. - The Chancellor believes that by increasing his duty by eightpence in the £ he will secure no less than £1,900,000 f rem tobacco. I speak of this tax with great impartiality, with some ignorance, but, I hope, with some intelligence. (Langnter). lam told that if you smoke two ounces a week 3ou do fairly well. Now, I repeat I speak in ignorance upon information given to me —(laughter)—and I am opt n to correction. If you do smoke two ounces a week, then the Chan- cellor's addition to the tobacco tax will foot up to 4s. in the year, and a shrer, d Scotch ft lend of mine says that he thinks it ren-arkably good business to offer an additional contribution to the revenue d 4s. in the year on the chance of getting 53. a week when he needs it. (Loud cheers). I see that you men of Warrington, at all events, are Lot going to grudge your contribution. (Cheers). Appirently there are no Dukes in this assembly. (Laughter). It is one of the most amazing things—the way in which the common folks in this country have shouldered manfully their share of the burden—(loud cheers)—without a murmer or com- plaint, and if you figure it out you will find that in pro- portion to their income they are bearing a heavier share of the burden than the rich. (Cheers). I have now come to the last source from which the Chancellor expects to secure mmey—those happy, favoured mortals amongst us who have wealth which they have done and spent nothing to win. Who are they? The first class, even that ingenious gentleman in front could not guess. (Laughter). The first class are those to whom at the present moment we, the people of this country, grant a</p>
        <p>MONOPOLY TO SELL DRINK. We say to these people: "Gentlemen, if you conform to the law and do not allow people to get drunk on your premises and do not allow people to leave your premises in an intoxicated condition,and open and close at the prescribed hours, we will prevent others com- peting agairst you. We will give you a monopoly in the sale of drink. We will not allow the magistrates to give licences to all corners. We will tell our setvants the magistrates—(for they are your servants and agents) to keep up the value of your monopoly, to deny a licence to all who ask it, to refuse absolutely to allow people to compete against you. We will give you a monopoly which is worth to you hendreds of thousende as a body, and we will see to it that you are protected in that monopoly." I know a house in a northern county in England which cost the owner £4OO to 'build. He got a licence for the sale of drink to-day and to-morrow he went and sold his house for £4,003 with its licence. The £3,60) which is the difference between the £4ooand the £4,000 is the value of the licence, of the leave te sell drink which the community gave to that men. (Cheers). We are the only oh ilized country in the world which has let this monopoly value slip clean through our finsers into the hands of private individuals. ("Shame.") Yes, sir, it is a shame, but we are going to take uncommonly good care that we shall repair that omission. (Loud cheers.) We are now, at a long distance of time, going to follow the example of every other civilized country in the world, and of our own self-governing colonies, and we are going to invite—l use no stronger term—a moderate contri- bution from these people who are in possession of a monopoly which we have created, in order to-minister to our needs. (Cheers.) For once in a way we, the people of this country, are going to take a portion of our own self-created wealth in order to meet the needs of the community. (Cheers.) The Chancellor expects that he will secure from that source £2,600,000 in the present year, and I am pretty sure he will get all his money. (Cheers). I have now come to the last source to which the Chancellor turns—a source from which be is going to invite this year a modest contribution, next year a larger contribution—(hear, bear)—the year after that a larger contribution still—(cheers)—nntil by and by you will sea, or I am very f tr cheated, that from this source he will secure many millions without, I will not say doing a particle of injustice to anybody, but with- out inflicting the smallest hardship on a single man or woman in the country. (Cheers) Where, you ask me, is that El Dorado? The answer is:</p>
        <p>needs of his neighbours out of the wealth which is derived exclusively from them. (Lord cheers.) We come lastly to the undeveloped land tax—a modest half-penny in the £on its capital value. What is unde- veloped land? That is a fair question ; the answer to which I do not believe one in a thousand of Lord Rosebery's hearers at Glasgow last Friday could under- stand or could tell you—at least I judge that from the speech. Now undeveloped land is not agricultural land. Undeveloped land is not pastoral land. Undeveloped land is not land which is this moment covered by buildings, either dwelling- houses, factories or workshcps. -Undeveloped land is not land upon which men have made improvements "What in the world, then" von say "is undeveloped land 7" Well, it is land which is quite suitable for being built upon, but is not built upon now, and the owner of that land is in no hurry whatever to build upon it. As long as he refuses to build upon it, as long as he keeps it out of the market, you say to him: "We will neither tax you, nor tate you upon your land. See you to it, sir, that you make no use of our land. See you to it that you keep your i neighbours off. That s all that is necessary, and we will see to it that you are not taxed and that you are not rated." That is your present system. I think that is</p>
        <p>• • . . • ". • . • . - • • - , "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>MR. URE AT WARRINGTON. A CROWDED AODIENCE APPROVES</head>
        <p>MR. URE AT WARRINGTON. A CROWDED AODIENCE APPROVES</p>
        <p>NEW TAXES.</p>
        <p>The great demonstration which was held in the Parr Mall on Monday evening was a magnificent cul- mination of the successful campaign which the War- rington Progressive Labour League has waged on behalf of the Budget and Free Trade. It was also an eloquent proof that Mr. Lloyd George's democratic measure has aroused the enthusiasm of all sections of the progressive forces in 'Warrington, as throughout the kingdom. Discussion of the Budget has only increased its popularity, for the community has not been slow to realize that the Chancellor of the Exchequer's proposals apportion the burden of taxa- tion according to the ability to pay, and open up a grand prospect of much-needed social reform. Monday evening's meeting was one of the finest political gatherings over seen in Warrington. The Parr Hall was crowded in every part,and many people had to be content with standing ro An. The Budget received .unanimous and hearty support, for through- out the meeting there was not a single dissentiont voice—on the contrary the speeches were freely punctuated with cheers. The Right Hon. Alexander Ure, K.C., M.P., the Lord Advocate, was the principal speaker. He has won fame as one of the most brilliant exponents of land reform and a stalwart champion of the Budget. His exposition of the Chancellor's proposals was singularly lucid,every point being dealt with in an able, homely and convincing way which met with general acceptance. The speech was illumined with many flashes of humour and it was delivered with a distinctness and -deliberation which enabled everyone clearly to follow the line of -argument. Not a point was lost on the audience and when after speaking for abont an hour and a quarter the Lord Advocate intimated his intention of conclud- ing there were loud cries of "Go on." The speech was one of the most masterly contributions .to the Budget discussion which has yet been forthcoming, and Warringtonians are indeed fortunate in having been .favoured with a isit from such an eminent advocate of progressive principles and ideals' The meeting was presided over by Mr. E. Wynne- Jones (President of the. Progressive Labour League) and amongst those present on the platform were : Mr. A. H. C'rosfield, M.P., Mr. C. E. Parker (Presi- (lent of the Liberal Association), Mrs. Parker, 3liss Parker, Aldermen Tinnion; Burton and James Evans, Councillor and Mrs. Wilkinson, Councillors Skelland and Locker, Rev. E. B. and Miss Keeling, Revs. C. Harvey .Cook, H. Wall, R. E. Letheren, T. Miller, J. B. I3uglass, J. .L. Milne and J. S. Hughes, Mr. and Mrs. James Dodds, Mr. and Mrs. C, Jackson Holmes, Mr. and Mrs. J. Upson, Mr. and Mrs. E. V. Walker, Mr. and Mrs. W. J. Walker, Mr. and Mrs. J. Howes, Mr. and Mrs. Withinshaw, Mrs. Walker, Mrs. Osborne, Mrs. P. Magian, Mr, and Mrs. Joseph Owen, Miss Waywell, Miss Rowlands, Messrs. T.Pemberton, W. H. Sherburn, E. J. Guest, H. W. Guest, J. Longland, Joseph Bailey, Thomas Adams, W. Bibby, J.T. Barton, W. Beswick, J. Barlow, N. Cook, W.Clegg, A. CorneY, T. Critchley, W. Clatworthy,W.H. Dutton, E.England, W. Furness, B. Farrar, John Griffiths, Charles Healey, E. G. Hughes, W. Hayes, J. W. Jeffery, . J. Jarman, T. Locker, E. J. Mitchell, P. O'Brien, P. Peacock, A. Rathbone, T. .Smith, J. Vose, James Woods. John Waywell, Joseph Wilkinson, G. Weaver; J. Morton and J. Howes, jun. (secretaries of the League).</p>
        <p>• THE CHAIRMAN'S SPEECII. The CHAIRMAN said his first duty wos to extend a welcome to Mr. Ure. (Cheers.) He was doing a splendid work thrdughont the country and he was making hundreds of converts to the Budget as he went along. (Chesrs.) Even Lord Rosehery had a good word to say for Mr. Ure. (Hear, hear.) The Lague had had a splendid series of meetings during the summer months and they had been told by men who had spent long years in the political world that the meetings had never been surpassed any- where. Those meetings had been held in favour of Free Trade and of that great democratic Budget. He thought he was perfectly just;fied in linking those two together for that Budget was a TRIUMPH FOR FREE TRADE and it would mean the death of Tariff Reform. (Cheers.) That Budget was a lessrn to Tariff Reformers in Free Trade finance. Politicians could no longer go about the country shaking their heads at the lack of money. That Budget had shown that it would prove equal to the requirements of the State. TLe Free Trade policy of the Government had justified itself by results. Nothing had made so vast and permanent changes in the life and the condition of the people as the repeal of the Corn Laws— (applsuse)—and the abolition of every vestige of Protection. The Liberal Party stood for Free Trade. It stood for somethine more; it stood for freedom in land. (Cheers.) Free Trade was only a half-won battle with- out freedom in laud—freedom to produce. Now they were not only going to defend but to attack and they were aoing step by step until they had won a double and a final victory over monopoly of every kind. (Applause.) lie saw on a placard : "Lord Rosebery smashes the Budget." Had Lord Resebery smashed the Budget? (Cries of "No! ") Could Lord Rosebery smash the Budget? (Cries of "No !") Could all the Dukes and the Earls stna-11 the Budget? (Cries of "No!") Ile did not see that Lord Rosebery had given any satisfaction to anybody. 'I he Tories did not seem to be satisfied. He said that the Duke and Earls were centres of employment.and bounty. What kind of bounty? (Laughter.) They were withdrawing that. No more grouse and no more subscriptions to football clubs. (A voice : "Poorfellows.") Countinuing, be asked if we wanted their bounty. (Cries of "No.") Did we want their charity? ("No.") We wanted a State in which there would be no room for charity. (Hear, hear.) We wanted a State in which every wan would be sturdy, strong and free. Lord Rosebery said that no property was safe. Did they not think that there was too much said about property? Were human lives and happiness nothing? Were the evils that existed to go on for ever ? Property was the thing that stood in the way of reform. A high value was set upon property and a low one on human life. They wanted to change that and make human life come first mad property second. (Loud applause.) There was nothing Tevolutionary in the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LAND TAX PROPOSALS.</head>
        <p>LAND TAX PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>They were the oldest proposals known to science and political economy. Land values were created by the people. Remove our population, stop our indu;try, and where would the land values be? They said tbat the present land system must come to an end. They said that it was wrong for people to be able to withhold land from use. If they were to go on monopolising land they would soon monopolise life itself. After all, the Budget would go thior.gb. (Applause). The Lords would not stop it, but if they did the people would stop the Lords. (Cheers). They did not object to the Lords wincing, but when they came to mince, the people would mince in turn. He believed that Liberals and Labour would unite on this great question. They would follow the Gove:nmcnt with all courage. Progressive opinion of all shades was upon their side. The people were prepared to act together. The progressive forces would unite to build up a country in which all men would be free to thrive in the air of justice and of virtue and. of truth. (Loud cheers).</p>
        <p>MR. lIRE'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>MASTERLY EXPOSITION OF THE BUDGET. TARIFF REFORM FALLACIES EXPOSED. The Right Hon. A. Um:, on rising to address the meeting, had a magnificent reception, the audience rising and cheering for several minutes. He raid : I am overwhelmed by the warmth of your welcome, coming from people who, as I gather, have already been surfeited with political information. Your appetites are apparently wholly unappeaf•able. (Laughter.) But there never were times like these, and there never will be, probably, for we were told on Friday afternoon, or rather my fellow citizens in Glasgow were told, that this was the MOST REMARKABLE BUDGET that had ever been presented to any Parliament in the history of the world. I quite agree with that state- ment. (Laughter and cheers.) It is one of the few statements in that speech with which I do agree. (Laughter.) But even the extremely keen insight of Lord Rosehery did not etable him to discern the quali- ties of this Budget which wake it the most remarkable that has ever been presented to any Parliament. It is not because of the amount of money which we ask, nor is it on account of the taxes which we seek to impose. These taxes, with one modest exception, are very old and familiar friends. The reason why this Budget is so remarkable is this : that now, for the first time in the history of this country, our Min,ster of Finance takes the people into his fullest confidence and asks them to express</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>DEEP-SEATED EVILS</head>
        <p>DEEP-SEATED EVILS</p>
        <p>. _ which have grown up around our vast and complex industrial system. We ask you to supply us with mohey in order that we rosy do something to develop the far too long neglected resources of our own country. (Cheers). Vhen a Chancellor of the Exchequer comes and asks yon to foot a bill for thirteen Millions of money then he must show you two things—first,that the money is going to be wisely expended, and second, that he is raising the money in the right way. (Cheers). You and I will lift not one-linger to assist him to procure his money unless we are satisfied that he will make good use of it when he gets it, and you and I will not trouble ourselves enquiring how the money is going to be raised until we are satisfied that it will be well spent. He who assails our Budget has an easier task. He must only make out one or other of two things—he can either attack our expenditure as foolish and unwise, or be can admit that our expenditure is wise but challenge our method of raising the money. I know just as well as you do that according to what are called ' the rulc; of the pine," mere party warfare, an Opposition is never compelled to prescribe until it is exiled in, but when the question is" How are you going to raise the money for a purpose of which we all approve ?" then the man who comes down tnl condemns the Budget must show you a better way of raising it—(loud cheers)— or for ever hold his peace. (tterowed cheers.) I who have come down here as a member of his Majesty's Government to defend the Budget—(cheets)—freely acknowledge that I must make out two things to succeed in my defence. I must satisfy you that we will spend your money when we receive it with wisdom and prudence and second, I must show you that we are going about the finding of the money in the right way and not in the wrong way (Cheers). We propose to devote a modest portion of the thirteen millions of additional taxation in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>STRENGTHENING THE NAVY.</head>
        <p>STRENGTHENING THE NAVY.</p>
        <p>Now I am speTtia;g7W—ti;7s--mOns—e-Utiel-risany men who will tell me that is not productive expenditure,that it is net expenditure which adds to the wealth of the country or which adds to the comfort and contentment of the people. My answer is" That is so," but this in our judgment is necessary expenditure, becsuse in the present temper of the naticns of the world,we regret to say in that mad competition in armsments which is at the present moment going on, it is not desirable, it is not expedient, but it is absolutely necessary that we, an island power, the greatest trading community thst the world has ever sem, must have at sea a navy invincible beyond all doubt or question. (Cheers). I may offer my economic friends - a crumb of comfort. We do not buy our ships, our guns or our gun mountings from Germany or the United States. We make them 'ourselves and we make them better than any other country in the.world. (Cheers and a Voice: "And cheaper.") Quite right. If they are better they are always cheaper. (Laughter and cheers). We give employment, if not productive, useful and honourable employment to hundreds of thousands of the highest skilled and best paid artizaos in the world. (Cheers). The second item of expenditure which we ask you to supply us with money to defray is the large sum of 29,000,000 at least which we need to enable us to pay</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>MODEST PITT...NCES TO OUR AGED POOR.</head>
        <p>MODEST PITT...NCES TO OUR AGED POOR.</p>
        <p>(Loud and prolonged -FrejOTc-a to find that our request for that money meets with your ready and cheerful approval. (Cheers.) I take it that every man and woman in this room will agree with me when I say that those millions are the best spent money in the British Emp're. (Cheers.) For think what you do with them. You relieve from want hundreds of thousands of the most deserving subjects of the King. You remove the dark shadow of the workhouse from the path of these aged people. You do more. You lift a heavy burden—a burden, I admit, often cheer- fully borne—off tho shoulders of many a deserving and dutiful son and daughter, and so you relieve a portion of their modest incomes to bestow on their own house- holds. (Hear, hear.) We want money from you to enable us to take time by the forelock and to see to it when times of depression come upon us again, as come they must, and come they will to every great industrial and commercial community, we shall have tsken pre- cautions to guard against the evils which come upon our workpeople when those bad times come upon us. We want you to assist us to set up a great system of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>STATE INS URANC E</head>
        <p>STATE INS URANC E</p>
        <p>against sickness, invalidity and bad times; a scheme of insurance to which employers and workmen will contribute, and to which the State will contribute to complete the fabric. We want some money to enable us to discharge a duty, far too long neglected by the wealthiest and the most highly civilized country in the world—l mean, to see to it that no subject of the King, man or woman, shall ever be compelled to work for let's than a living wage. (Cheers). We invite you to give us the money that we may hold out a helping hand to those for whom in the past there has been no eye of pity and no hand to help. We want you to give us some money to enable na to make a beginning, far too long deferred, to develop and to improve the natural resources of our own country—(cheers)—money which you cannot expect vrt individual or any company of individuals to expend, money which moat be expended by the State if this great improvement in our natural resources is to be accomplished, for you cannot expect assistance to agriculture, assistance specially to affores- tation, assistance to enable our agricultural population to co-operate with one another, and to fold a ready, an an easy and a rapid market for their produce, unles the State itself lends a band to its own subjects. (Cheers). These ara the purposes and ohjEc's to which we propose to devote the millions which we ask you to raise. Do you approve of these objects or do you not? (Loud cheers). If you do, shall sse now? turn and see from whence we are to procure the money. How would you procure it now? There are some bud- ding Chancellors of the Exchequer here. (A voice : "Froth the land "). No, you wtn't get it all from that quarter. (Laughter). Tbat gentleman lives in a period two centuries hack apparently, before we bad become the great commercial and industrial nation that we are. No, we must look to other sources. (A voice: "Let us all contribute ") That, sir, is the right answer to the question, and I am going to invite you to look for a moment to the conditions of the problem, because after all, it is a question of the distribution of the burden. Here is a certain sum of money—.Cl3,ooo,ooo— has to be raised from the people who are resident within a certain specified area—the United Kingdom of Great Britain and lreland—and the only question which this Budget or which any Budget raises is: How are lie going to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>APPORTION THE BURDEN</head>
        <p>APPORTION THE BURDEN</p>
        <p>among those people? Are you going to lay the heavy burden on the broad hack and ease the burden off the back that is least able to bear it, or ars you going to do do the reverse—to increase the burden on tha backs of those who are least able to bear it and to lighten the burden on the backs of those who are best able to 'mar it? I think there is but one answer to that gin ,:tion—to see what is the character and quality of the community from which you ara going to raise your .£13,600,C00. You have a community with 41 &amp;Mons of population, a community of enornious wealth, whose weelth is every year increasing faster than its population, a community which at this moment while I speak has hived-np savings, placed heyond ell risk and danger, amounting to no less than 12,000 trillion pounds—l reel off that figure, but I do not realise what it means—(laughter)—the millionaires who surround rue perhaps do ; (renewed laughter) —they will supply my deficiencies. (Laughter)- - a community which in this present year of grace enjoys an income of no less than 1,800 million pounds, steadily increasing mind you ; a community, I rejoice to think, whose public finances are at this moment on a sounder, healthier and more more secure basis than the public finances of any country in the world—(cheers)---a community whose vast and fabulous wealth is distributed so unequally that you find vast riches side by side with the deepest destitu- tion ; s community not exceptionally rich in natural resources; a community which requires to buy from across the seas one half of its food supplies, and to pur- chase by far the greater portion of its raw material to feed its great industries from countries far distant. That is the community from which you are invited to raise £13,030,000 in this year of grace. How will you do it? You can, if you like —after all you are the sole masters—secure all your money by levying a heavy tax upon everything which men need and use in their daily lives. You can, if you like, secure all your money by hampering every industry, by hindering every trade in the country, by harrassing your great commerce. Every penny can be raised in that fashion, if you please. Will you raise it in that way ? (Cries of "No.") So says the Chancellor of the CI -ncellor of the Exchequer. (Cheers.) Will you raise son,e of your £13,C00,000 by inviting contributions from men who are Nvell able to pay you without stinting themselves of a single luxury, a single comfort in life, not to say anything of the necessities of life? Do you think yet' would like to have some of your £13,000,00 from that quarter? (" Yes.") So says the Chancellor of the Exchequer. (Cheers). And do you know, I rather think he will get his money there. (Laughter and cheers). What would you say now to procuring a certain portion of your £13,01'0,000 by taxes levied not upon the necessities of life, but on the ordinary comforts and luxuries of life ? If you think you would like to secure a potion of your money from that source, so says the Chancellor of the Exchequer. What would you say to inviting a contribution from those of your fellow-subjects who are found in possession of wealth which they have done nothing and spent nothing to gain, wealth which as come to them from the activity, the ecera, the industry, and the expenditure</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>COST .OF COLLECTION.</head>
        <p>COST .OF COLLECTION.</p>
        <p>I remember it—(laughter). Our coastline Is long ; our creeks, our bays, our hirbours and our estuaries are numerous : the smuggling instinct in us is strone— (laughter)—and apparently still lives. (Renewed laughter). We require -193 sep irate and distinct new custom house buildings; we require many battalions of custom house officers—highly paid officials; we require to set up training colleges to train them in their duty. Your new tariff will contain at the very least 1189 different articles ; your cost of collection on a very moiert estimate will be 21,000,003 a year. (Laughter). Will you now do for me a simple sum in subtraction? (Laughter). Will you take £4,000,000 the cost of col- lection from £7,500,000 the yield of the tax and if your arithmetic is as good as mine—(lsughter)—yon will find that the answer is £3,500,000. But we need £13.000,000 so that your sum like poor Lord Avebury's, will not add up. You will need to try again. (Laughter.) I will now follow the fortunes of the £75,000,000 worth of manufactured goods which your 10 per cent duty has kept out of your ports and harbours. You will sly to me, "Now our 'home manufacturer will make these goods himself." He will do nothing of the kind ; but these gentlemen do not say so and I suppose they know what they are talking about and I will assume that they are correct. I will now assume that the home manu- facturers make all the £75,000,000 worth of goods which you have kept out of your ports and harbours. Now I will ask you to reckon up with ma our gains and our losses. I will take first our gains. You have kept out the £75,000,000 worth of mannfactured goods. You will make 'these goods yourselves and you will give additional employment to all who are engaged in the I trades which make these goods. Business will be brisk, ' more bands will be engaged, more wages will be paid. Second,you will give remunerative employment to regi- ments of custom house officers. Third, you will give employment—l will not say respectable and reputable employment—to smugglers. (Laughter.) I will ask any gentleman in this room to tell me if I have omitted any item on the gain side. If he will not speak now, let him he for ever silent. I will turn now to our losses. The goods are kept out. All the British ships mroned by British seamen, who bring thire goods to your ports and harbours are thrown idle. You have them on your hands. What will you do for them? Will you give them old age pensions? (Laughter.) Indeed you will require to do something for them because we never in this country, by an Act of Parlia- ment, deprive any man of lawful employment without offering him compensation. What will you do for the sailors and the shipowners ? Battalions of dock labourers are every day—are at this moment—engaged in unloading the foreigner's goods from your ships on to your quays. Regiments of carters are carting these goods from the quay to the warehouse. At the railway station railway servants are engaged in transporting than] across your country. All these people will be deprived of their work because you kept the foreigner's goods out. What will you do for them ? They are on your hands, and you must attend to them. Do you think now that your troubles are at an end? They are only just. beginning. The foreigner's goods are kept out. I suppose the foreigner is paid for his goods. I have never heard yet of a foreigner who sold goods and refused to take payment. (Laughter.) If you know him mention him to me and I will deal with him. (Laughter.) If the foreigner's goods do not come in we will not pay him for goods which he does not send—at least my countrymen never pay for goods they do not receive. ,(Lauehter.) Very well, you will not pry the foreigner for his .275,000,000 worth of goods which he does not send you. How do you pay the foreigner to-day for his £75,000,000 worth of goods? Do you pay him by golden sovereigns stored in ships holds? Did you ever sea a ship full of golden sovereigns? (Laughter). Do you pay him by English bank notes packed up in bales like cotton bales? (Laughter). You do not. How do you pay the malevolent foreigner? (A voice: "Exchange ''). The gentleman in the front knows very well. He will tell you that (just now—while I am speaking—busy hands of men and women are engaged in making goods in your own factories and workshops which are sent across the sea to pay the foreigner for the goods he sends here. (Cheers). You deprive these people of their. employment. You have kept the fortigner's pods out and these people are not engaged in making goods to pay him for goods which he does not send. They are on your hands. Will you supply them with pensions too? I think you will need to do it, because again I remind you that you are not entitled in this country, by Act of Parliament, to deprive men and women of their lawful occupations without giving them money compensation in return. (Laughter.) Now I hope you see, although- the gentle- man on the sands, in the caravan, or on the top of his tub at the street corner will never see, that your LAST STATE WILL BE MUCH WORSE THAN YOUR FIRST if you succeeded in keeping the foreigner's goods from your shores. (Cheers.) There is then no alternative, NO OTHER OR BETTER WAY</p>
        <p>by which to find the money than the way pf,inted out to us by the Chancellor of the Exchequer. (Cheers). Shall we see, then, whence come our £13,000,000? The Chancellor of the Exchequer believes that this year be will be enabled to secure three-and-a-halt millions of his money by levying an additicnal twopence in the pound of incorno tax on these who are receiving safe, steady, secure, unearned income—he leaves the earned incomes, the income men gain by the sweat of their brow end the labour of their hands end their brims, untouched —by levying an additional twopence in the 2 on unearned income and an additional tax of sixpence in the £ on those who are burdened with incomes of £5,000 a year and opaards. (Cheers.) I am not going to pause, and neither are you, to shed a tear over the sorrows of the gentleman burdened with the £5,000 a year who is asked to offer 2.L.d. in the £ extra to the needs of the country and still less shall we bestow our sympathy upon those in receipt of £lB,OOO a year who are invited to sub- scribe fivepenee in the £ extra in order to minister to the needs of the community. (Cheer.) The Chancellor at the Exchequer believe 3 that by steepening—aat is rtn expression which is dear to the heart of Lord Ilosebery—(laughter)--he loves to dwell upon it— that by STEEPENING THE DEATH DUTIES</p>
        <p>he will be enabled to secure £2,850,000 this year and a good deal more in the years that are to come. -(Cheers). Well, opinions differ about the death duties. I for my part have no sympathy at all for my executors— (laughter)—but some people have. There is no accounting for tnstes in that mattar—(laughter)—and those whose feelings are a little beyond their control, when they come to think of the death duties will, if they take my advice, turn back to the Parliament:ry debates for the summer of the year 1891 and if they find that their vocalulary is quite insucficient to enable them now to give free vent to their feelings, they will find in those debates a rich storehouse o? epithets, a whole armonry of robust language which will far more than suffice to meet all their needs. (Laughter). We have now 6,1 millions ; we are half way no the end of our journey. Now shall we turn to the luxuries from whence the Chancellor hopes to secure a portion of the money needed. A Chancellor on the hunt for money never thinks of taxing luxuries which are in use by a very few men or women, and that is the reason why Chancellors decline to touch French millinery or ladies' silks. (Laughter). As businessmen know, if von tax thf se things the demand shrinks and the cost of collection more than eats up the ield, and that is very bad business. A Chancellor on the hunt for money—and, after all, a Budget is simply an instrument for extracting money from your pockets and putting it in the pocket of the Chancellor of the Exchequer, nothing more or less—always selects a luxury or comfort in widespread use, and the eyes of the present Chancellor have, resteJ upon whisky and tobacco. (Laughter) He thinks he sees hTs way by levying an additionpl 3s. 9d. per gallon upon spirits to secure an addition to his revenue of £1,660.000. That is a good round figure I hope and believe that him expectations of the yield from that tax will be dis- appointed. (Cheers.) I know very little of your customs and habits. Judging from the customs and habits of my own peeple; Iwill,say that if the</p>
        <p>SPIRIT TAX</p>
        <p>were to lield nothing front Scotland, it would be a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>LAND.</head>
        <p>THE LAND.</p>
        <p>It is around the land taxes, as you know, that the tempest has raged and the heathen have raged so furiously during the past few mouths. (Laughter.) Why, you ask, does your respected member, Mr. Crosfleld—(load cheers)—sit up all day and all night fighting for the land taxes wbich will yield you at the most this year nn more than half a million towards your 13 millions? The reason I will give you is'that in these land taxes is embodied a principle which is capable of remarkably wide application and very indefinite extension. (Cheers.) I am saying nothing very extraordinary or alarming when T say that. I am not as a land reformer keen for any taxation at all. I would not tax anything if I could help it. It is a dis- agreeable necessity, and I tell you frankly that in my land reform I am much more eager to untax building, machinery and improvements than I am to tax land. (Cheers.) This is, I admit, an obscure, difficult and com- plex subject, Much misunderstood by many people who ought to know. I do not say it is misrepresented intentionally, but it is</p>
        <p>GROTESQUELY_MISIIRI4tESE:s'TED</p>
        <p>and I repeat to-day what I said on Saturday afternoon —that the most attentive student of Lord Rosebery's lengthy speech coutd not, if he had not read or been familiar with the Finance Bill, have discovered from that speech that the Government proposes to tax land, bare land alone, apart altogether from buildings, improvements, machinery, structure of all kinds, or even apart altogether from its agricultural value. Now shall I turn and in as few simple and plain words as I can command, tell you what are the threa new taxes. There is first the increment duty. Increment is als rue and unfamilar word, but increment duty means this, and only this: The Chancellor of the Exchequer says: When my Bill passes "—as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PASS IT WILL</head>
        <p>PASS IT WILL</p>
        <p>—(loud cheers)—" I would like to find out the value of the land which every landowner in ithis • country possesseA. I don't worry about- his buildings . I care nothing about his improvements, fixed machinery, or structure of any kind. What I want to find out is the value of the bare land and when I -have found out that value I will write it down in a book—(laughter)—and if at some future period that man sells his land, bare land mind you, at an increased price, then I will invite him to give me a portion of that increased price for the service of the community." (Cheers). I offer a simple illustration. If at the time when the valuation is made a man owns land worth £lOO, the Chancellor records that in his book and when that man at. some future period sells his land for, shall we say. £2OO, the Chancellor will say to him : "Sir, I observe that you have made a profit of £lOO upon the increase in the value of your land,.—bare land. Be good enough, sir, to put LSO of that in your pocket and offer me £20." (Laughterand cheers). That is the increment duty. Now for the reversion - duty. That again is an unfamilar word. You in England are accustomed, I believe, to a system under which the man who owns land gives a long lease of it to another man on which to build. Now we do not in Scotland enceurage or like this system of long leases. We abhor it. A long lease lasts thirty, forty, fifty, sixty, ninety, ninety-nice, or even 999 years I am told, but you know a Scotchman never likes to part with his property even at the end of 99 years. (Laughter). You do not worry about that. (Laughter). " But "says the Chancellor "when my Bill passes I would like to find out what was the value of your land at the time when you or your predecessor united a long lime of it and when I have found out what the value of your land was when you or your ancestor gave a long lease of it, I will write that down in a book— (laughter)—and, if when the lease comes to en end, and that land falls back into your hands again, I find as I think T will find, that it has increased in value. I will invite you to give a portion of that int,rease to me for the service of the State." (Cheers). Again I take a simple illustration. At the time when a man granted his long lease his land was worth £lO3. When the long lease comes to an end and the land falls back into his hands or the hands of his successor, the land is worth £2OO-1 am tauing,you see,very moderate figures; you will fill ur all the blanks for yourselves— (laughter)—the Chancellor says : " Sir, here is your land increased in value—bare land, mind you, increased in value by £lOO. Be good enough to put £9O of that £lOO in your pocket and give me £lO for the needs of the community." (Cheers and a Voice: "It is not enough.") That gentleman, like myself, is a very edits:lced land reformer —(laughter) —and he forgets that the vast bulk of his fellow countrymen and my countrymen are cautions, steady, slow-going people, never liking to take plunges into unknown waters with- out plumbing the depth first, and never pushing their hana out further than they can pull it back _again. (Leughter). Let me ask all hot heads in this room, like this gentleman and myself—(laughter)—to ramember that the vast bulk of our countrymen are cautious, steady, slow-going folks and that if we are MAKING A BEGINNING he and I ought to be very well content. (Hear, hear.) You ask me what right has the Chancellor of the Exchequer to take any portion of the increased value of that man's land from him? Why should that man not be allowed to put the whole increase in value in his pocket? ICIv answer is that if his land were in the wilderness it would be worth nothing at all—(laughter) —and if his land were in the middle of a sparsely peopled country it would be worth uncommonly little. But if his lend were in the midst of a busy, hustling, active, energetic, progressive community, it would be going up by leaps and bounds, mounting, mounting, mounting in value until it would cost you a whole fortune to buy a few square -Yards of bare land. (Cheers.) Whence does its value come.? Not from any- thing the owner of the land :does or spends. He may be at the Antipodes all the time. He may be asleep all the time. He mav be on an extended foreign tour to distant countries all the time. And while be is absent, while he is aleep, while be is bestowing rota passilg thought on his land,while he is spending not one sixpence upon it, it is steadily mounting. mounting' mounting, in value, owing to the sleepless activity of his neighbours all round. (Cheers.) I say it is</p>
        <p>. JUST AND *AIR that that man should offer a modest contribution to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WHOLLY WRONG.</head>
        <p>WHOLLY WRONG.</p>
        <p>It is a direct encouragement to amen to make either no use of his land or a poor use of it, and it is a direct discouragement to him to make good use of his land, to put it to its best possible use. How' shall we cure that? The Chancellor says: "Sir, I wasld like to find out what is the market value of that vacant laud of yours—the price that you could get to-morrow if you put it in tbe market,—and when I have found that out I will write that down in my book—(laughter)—and every year that you keep that land out of the niarket I will ask you to give me a modest, a very modest coatrioution of one half-penny in the 2 to rainister to the needs of the community." (Cheers.) The Chancellor thinks that by that means we will secure some revenue and I think he will secure a good deal more that he expects. I have some idea of the value of vacant land la this country —(cheers)—and when the valuation is made there will be eye-openings to a great many people who at this present moment are sceptics. But that is a prophecy and it is safe not to prophesy unless you knew. (Laughter.) The Chancellor believes that he will do even better than that. He believes that these gentle- men who are waiting for their price—a price ten, twenty, thirty, fifty, aye a hundred times more than they gave for it, a price which cornea to them exclusively from the expenditure and the needs of their neighbours—will have tlieir footsteps quickened a little. They will come into the market with their land, tumbling over one another in competition— (cheers)—and part with their land and we shall have</p>
        <p>AN END TO OVERCROWDING, an end to the curi.ing of the growth and expansion of a progressive community, an end to dear houses—which always follow dear land and higher rents which always accompany expensive ground. The Chancellor thinks —and I think too—that the indirect result of that tax will be even more bensflcial than its direct result upon the welfare of our country. (Loud cheers). .A!1 that seems to you, as I have stated it, fair and reasonable enough—(hear hear)—and I assure you I have stated it with scrupulous accuracy and precision. But you ray to me : Why is it, if your proposals are so fair and just, that they have met with such savage, violent, ferocious and unreasoning opposition? That is not an easy question to answer. Upon my word, I sometimes think it is because they do not yet comprehend the nature of the land taxes end their meaning ; but that is not an easy hypothesis where yon have such clever and intelligent men as face us upon the other side of the table in the House of Commons. There is an old saying that "There are none so blind as those who do not want to see "—(langhter)—and I sometimes think the very mention of the word "land " or " land taxes "deprives some people of their reason (laughter and cheers). But when they do condesc nd to reason, as they sometimes do—(laughter)—they say to us—Lord Rosebery said it much better and more forcibly and in much more grace- ful terms than any of those gentlemen had ever done— " You are not entitled to treat, land in an exceptional way,in a different way from other commodities. Land is just the same as other commodities and you have no right to treat the owners of land as if they belong (says Lord Rosebery) to the criminal class, as if they were outcasts. You have no right to treat them as if they were the enemies of mankind to be hunted down." Well I do not want to treat them as the enemies of mankind or to hunt them down. I only want to tax them. (Laughter and chee-s). But is it true that land is the very same as other commodities? 1 think tot ; and I have ventured on ens occasion at least to give my roasons in answer to a challenge across the floor of the House of Commons. (Hear, hear.) Lest peradventure any of you who read Lord Rosebety's speech should suppose that T inadvertently let my cat out of my bag—(laughter)—or found that my cat was too large in dimensions for its bag— (laughter)—l wish to state that I have neither bag nor cat. (Laughter.) I have nothing to conceal; I made no unguarded statement. 1 said deliberately—and I repeat to-night deliberately that lsnd possesses six charactelistic features by which it differs from all other commodities that I know of. Now you are the judges. Will you listen to my enumeration of my six characteristic features and say whether or no you, who a-e after all the masters in these questions of finance— nobody else is entitled to dictate to you upon a ques- tion of finance—agree with me. First, I say that land owes its existence directly to the hand of the CI eator. Second, I say that land is strictly limited in quantity ; third, that it is absolutely essential to man's existrnce ; fourth, that land, bare land, owes none of its value to anything which the owner of the land does or spends; fifth, that it owes all its value solely and exclusively to the community—(cheers) ; sixth and lastly (but not as Lord Rosebery said on Saturday, mainly) and dearest of all to the heart of the tax collector, you cannot carry land away and .rou cannot con:eal it. (Cheers). I don't know why</p>
        <p>LORb ROSEDERY should have been so angry with me for stating that very convenient quality of land. He seems to think that I insinuated that he and his brothers landlords would, if they could, willingly evade the clutches of tbe tax collector and that we have seized upon land because they cannot carry it away. (Laughter.) I bad nothing of the kind in mind. But I readily admit that as a law officer of the Crown, part of whose official duty it has been during the past three years to see to it that the taxpayers obeyed their obligations—(liughter) —I came to know that the old familiar maxim "Honesty is the best policy " had no place in the region of taxa- tion. (Laughter.) Although my opponents in the House of Commons have not been able to take up my challenge, there are very clever and ingenious people in this room who may be able to do what those skilled gentlemen failed to do. If you know of any commcdity which possesses these six characteristic features name it to me and to-morrow I will name it to the Chancellor of the Exchequer—(laughter and cheers) —and I will be bound that in next year's Budget it will receive his attention. (Laugh- ter.) I will mention before we part from this subject, something about land which you cannot say of any other commodity. If land is required in the public interest for a public purpose and the owner of that land is unwilling to sell, we, the people of this country, by law corni,el that man to part with his land at a price which he does not name, to a person wham he may dislike, for a purpose which he may detest, at a time which is most inconvenient for him' and subject to conditiens which he cannot make but which are laid down for him in an Act of Parliament. (Hear, hear) Ores any man in his senses tell me that you could say that of consols or of railway shares? Who ever heard of an Act of Parliament passed to compel men to part with</p>
        <p>CONSOLS OR RA I LWA Y SHARES?</p>
        <p>_ And yet these are the only two things in the wide world that Lord Rosebery can suggest bear the smallest resemblance to land. I say they possess not one of the six ckaracteristic features of land, (Cheers). if I own a railway share, I think, although I may be mis- taken, that I am in my own modest way an employer of labour, and if Igo to the annual meet- ing of shareholders I think I am entitled, as an employer of labour, to scold the general manager and to speak plain words to his ttnder-officials, if as my servants they do not perform what I consider to be their duty. What does a man buy who buys a bit of vacant land? Does he give employment to anybody? Does he produce anything useful for the community? What do you buy when you btu a Lit of vacant land and keep it so? Gentlemen, you know as well as I do,that all you buy is a leeal right to exclude your neighbours from a certain defined area of God's earth and rat thing more? (Caeers.) You may, if you please, put up upon your land a board intimating that "trespassers will be prosecuted "and I will tell you as a lawyer that that is an idle precaution, for the law a ill not entitle you to prosecute any trespasser on your land. (Cheers.) Ladies and gentlemen, I think I have exhausted the Budget, and I* am sure I have exhausted you. (Cries of "No," and" Go on.") I have endeavoured to dis- close to you the main outlines of the plan by which the Chancellor of the Exchequer proposes to procide for the great, the growing, the increasing needs of the com- munity. It is a plan by which all the money can be raised without reaching on the modest sayings of a single man or woman in the community, without laying an excessive burden on the shoulders of any man or woman in the community, and what is perhaps more to the purpose, without arousing. in the heart of any of the contributors n feeling of soreness, a feeling of bitterness, a feeling that he or the class to whom he belongs has been unfairly treated. Here is a plan by which all the needs of the nation can be met, which lays no burden of any kind upon a single productive industry in the country, which gives an absolute assurance not merely to the great capitalist and the great employer of labour but to the artisan, the tradesman, the humble labouring man, not merely to the great financiers, the great bankers, and the great ship owners but to the small shopkeeper and humble folks all over our country, that so far as the Govt-roment is concerned and so far as the tax collector is concerned, each one of them as ill be allowed to prosecute his own calling, to carry on his own business without let or hindrance, will be allowed to secure for himself the fall fruits of his own industry and toil, will be allowed to secure for himself everything which his abilities and his opportunities offer. This is,' therefore, a plan which with great confidence I commend to your earnest, your thoughtful consideration—l think I may add to the favourable consideration of every man and woman in this room</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>who sincerely desires the comfort and the contentment, of their fellow countrymen. (Loud and prclongeo cheers during which Mr. Ure resumed his seat, havul; spoken for one hour and twenty minutes). MR. CROSFIELD MOVES A RESOLUTION- , Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, M.P. who was received vita great enthusiasm, moved the following resolution : "That this meet kg heartily endorses the Budget: beliering that the principle of putting the heaviest burden on the broadest back is fair and equitable, arla it pledges itself to defend the Government in its non- stituticnal struggle to uphold the right of the House of Commons to control nationel finance." (Cheeis.) Hae said that gas a resolution which he would be PI under any circumstances to mote at a time lite th which was certainly not the least memorable time we bad had in the whole political history of our country: (Hear, bear.) But he felt doubtly proud to be entrustea with the resolution on that occasion, because one lest standing on the same platform with an honoured g-, who bad not only given them a mgnificent esrositi°l"sl and explanation of the Budget but who had pera 101. s. helped to further the reforms that were embodied in (Cheers) By his brilliant gifts of intellect and bY work Mr. Ure had won a great position at the Bar Scot: land and he (Mr. Crosflold) ventured to think that bit as was the position he had already achieved, there we , yet higher places in the State. which in good dine the 4 might hope to see filled, and honourably filled by the's guest. (Cheese). All the exacting toil and the conston_ strain imposed upon a man who filled such an office; the Lord Advocate, had not prevented Mr. Ure rOO giving strenuous, earnest and most unselfish woro behalf of the cause of the people. (Cheers). Therefor, Lera they might truly welcome him not merely as the Advocate but also as THE PEOPLE'S ADVOCATE. the' (Loud cheers). It was his (Mr. Crosfield's) ra•,siis unfortunate duty immediately to follow the sele9l't • speech they had listened to. They were all fasnty,_ with the good old comparison of the farthing on; and the sun—(laughter)—and he thought he had s misfortune on the present occasion to be the farthi,nia candle. (Laughter). He was, however, heartily ghe to have the opportuni y of emphasising, a few of '-o 'points dealt eith by Mr. Ure and to give most et-11047,1 approval, on his hearer's behalf as well as on his to every single important point in the Budget. (Cheer were An eminent observer once remarked that there but two excellent things—good water and good wise-- qv that there was nothing worse than a Inist.:6„st of the t vo. (Lauilat sr). They had be bad some of the soundest and very best wine in s''.o3 political cellar, and it would be rather unkind of "iLi- (Sir. Crosfield) to treat them to a g'ass of water luZe it. (laughter.) That was an additional reason wilY,s;. should not attempt to compete with Mr. for (Laughter). He asked them to support the Budget 13 three reascns—fi rat, tecause of the purr(); es for W. the money was intended ; secondly, because tu`,4 approved of the fair methods, as regarded every class:ll the population, by which the money was raised; thiro_, -because this Budget was Going to snuff out Protectin.n. (Cheers). There were in this country and, hapPilY' sif America and Germany, an increasing numbed, employers of labour who.as far as opportunities ofrereof endeavcrred to improve somewhat, the conditions commerce, especially by providing, outside of busiLti, hours, facilities for mental and physical pursuits added to the int( rest,usefulness, helpfulness and harro-t ness of life. While such efforts helped to some extlso- to civilise the conditions of commerce, they might say,taking a broad view of the Budget in all its espec,o, that it would help to no small extent trtro civilise the conditions of individualism. Mr. oy had said that the money would be raised without injustice. It would also mean the putting into Pre,,-d- -tice of three pr;nciples which he (Mr. Crosfielel) hn often discussed with members of the Progressive Laljeouc tsi▪ g League and others in Warrington—those of gradna,, taxation, o- qualifying individualism, and adjesting able tion according to the strength of those who were litho to support it. All the hullabaloo they had heard instee country, and which he had also listened to night ▪ p night and week after week in the Horse of Comnlo' about land taxation, seemed to him to be the most EXAGGERATED NONSENSE he had ever heard in his life—(hear, hear)—becrelse "f the land taxes erred at all, they erred on the side ;oh moderation. (Cheers.) As to the practical work whllie the Budget would enable them to db, there was first t_ the question of unemployment. The State would sssist funds that were to be raised by joint contributions.el'A guard against unemployment in three of our grea:ss. industries—ship building, engineering and bunch:Ts Many members of the House of Commons were takul the deepest interest in that question, and not the I.es:d active, and certainly not the least able was their Mr. Vivian—(cheers)—who bad oft en spoken to Warring ton gatherings. He (Mr. Crosfleld) bad never read nn); thing better on the whole subject of unensploymenttdile Mr. Vivian's pamphlet and the remedies he suggeste te different industries. The State would also contriint'eci to the cost of labour exchanges by which they w9en,bst to get rid as far as possible—he hoped entirely—Gt.' `iros• heart-breaking business, that waste of time 1%11 involved when men bad to go about the country, to the wrong places, in search of work. An importi:he factor in dealing with unemployment would Lee our grant for the development of tbe resources or sr country, because work would either be hurried held back according to the briskness or slackness labour market. Be asked the meeting to tear in wilined how each of the measures he had mentioned dove-te'the into the other and that was the kind of work which Lhbe additional funds to be placed at their disposal bY riff Budget would enable the State to support. le or, reform would now receive its quietus. He would I; dwell upon that topic because he thought s 016 TARIFF REFORM WAS HAVING A PRETTY POO.- of it in Warrington—(laughter)---and he was re: ifd afraid that it would be a long time before his old fries,o Alderman Bennett would persuade any ava rri giNot audiences he might tackle to reject the Bud" ss, es in order to adopt his food taxes. (u"e"brey To deal with a question about which t to, had beard a great deal in Warrington, rae maintained that everybody who was keen on the defekcr) of our country and the Empire—and he (the 5P,e3„t...-- was very keen on it—should support this Btlug' re (hear bear)—because it would find the funds that ( necessary for the triple purposes they had in view. Ure had alluded to the expenditure cn the navP ose the whole question of defence, that came first, besot,. unless we kept wtat the Prime Minister bad calledibe unchallenged and unchallengeable command of seas, nothing else would avail us at all. It was n° pretending that</p>
        <p>A NATION IN ARMS iefer was going to save us if we lost command cn itiou for e e should be starved out very quickly. In au"," 0r to the navy, uhich was the first essential, we han _olcl regular army for expeditionary purposes over sea Poe we had a great deal of weful military material iv rss- Territorial Force which Mr. Haldane had so Enctose fully helped to create—(Cheers). Each of %teal branches had its use and, taking the three of of together, he said emphatically that at this mometroee all the great countries in Europe, there was that pcssessed the security we had in Eng'and (Cheers). Really ore must ask: Mat more di" be want ? Were we to draw the line nowhere? wheno.3o heard some of the things said nowadays lie a reminded of what Lord Salisbury said not verY ,LA° since: "If you give the ultra-militant school its• they will never be satisfied until they have garriforibi the moon to protect the earth from an invasion 40. Mars." (Laughter.) He (Mr. Crosfield) also callesfld.,:„ mind some lines which his uncle George Croel" (che 1i ers)—was rather fond of quoting—lines writtenble century since but which he was afraid were 00 ic tsted in these days when naval and other scares were tro out : "Alas, the country How shall tongue or pen bewail our gentlemen; The last to bid the cry of warfare cease, The first to make itmelcdy of peace." •r' There was one more reason why he asked for"l"..heo earnest and hearty kupport to the resolution. " he our Colonial friends came to visit us the other aqi'tee saw it very truly stated in one periodical that of a. ~,re things we had to show them in the old country, t"e dot was nothing more beautiful and perfect than the ver and wild flowers of the woodlands and meadows. ,o, were all proud of the beauty of old England erreet were they. And yet, as had been quoted in dila to parts of the country. one and all of them point ver those unhappy contrasts, those placue spots in the ° the congested centres of population which were reollY 10, greatest diagrar to the civilization of England te-u (Cheers.) Progressives welcomed the tax on undevel,°rPos I,ind not only for the reveuue which would accrneitlreet it but for what was much more important—the in°,;iog effect of that tax and of the Budget as a whole 1n g 1 a better chance to the workacNy population 01 workaday country. (Cheers.) .a Mr. J. awer.e, in seconding the resolution, sal roe believed that the Budget would help to remove s,,trio of the obstacles that lad been handicapping Warrin the as a trading centre. Many of them knew fre„Talis history of the past that the Crewe Locomotive ought to have been here, but the land could not be go, They also knew that Messrs'. Lever-Brothers and Ket-seeo Lloyd and Lloydvof Birmingham, would have -00 down at Bank Quay only for the landlords. That fie one of the reasons why he supported the Budget' too. believed it would help the trade of WarriET,od Referring to Lord Rosebery, who said that caPi.ttl,,`C, be' labour were twin sisters, the speaker said they Inlg': sisters, ter- but be thought capital was the younger sisnia Andrew Carnegie said that something like 680,000 P°7o3 , of this country owned £12,000,000,000 of wealth, 0000' they paid in taxation some £38,000,000. 5,1° 'ead middle class of this country owned £3,000,000,000. see- they paid .£12,000,000. 38,000,000 of the working Clasfelid of this country owned £1,000,000,C00, and theY, 40re £40,000,000. Et was about time we bad a revenl'ical for such taxaticn as that ought to be altered. with such a Chamber as the House of Lords, and let have a Chamber built up of intelligence. (APPlaue</p>
        <p>RESOLUTION PASSED. 00c1 The resolution was then put to the meeting, carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS. Mr. JOSEPH HoWES proposed a vote of thanks t° 14t tire. Mr. PARKER seconded. Mr. ETRE briefly replied and proposed a vote of ttol.l°)Y to the Chairman, which was heartily accorded.</p>
