      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>NOTES BY " MUST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " MUST."</p>
        <p>lAt last there is something to note. Warrington a4e,won a match, and as the victory was on foreign fo it is all the more meritorious. `Last season they 041111„d the task at Rochdale just too big for them, but aturday they made amends by beating the Hornets thilaints to (1 points. There are no "gifts" among anecl 11.a37 matches of the Northern League nowadays, .134,111 any a club will come away from Rochdale with kr,'"`l°llB little to show for their efforts. Warrington exew what was demanded of them and realised Peetations in a workmanlike fashion. -</p>
        <p>11,11-nets did not succumb without a hard fight. •val they had a lead of 3 points to nil, but had been playing a waiting game against This was soon evidenced on the change of he Wirepullers at once bore down on the the forwards brushing aside 'the opposition , style and Fish equalised within a few The pressure was maintained by the front .3 °ppm tunities coining quickly. Brookes and Ret ti led further points before the Hornets could froitt te.r backs up again. It was Warrington's game 11144 nOW to the close, though the, home team Vr,`4 aged to notch another try and reduce the margin. it l .:11, ngton have yet to record their first goal, and be noted that Fish followed the example of "'son on Saturday by hitting the upright.</p>
        <p>• Th. Was one very gratifying feature from a War- -111-e4lll Point of view, and that was the superiority of Pla,,`°rvrarcls. The advent of two accomplished efirB like O'Malley and O'Neill made a difference, both`!'e sextette easily outplayed their opponents— the scrummages and in the open. O'Malley ecl everybody in his first turn-out in the arri (14 tn,r'gton colours, showing dash,speed and resource 4141—fisnY occasions. Ile appears to be in grand form, same may be said of the cool, calculating who soon proved that he had lost none of his iltY for finding fin oj ening by the way he sorted 11,14 Moks for a try. The Waleington pack, once it tlOh ettled down to scientific methods, ought to lead tliatl'aY in many an asmult. lam hopeful of seeing eolnbination among the r:ont rank men which so 4111.4111,Rtlished the Wigan pa ek of last season, and has ..ts been a feature of the Oldham play.</p>
        <p>---- 1011}, the respective baek.divisions there was not much Warrington were better at half than the lik; before, and in the rime pcsition the veteran Wa-Z Played a clever and very robust game. The festi:-Tton three-quarter rne has not yet found its very kiln the men are coming on. Cartwright made a g°oll substitute for Dickenson, and displayed f.orra than he has shown fora couple of seasons. , I'6 Of notice was taken of Warrington's young 'est , Mclntyre, who came through a pretty severe tio4l:ith flying colours. He fully justified his selcz- lrape,,lnd rtust be persevered %.ith. His kiek:ng was "1111/7,and on one o :asion from a long punt he ailed to gather the ball by inches at top speed 40 one to beat. The whole of the play orly saw lecia„auglit with the ball once, this alone being- a improvement on Tilley's display of the week /1144 ' Warrington somehow always find a full back tralia°e,.easion arises. One has only to think of the tist : Boscow, Heescm, Hallam, Berry, Tilley, llelntyre, who Pan I,;ck a length with rny of has just the brqd for the position. 'l'4A ''tit 44Y ought to see more points a:Truing .aom the pet St. Helens especial y so as there was a .margin ,!ild,,l3'3ints between the teams when they last visited :341-IsPnol. The pill-men have got an improved t granted decent weather the Wirepulleria .7„t% 414 forward to a substantial margin. They must 'hat •le ball about and rely on speed and combination. h'ileti'3 their natural game and it always succeeds 'Acid 1111)Perly played—as witness Wigan. and now ersfield.</p>
        <p>t ---- ;hl4!:'lllnately Jenkins will be an absentee, as he " ill during the week. Lloyd partners Brooks and I hope he will realise the necessity of kßarhag the bill out smartly. Dickenson is sound d.s should strengthen +he threequarter line. l'e-ted term is Mclntre, full back ; )I,oycille°ll, Taylor, and Bradshaw, threequal ins ; 1.44 and Brooks, half backs ; O'Neill, Thomas, eY, Shugars, I3oardman, and Harmer, forwards. : Turtill full bael.-. ; Flanuagan, J. ;°V, Greenwood and Creevey, threequarters . ,eifth Trenwah, half-backs ; Bradley, Briers, Lee, iAlit,rrescott and Walker, torwards. The famous '•slllthinelc,” Turtill should receive a heal ty welcome ;11 tiloe Warrington crowd, who have not forgotten Ittlal footwork and unruffled calm when hard ted.</p>
        <p>, tokit,„4 team continued their victorious career though it speaks badly for the reserve ttti.`ten Forster has to be brought in at three- Daintith gave a eqpital e7liibition in hi, 40k`e of full back. His chief fault is a tendency Neat his boot when punting. The team to go to 14,1%141% to-day contains W. B. Gibbs at full back. tl Yer is another of the Stockton Heath lads, rid IlfLePtlt„'"f K. Gibbs, the forward. He has gained quite Iptis —tion as a junior custodian. The team is: Qeke' NI back; Landor, Cartwright, Jordan and three-quarters ; Kay and Daintith, half- -1111'"elton, Chesters, Naylor, Harrison, Woorall, -''ishaw, forwards. koelens " A " : Wolfe full back; Cottam, Ryan, thrf 3-qua;Ors ; Toole and Hall, half illey, Pope, Unarton, Johnson, and leet •-- tiedee,- sides from the local schools indulged in a 14110,2ellind the Church on Tuesday evening, and 11,,,t4'71 ere long to get a proper Rugby Schools 1411 full swing. Then we may see matches IN ;th Ithbonring towns betwcm the juveniles. I ~k11,114,i1e Warrington Committee are keen on getting `4Ol-IToSY game recognised and embraced by the and I hope the authorities will give all 0 can to the scheme. There is no game N:tlko-IfeloPs the body and the spirit of manliness ,glily than Rugby, and the rising genera- e• 'le all the better for a cour.se of the handling ItIllt:$1,11i°11 Ind Knowles, and Rylands Bro 3- men, have N.U.. Rugby teams and join ri the St. District League. The forgemen have IN:eh, e, use of the ground behind St. James' %IY, %ell", Play their first match there. at 3.30 'lsitors being Denton Green. There should Contest when the local teams meet.</p>
