      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Warrington's Smart Win.</head>
        <p>Warrington's Smart Win.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men recorded their first win of the season on Saturday when they visited Rochdale and scored 9 points to 6 against the Hornets of that town. Dickenson was a prominent absentee from the Warring- ton ranks, bet the forwards were strengthened by the inclusion of O'Malley, the Australian, and O'Neill, the Welsh International. There was a good crowd of spect *tors, and a very fast, keenly contested game was witnessed, the visitors only proving their superiority in the closing stage., Teams : Rochdale—Hollingdrake, full back; West, Robinson. English and Taylor, three-quarters; Baxter and Schofield, half-backs; Dearden, Turner, Fitsimmons, Whittaker, Hayes, and Richardson, forwards. Warrington—Mclntyre, full back •, Fisb, Cartwright, Taylor and Bradshaw, three-quarters; Brookes and Jenkins, half-bac: s; Thomas, Shugars' Boardman, O'Neil, O'Malley and Harmer, forwards. Referee, Mr. McCutcheon.</p>
        <p>Warrington kicked off against the wind and were very early aggressive, O'Mally being at once prominent. A nice break away by Schofield locked dangerous but after a couple of transfers Fish accounted for the wing man. Mr. McCutcheon at once showed how the game was to be conducted by penalising Warrington for a charge after a Rochdale man had parted with the ball. Baxter kicked at goal but failed. Rochdale for the first ten minutes obtained possession constantly from the serum. Bradshaw missed a• pass when there was just a chance of something turning up. There were a number of scrums at midfield and play was very even. Hereabouts Afcintyre missed the ball from a L.igh kick but Taylor as usual came to the rescue. A War- rington forward kicked a bit strongly crossways from the scrum right into Baxter's hands and he broke away nicely from the Warringtan 25 line. Ile passed the ball to West at the right moment, for the latter to saore a very good try after 17 minutes play. Robins In's kick at goal failed. Baxter after another breakaway and repass was over but was called bask for a forward transfer. Half-time arrived with the score: Rochdale,3 points; Warrington nil. Rochdale re-started and after a couple of transfers between both sets of backs a Rochdale man was off-side and Fish bad a shot at goal but was nowhere near. Fish soon scored as the result of a break away by Jenkins who was playing outside half, having changed places with Brookes. Thomas's kick at goal failed. O'iNle iI 1 a few minutes after twisted round in.his own way and sought Brookes out in a line with him on the Roch- dale 25, and the latter scored comfortably. Fish's kick at goal hit the post. Warrington were simply swamping the home players forward at this period. Brookes was almost over a few minutes after, dropping a foot from the line. A moment later saw Fish score h's second try after nice passing by Boardman Jenkins and Cartwright. Mclntyre made a good shot to improve from a very difficult angle. Rochdale seemed to waken up all at once. and Baxter gettine hold at midfield passed out to the wing who made good head- way, then passed inside. The tall was handled by four more Rochdale men for Richardson to score in a good position with still eight minutes to play. The kick at goal failed miserably. Warrington rushed straight away to the other end where Taylor had a drop at goal but it went wide. The visitors kept the pressure up and Fish missed a glarions opportunity when he went inside instead of passing to Cartwright. Mclntyre was upsetting the Rochdale's metis calculations by his high punts and following up. From a high kick Taylor followed up and gathered and apparently did not see O'Malley with an open field or he must have passed the tall, Holling- drake bringing off a very fine tackle. A free kick for obstruction awarded to Warrington enabled Mclntyre' to have a shot at goal from just inside half-way. He made a fine kick, but failed to reach the mark, and the whistle then sounded the "final." Score :—Wartingt on, three tries (nine points) ; Rochdale Hornets, two tries (six points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WIDNES TRIVIIPH.</head>
        <p>A WIDNES TRIVIIPH.</p>
        <p>For the first time for many years Widnes beat Batley on Saturday, before 3,000 spectators. The home team won the tos., and had the advantage of a slight breeze. For a time Widnes had the best of matters, and a com- bined movement by the tacks ended in Reid scoring behind the posts after beating Ward and Chide. George Aspey improved from an easy position. Batley got into their opponents' quarters, and Wattle Dasies had an unsuccessful shot at goal from long range. Widnes returned to the attack, and affer a pretty round of passing by Noon, Lally, and Taylor, the latter scored. George Aspey again improved. Just before the interval Davies scored for the visitors and placed a goal. Interval—Widnes, 2 'goals 2 tries (ten points) ; Baoey, 1 goal 1 try (5 points). Early in the second half Batley made a hot attack on the Widnes lines, but without success. By the aid of touch, Widnes worked their way to the Batley quarters, and hoai a scrummage under the posts Lally dropped a smart goal. Shortly afterwards Davies received a pass, and beating all opposition, registered a second try and kicked a goal. Final :—Widnes. three goals two tries (12 points) ; Batley, two goals two tries (ten) points). Teams and officials:— _</p>
        <p>Widnes—Lambert, back; lace, Taylor, Reid, and McAlister, three rafter backs; Lally and Noon, half- backs; G. Aspey, S. Aspeyr Carr, Lloyd, Rigby, and O'Gara, forwards. Batley—Childe, back; Davies, Senior, Ward, and Jones, three-quarter backs; Oakland and Thomas, half backs; Gatia, Kitson, Settle, Fozzard,llill and Hodgson, forwards. _ _</p>
        <p>-Referee, Mr. H. Farrar, Keighley. Touch judges, Messrs. R. H. Jackson, St. Helens, and T. Winstanley, Warrington. ST. HELENS AND RUNCORN DRAW.</p>
        <p>St. Helens commenced their home fixtures, when Runcorn were entertained as the visitors. St. kittens lost the toss, and Councillor Ellison started the game on behalf of the home team. By the aid of long kicking the visitors took up a footing in the home quarters, and from a scrummage en the line, Trenwith received and kicked, Kenedy catching the ball, and although tackled, fell over the line and was awarded a tlry. Padbury took the place kick and landed a goal. Half-time score : Runcorn 1 goal I try (5 points), St. Helens nothing. Walker started the second half which was senca- tional. After some even play at the centre, the leather was cross kicked by Creevey to Flanagan, who trans-</p>
        <p>ferred to Kelly, he to Greenwood, and the latter to Lee, who scared a grand try near to the posts. Creevey tock the place kick, and was successful in making the scares level. Jolley, for Runcorn, had a shot at goal, which went wide. Result :—St. Helens, 1 goal 1 try (5 points) ; Runcorn 1 goal 1 try (5 points). Teams and officials :</p>
        <p>St. Helens.—H. S. Turtill, back ; 3. Flanagan, H. Greenwood, T. Barton (captain), and C. Creevey, three- quarter backs ; A. F. Kelly an:i F. Trenwith, half backs; W. Briers, Bradley, F. Lee, J. Prescott, J. Helsby, and Walker, forwdrds. Runcorn.—Harris )n, bsck ; Hughes, Butterwortb, Moran, and linsworths three-quarter backs ; Jolley. and Wattan, half backs : Padburg (captain), Walker, Wood, Kenny, McDoaald and Peacock, forwards. Reteree, Mr. C. Platt, Swintov. foccb judges, Messrs. A. Critchley, Widnes, and F. Lune:, War- rington. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 18, 1909</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON" " v. ROCHDALE " A."—A large crowd witnessed the encounter between these tams at Wilderspcol on Saturday. The visitors started well Roger made a fine attempt to break through. Warring- ton showed better form and a good passing bout looked promising,but Landor just failed to put on the finishing touch. The home team kept up the attack and Oakea railed over at the corner, Jordan failing at goal. A little later Foster added another V./ for the Warring- ton;ans and Daintith augmented. Warrington ruin- tamed the pressure, but did not make the best use of the openings which presepted themselves. Near half- time P. Boardroen scored, and the goal kick being a failure, Warrington led at the interval by eleven points to nil. In the second stage the game WAS evenly con- tested, and Rogers scored an unconverted try for the visitors. Result :—Warrington, one goal, three tries (eleven points),Rochdale Hornets one try(three points.)</p>
        <p>PROPOSED FORMATION OF A NEW ZEALAND</p>
        <p>LEAGUE. At a meeting of the Northern Rugby League at Huddersfield on Tuesday evening practical illustration was afforded of the spread of Northern Unionism in New Zealand. An official application was read from Auckland stating that a league had been formed under Northern Union rules, and asking to be affiliated with the Union. It was stated that the movement had Leon given an influential fillip at a meeting in Auckland City, at which a resolution was passed forming a league and adopting the rules cf the English Northern Rugby Union. The Mayor of Auckland, Mr. Charles Grey, was appointed president, Mr. E. Glover, M.P., vice-president, Mr. W. Wynyard, one of the most famous of the first team of "All Blacks" which visited England, honorary treasurer, and Mr. E. W. Watts, wha acted as secretary of the tour, was appointed secretary of the naw league. Tne communication stated tint grounds had already be. n secured for the clubs, and the clubs, and the prospects of the league were extremely bright. The league officials asked that no arrangements for international matches be made except through the New Zealand League. The Committee of the Northern Union viewed the application with favour, and it was decided to risk the new league to forward copies of the proposed rules for their constitution. Thirty clubs were admitted to honorary membership of the Union. Messrs. J. Welsh (Stockport), J. E. Warren (Warrington), and D. F. Burnley (Batley) were appointed a League Appe.4 Committee of the Union. The following reported players were dealt with: Hammond (Halifax) and F. Jones (Merthyr), each suspended one match for wrestling: McDonald (Ice), suspended to September 25th for tripping when playing against Widnes "A"; Beetliam (Broughton Rangers), suspended to September 25th for kicking in the match with Huddersfield on Saturday last. PLAYING FIELDS ASSOCIATION. On Tuesday night a meeting of the British Playing Fields Society was held at Huddersfield, J. H. Houghton, St. Helens, presiding. A grant of £l5 was made towards the rentals of fields for interaiediate football, and a loan of £lO 10s. was made to the Wigan District Leegue for the same purpose. The Hudders- field club were thanked for a donation of £2l, the pro- ceeds of a practice match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Results of Matchet, September 11th. Wellfkid 4, Howley Earlestown St. John's 4, Newton Amateurs 0. Matches for Saturday, September 18th Timbrell United v. Calder and Mersey. Howley v. Walker's Brewety. Er destois a St John's v. WHtet.ross,</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION'.</p>
        <p>WARRIVGTON ALBIONS V. SUrfOS COMMERCIAL.— Warrington-Albions opened their home prograw-ne on Satur(Ny when they registerai a victory at the expense of Sutton Commercial. Sir Gilbert Greenll kicked off in the presence of a large crowd. The first I,slf was very keenly fought and the visitors had the m'qfortune to lose the services of Spading, a half back, at an early stage. From a scramble in the goal mouth Hunter scored easily for the Albions,giving Skidmore no chance. At 18.1 f-time the score was: Albion, one goal; Sutton, nil. In the sccond half the Albions, by the advantage of the wind, increased their lead by one goal scored uy Chester. The visitors played a good game but strove in vain to score, the result when time was called being: Albion, two goals; Sutton Commercial, nil.</p>
