      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>FOOTBALL GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>NEW SEASON FORM.</p>
        <p>BY B. BENNISON.</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED BY JACK DODWORTFT.</p>
        <p>One must not attach too much importance to football results at the beginning of a new season. Footballers do not feel their feet readily; .hardly ever consistent, they are most times hopelessly inconsistent during the month of September. And yet, without attaching too much Seriousness to the matehos already played, there have been happenings among our League clubs that 'have been most significant. We have had it proved :n a most indisputable way, I think, that with all their admitted greatness Newcastle United are apt to play in quite a feeble and unconvinc- ing way.. During th,..ir tour at the opening of the season it was als:ed that thy had seldom played better; Evert‘u people, I am sure, will male the admission with the utmost frankness, that, their players have rarely, if ever, been so completely overcome; the possibilities of the Neweakle team when • they, were at Goodison Park seethed colossal, and yet we see that they were beaten at St. James's Park, where 4o.ne must always regard them as being almost, in- invincible, by Notts Forest. Notts Forest Doing Well,</p>
        <p>It is altogether startling and utterly perplex- ing to the student of form that Notts Foreet should have made such a tremendous beginning. A year ago one would have said that they were not much- more than an average side, And now with practically the same team they go and win their first three matches. One would like to see Notts Forest have a good season. They are one of our oldest club, and in their time have given to us some of the finest exponents of the game— Tineley Lindley, for instance, to mention only one player. I should not care to say that Notts Forest are going to be a great side, but their</p>
        <p>splendid beginning will be of immense comfort to them. There is nothing like making points early. Very often a prooperous opening means everything. Footballers are not supposed to be the least bit susceptible; some folks would smile if you say they are sensitive, but it is neverthe- less a fact. I have known many really talented beams have a matt disastrous season for no other reason than that they have failed to get off their marks well. .</p>
        <p>-------- The fact that Newcastle United lost to Notts Forest was surprising, but if one pauses to think it is easy to understand. Newcastle United would never have become to be regarded as per- haps the most brilliant tea:n ever got together since the old North End eleven and Sunderland when they were led by that prince of centre-for- wards, Johnny Campbell, if they did not go off at tangents. The ideal team, from a match-winning point of view, is a combination of deep thinkers and hard, dour. determined men. If I were asked to say who played the most captivating and the most polished football I should decide in favour of Newcastle United; but in a set con- test of unusual importance I should always pre- fer Nlancliester United, for the reason that they represent more practical workmen; they are not so artistic; they are not so concerned agent style; they are not so easily upset by making mistakes. And here vou have the secret of the successful way in which they have started a fresh cam- paign. Having •seen then: at Tottenham, I am forced to the conclusion that if Meredith does not rejoin the club they will not be so effective forward. • Woodward's Successor. - . .</p>
        <p>-•--- As to Tottenham, who have improved their home by the erection of a palatial grand stand, and -so made it snore in keeping with the grounds of Chelsea and Fulham, one must wait a while before expressing any definite opinion as to their worth. It is thought by a good maat people that the absence if 'Vivian Woodwaigi SPUR: Man. U tiff°</p>
        <p>will be severely felt. Now, I have always held Woodward in the highest Telfard, and I am truly sorry that he has decided to retire from big games; but, if I am not mistaken, Minter, who has succeeded him as centre-forward, will com- pensate for the retirement of the famous ama- teur. Mint&amp; strikes me as a very effective player indeed, and in this respect more danger- ous than/Woodward was wont to be in a hard, punishing game. At all events, it is good to know that Tottenham have found such a promis- ing young man as Minter. Centro- forwards 'of convincing merit have been strangely few. You could count them on one hand; and, ..for practical purposes, I should place Quinn, of the Celtic, at the head of a pre- cious little- army. Chelsea attribute their heavy defeat by Aston Villa very largely to the in- ability- of George Hilsdon to lead the forwards; but I think that Aston Villa are going to be one of the strongest sides of the season, and that they would have defeated the West London dui, even if Hilsdon had been playing. , Aston Villa unlike Birmingham—how marked has .been deterioration of Birmingham I—could not have done better than they have done; and the fact that their present form is more in keeping with the hisll traditions of the club than perhaps at any time last term promises well for the League competition. If one is not completely mistaken, the fight for high places is going to be more strenuous than ever. The team that win the first League Championship must needs be a</p>
        <p>great one in every respect. if the show made by Mkldlesbrough and Bradford City—clubs who last season were no great shakes—is to be taken at all seriously. There has seldom been so much enthusiasm in Middleebrough before: and if you examine the team's record, taking particular notice of the ready way in which they have found the go,al, they are bound to give consider- able concern to their strongest opponents. The (lame Still Popular. • Bristol City have not so far lived up to the form they displayed in last season's Cup ties; and .Bury do not suggest that they will show any improvement. Nott-s County seem to be just an ordinary side, while Woolwich Arsenal have dis- appointed everybody. But. as I have said, most teams have yet to settle down; there can be no form at present. What everybody must be pleased with is the fact that the game has not lost any of its popularity, and, better than all, there is a promise --remote it may be a better playing standard. An:hie Kyle. the. Scottish Internationalist, ha been transferred from Blackburn Rovers Lo Bradford Park, while there is word that Green, the Birmingham player, has been transferred to Burnley.</p>
