      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S RECORD FOR TILE SEASON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S RECORD FOR TILE SEASON.</p>
        <p>Warrington hive bad a remArkably successful season, the chief achievement, of course, being the capture of the West Lancashire League Championship. They passed through the tourney without a single defeat, and it Is interesting to note that they have made 1,519 runs for the loss of 82 wickets. an average of 18.52 runs per wicket. The record for the season was ten wins, twelve draws and only one game lost. Much of the credit for the satisfactory position of the eleven is due to Mr. B. Silcock who has discharged the duties 9f captain with tkill and judgment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SEASON'S RECORD.</head>
        <p>THE SEASON'S RECORD.</p>
        <p>April 17.—Warrington 29 for three against Ashton- under-Lyne. Rain stopped play. April 21.—Warrington, 80; Sale, 47. May I.—Warrirlgton, 182; Bouebton Hall, 47. May B.—Warrtngton, 185; Asoton-rnder-L}Je, 109 for five wickets. *May 15.—Prescot. 107; Warrington,l7s for seven wickets *May 22.—Warrington, 182 ; Newton, 63. May 29.—Boughtou Hall, 24 ; Warringtor. 83. june 4.—Stockport. 129; Wart ington, 117. - *June s.—St. Helens, 67 ; Warringtor 60 for seven wickets. June 12.—Werneth, 51; Warrington, 198 for seven NT ck eta. *June 19.—Earlestown. 113; ington, 191. *June 26.—Warrington, 218 for seven wickets; Prescot, 131 for nine wickets. *July 3.—St. Helens, 145; Warrington, 134 for seven wickets. July 10.—WarringLon, 45 for three wickets agairst Stockport. Rain stopped play. *July•l7.—St.Helens Recreation,l4 ; Warrington,l2l. *July 24.—Newton, 125; Warringtor, 135. *July 31.—Warrington, 165; Earlestown, 63 for nine wickets. August 7.—Wigan, 205 for seven wickets; Warrington, 131 for seven wickets. August 12.—Mr. F. C. Hunter's Glorsop team, 175 for five ickets ; Warrington, 113 for four wickets. August 14.—Werneth 165, Warrington 103 for tight wicketr. August f‘.l.—St. Helens Recreation, 241 ; Warrington, 118 for nine wickets. August 28.—Wigan, 132 ; Warrington, 98 for six wickets. September 4.—Cheetham match abandoned through rain. September llth.—Sale, 103; Warrington, 50 for five wickets.</p>
