      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER ISTH,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>• Lord RoSEBERY is LORD ROSEBERY never so delighted as ON JOHNSON. when he is dealing with a literary sub- ject. Not only is he one of the most polished' orators of our time, but he has long _been accepted as one of our greatest living judges of literary merit. Probably no public man since GLADSTONE has knosim so much, not merely of the great highways, but also of the bypaths of literature. That his lordship should have been the central figure at the Lichfield celebrations on Wednesday was appropriate in every way. A ceremonial occasion or a commemorative festival suits the ROSEBERY temperament admirably, and those who listened to his eulogy of JOHNSON and his immortal biogropher had a race treat. It is two hundred years to-day since JoinisoN was born in that little Midland town and Lichfield is giving itself up to several days of commemmoration. Lovers of JOHNSON from many many parts of the English-speaking world are •to be found in the town, which will take care that the memory of its greatest citizen never dies. In his lifetime, " the great lexicographer," the essayist and poet, was inordinately proud .of Lichfield, and Lichfield certainly has repaid his love with interest.</p>
        <p>This small city has, indeed, left its mark on our history, for JOHNSON'S is not the only great name associated with it, although it may be allowed to be the greatest. JOSEPH ADDISON, essayist, poet, dramatist, states- man, was the son of a Dean of Lichfield. DAVID GARRICK was one Of JOHNSON'S two pupils when he started his ill-fated " academy " at Edial, near _Lichfield. ELIAS ASHMOLE, whose name is associated with the Ashmole Museum at Cambridge, and ERASMUS DARWIN, grandfather of CHARLES DARWIN, were both born and lived at Lich- field. But JOHNSON, of course, was by far the most notable of these, and his memory, at all events, is not likely to be allowed to</p>
        <p>What is it that constitutes JOHNSON'S claim to lasing remembrance? Lord ROSE- BERY declared on Wednesday that most of JOHNSON'S works are never taken down from the shelves. That statement certainly is true, for the majority of people have but a superficial acquaintance with the works of JOHNSON'S own pen. Probably most of us, have a tolerably intimate knowledge of his "Lives of the Poets." Perhaps the only passage even in them which has , any claim to immortality is his comparison between the work of Pope and that of Dryden, As for his' other works,they are, if not forgotten, at least neglected. It is not because of his writings, which are in a style hardly congenial to present day readers, that we remember JOHNSON so much as because of his recorded conversations. It is to his biographer, BOSWELT.,, that we owe nearly all that is worth preserving in regard to him, and BOSWELL has shown us, as Lord ROSEBERY pointedly reminds us, a JOHN BULL abounding in common sense and also in some sense that was uncommon.</p>
        <p>JOHNSON'S conversational powers marked him out from the men of his own time, and are a wonder to us to-day. He was often coarse, often brutal, and his sonorous Latini- fied periods would hardly have suited our time. But he was a tremendous companion, full of information, largely gained from the fact that he spent so much of his youth among the book shelves in his father's shop. He was insular and narrow, but he loved company. Had it not been for BoswELL, it is possible that the ceremony at Lichfield would never have taken place. It is not so much what JOHNSON wrote himself a''s what was written about him that has made him remembered. To say this is in no way to detract from his greatness, for surely even a literary man may have great qualities.that do not always show themselves in his writings. JOHNSON is remembered because he was interesting, because he was hum an, because he was manly, and, perhaps, also, because he had many of the failings of his qualities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, September 18, 1909</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, September 18, 1909</p>
        <p>Warrington was MR. URE AT specially favoured on WARRINGTON. Monday_ by .a visit</p>
        <p>'from the Lord Advocate of Scotland, Mr. Alexander Ure, K.C., M.P., who in recent times has very properly come to be known through the length and breadth of the land as the "Missionary of the Govern- ment" and " Budget Advocate." He has made " this most remarkable Budget "—to use the words of Lord Rosebery—a subject for special study and enthusiastic exposition, and no man is better fitted to lay its many innovations and new sources of taxation before the public in an illuminating way. Warrington people have for months past, through the agency of the Pro- gressive Labour League, been treated to Budget speeches, but that they are far from surfeited was Unmistakably evident from the nature of Monday's demonstration. Parr Hall was crowded in every part by an audi- ence comprised almost entirely of voters, and no man ever had a better hearing than Mr. Ure. The people literally hung on - his accents, and the concentration of attention throughout the Lord Advocate's eighty-five minutes oration was something to marvel at. Audiences are apt to beconie restive during a speech of this duration dealing entirely with legislative proposals and containing a good deal of solid reasoning and statistical comparison. But the Budget has irresistible claims on public attention. It has caught the popular fancy in a way that no enaotment since the repeal of the Corn Laws has ever done. People have suddenly realised that taxation has for generations been unequal and unfair in its incidence. The biggest burdens have been resting on the weakest backs, and they see with upclouded vision the dawn of the day when land and capital shall take their fair share in bearing the burden which has so long bowed down the toiling masses of the country. The Budget Marks an epoch in national government, and they are blind who cannot see that the people are as one in their determination to have its proposals enrolled among the laws of the land. • Our readers will find ON THE LAND a verbatim report of TAXES. Mr. Ure's speech on page 2. It contains much more information than we can com- ment upon in the limited space at our dis- posal here, but there were one or two por- tions of it which stood out beyond all the rest in the interest and enthusiasm they excited. First and foremost come the land taxes— about which "the heathen rage so furiously together." They are only estimated to yield half a million pounds towards the thirteen millions required by the Government from the extra taxation, but they embody a great principle, and they are capable of great expansion. This is why the selfish soul of the landowner is troubled. He sees that the end is in sight of those golden days when the community could be made to pay one hundredfold for values created by its Own ceaseless activity. In future a propor- tion of that increment value must be handed back to the community. There is also a very modest charge on the value of undeveloped land, but which will probably suffice to bring the land into the market—which is its proper place in these days of municipal expansion and co-operative growth. There are people who argue that land is indistinguishable from other, classes of property, and place it on a par with Consols. Taxing land values to them is taxing property—and, well, property is sacred you know! Mr. Ure took the trouble to deal with this loose kind of criti- cism, and illustrated in a most convincing way the six characteristic features of land by which it differs from all other commodi- ties. For us it suffices to remember that land is God's gift to man; and nothing more equitable was ever proposed than that those who " own" land should pay a tax to the community on its value as a marketable commodity. The intense absorption of the audience in all Mr. Ure had to say on the land taxes and the evident approval of his singularly clear and logical exposition were beyond all question or cavil. The mind of the people is made up, and the taxation of land values—so long a dream of the ardent reformer—may now be considered a settled thing.</p>
        <p>If Mr. Ure was con- ON TARIFF vincing on land values REFORM. what shall we say of his criticism of Tariff • Reform? It was destructive, annihilating. What a chimera the scientific tariff is when one applies it to the needs of the moment! A ten per cent, tariff on imported manu- factures is all the Tariff Reformers have to offer as an alternative to the Budget for the raising of the necessary revenue. What a ghastly failure it would prove was demonstrated in that cold calm logic of which the Lord Advocate is such a master. Even a Tariff Reformer must have felt that he was pursuing a *lll-o-the-wisp, and have longed instinctively for a real pro- gramme from his party in preference to the dry husks of tariff-trickery. We cannot maintain England by taxing the foreigner, and in the days when such heresies are abroad it is something to be thankful for that the Government, at any rate, has not lost faith in the productive power of the nation and can show us a way to meet necessary expenditure with equitable taxation of home resources.</p>
        <p>To the lugubrious A SHOCKINGLY vaticinations of revolu- CHEAP REVOLUTION. tion Mr. Lloyd-George has replied with a humour such as seldom gleams through mere figures. What is this terrible revolution. this "end of all things ? " The new Death Duties, translated into terms of income tax, represent on estates of from £150,000 to £250,000 a tax of 31d. in the pound. Tariff Reformers often accuse Free Traders of worshipping cheapness. But • did the most ardent devotee of cheapness ever before go to such lengths as to hawk about a three- penny-three-farthings revolution? The chief argument of the Opposition against the Death Duties is that they will have to be paid, not out of income, but out of capital. Even if this is so, it is absurd to suggest that the capital of the nation is thereby depleted. A Government amend- ment is proposed by which landlords may pay Death Duties by the surrender of a piece of land. Is it seriously' suggested that the use of such a piece of land for small holdings, instead of (say) as a deer-park, would lessen the collective wealth of the nation? The Opposition talk as if capital in the hands of the State ceases to have any value. According to this theory, it is a catastrophe if the State takes tax of the superfluities of a "Jubilee Plunger," in order to use it for developing the resourdes of the nation.</p>
        <p>The reception of the AN INGENIOUS Trade Boards Bill and</p>
        <p>CONFESSION. the Housing and Town Planning Bill in the House of Lords was better than many Liberals had expected. But after all it is obvious that no legislative body could be wholly and unservedly mischievous without</p>
        <p>coming to a speedy end. Still, the House of Lords—and it would be well if it could appre- ciate this grave fact—is not threatened becausp it stands distinctly and stupidly cin the ancient ways, but because it is attempting to encroach in a province which for more than two centuries has been barred against it. The immme- diate cause of revolutions—Lord Rosebery should be historic enough to understand—is violent reaction. Still, Liberals will be ready to give the Upper House credit for any sym- pathy which they may show with measures to ameliorate social conditions. Apparently- some of the Conservative Peers are also pre- pared to give credit to a Liberal Government which they would not give to a Government of their own Party. Lord Onslow said that he would not object to the powers given to the Local Government Board if there was a chance of such a statesmen as Mr. Burns being in control. "But he had heard of a General Election." Lord Onslow, an ex- Minister, would trust Mr. Burns where he would not trust a Conservative President of the Local Government Board. .</p>
        <p>Take one illustration of bow Glasgow, for instance, is interested in the land. Over a hundred years ago the site of the Glasgow municipal buildings was sold by the city at 2s. Bd. per square yard, or some £BOO in all. About twenty years ago it was bought back at £35 16s. per square yard, or so £175,000 in all—an increment of £174,000. If the incre- ment duty had been in force, the tax on this "unearned increment" would have been over £31,000. Is that " revoltgion ? " Surely Lord Rosebery was speaking more worthily of himself when in 1894 he described the taxation of ground values as " a sound and just principle."</p>
        <p>Take another point. Does Lord Rosebery know—he must know—that 60. per cent, of the people of Glasgow occupy houses of one and two apartments, and that over 100,000 souls are condemned to live in single-roomed tenements? And all because the land is too dear for a successful Ipplication of the Housing Acts? 1t would perhaps have been a good preparation for Lord Rosebery's speech if he had read, the report of Dr. Russell, the famous Glasgow medical officer, on the over- crowding conditions in Glasgow :•</p>
        <p>Fi;--e thousand of the one-room and 25,000 of the two-room houses contain lodgers, and in some cases six, seven, or eight people sleep in the one room.</p>
        <p>* * * When great Lords—whether Dukes or Earls—say that our big towns are interested in the housing question, they might do well to spend a day or two in these slums, where not even death can come -decently. Hear Dr. Russell:</p>
        <p>I cannot speak to you in decent language of the horrible details. It is these houses that give the enormous death-rate. The exhausted air and poor feedibg fill our streets with bandy-legged children. There the death-rate is 38 per 1,000, while in the districts of larger houses it is only 16 per 1,000 of the children who die before they are five years old; 32 per cent. die in houses of one apartment, and not 2 per cent. in houses of five apartments.</p>
        <p>* * Men who have solved the housing problem by dividing their presence between five houses might reflect on this little touch in Dr. Russell's report, describing the conditions of infantile mortality in Glasgow: There they die, and their little bodies are laid on table and dresser so as to be out of the way of their brothers and sisters, who play, sleep, and eat in their ghastly company. ** * _ _</p>
        <p>And where are we to find the reason for this state of affairs? Why in those very increased values of land which the Govern- ment are being denounced as "revolu- tionaries " for desiring to tax: rutney Vale Cemetery is now purchasing fifteen acres of ground, for which it is paying £450 per 4cre, and the cemetery authority 'states that the reason why it is now acquiring this land is because other cemeteries have had to pay as high as £2,000 per acre. The increased charges which are thus necessitated are naturally most keenly felt by the poorer class of people. "Most keenly felt"! And then they wonder that the Budget is popular!</p>
        <p>VICTORIA PLUMS, 2s. PER Doz. DAMSONS next week. Watch my Prices. CANARY BANANAS, Gd. per doz. McDERMOTT, "Cheapest Man Alive."</p>
        <p>MARKET, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>AND N OTION S.</head>
        <p>AND N OTION S.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall's photo re-appeared on Saturday in our neutral contemporary. If the picture has to be inserted regularly, we think the name underneath might now with safety be omitted. * * *_</p>
        <p>Congratulations to Councillor George Howard. He stood as an Independent, and he ,von as an Independent. The Liberals backed him because Vother side were out in full force, and spoiling for a fight. Mr. E. J. Hall has been quite sarcastic about "independence." As director of a certain company we wonder what he thinks of "neutrality." * *</p>
        <p>The result of the Howley Ward election has proved a sore blow to the Tory party. The fiat had gone forth that the election was to be won and that no stone—no tap either we may presume—was to be left unturned to secure a victory. The yellow war chariot from Walton was in evidence before noon and Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall dispensed smiles through the streets of an admiring ward. After a day of such vigorous exertion the result must have brought great disappointment; but by this time it must surely have begun to dawn upon our brewer- patrons that workingmen are learning to set a higher value upon their votes, and decline to sell them for either beer or smiles. * *</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert's "preliminary canter" has broken down at the first hurdle and on ground which the part y\ pack should know I intimately. If our Conservative friends can come such a cropper on a selected course, what will they do at the General Election? Sir Gilbert told the electors of Howley that he looks upon municipal contests as a pre- liminary canter for the greater event. Howley is not a very good omen for his party and we can understand the gnashing of teeth which took place at the Conservative Club. The defeated candidate was the most cheerful and amiable man there and if he could only fight his battle with more _privates and fewer generals his chance of success would be greater. * * *</p>
        <p>"Peter Pan-demonium," who has been oscillating between Warrington and Preston for a few weeks—much to the uneasiness of the, respective Chief Constables—has at last settled in Preston for 14 days at least. He created another disturbance there and was responsible for the police station being besieged by, 10,000 people. The magistrates were less lenient than those of Warrington, and Peter of the many aliases has to spend a fortnight in gaol. There is a general hope in this part of the country that he will seek fresh fields and pastures new when he is once more a free man. • _ _ _</p>
        <p>With dukes and earls threatening to dis- pharge their gardeners, to " dock" charities and even to withdraw one pound per annum from a football club subscription list all because the Budget proposes to tax their unearned increments from land, it is a pleasure to come across an instance of public spirit and generosity in a landed proprietor. Mr. Edward Gandy of Fearnhead near War- rington is a farmer and a landowner—a small one. By his yards of land other people —Dukes and what not—might measure their acres. Yet the Budget has not scared him into peevish parsimony. The other day a deputation of Independendent Methodists from the neighbouring village of Risley waited upon Mr. Gandy to purchase a site for a new Church. They had little money but they were willing to pay. Mr. Gandy was willing to sell but he preferred to give, and after expressing his approbation of the work of the Independent Methodists (of whom he is Tiot a member) he astonished and delighted the deputation by presenting them with 1440 yards of freehold land— worth at a modest computation £l5O. ** * _</p>
        <p>Now that the froth ba-s gone,it is beginning to be seen that Lord Rosebery's speech is, after all, rather :small beer. It is full of rhetorical phrases, and it is distinguished, of course, by that brilliancy which Lord Rosebery always displays. But there is scarcely a statement in the speech which will bear sober examination. He talks,for instance, a great deal about "insecurity," " increased unemployment," "depression of trade," and so forth. Would any outside observer who read these phrases gather from them the fact that since the Budget was introduced trade has been steadily reviving, security increasing, and employment improving? We say nothing of employment, which naturally improves with the .(summer. But we do say that the single faet that trade has steadily advanced since the Budget was introduced by Mr. Lloyd-George on April 29th knocks all those fine phrases into pulp. * * *</p>
        <p>But wherever one tests Lord Rosebery's speech it shows a similar looseness of fact combined with a surface brilliancy of phrase. His attitude towards the land taxes, for instance, must be described as simply being one of" fright." This is surprising, because in regard to the super-tax, which is a far heavier burden than the land tax, he is quite sensible. He offers to pay even more. And yet the land taxes are much lighter! He talks as if the land taxes were laid upon all landlords in country and town. He entirdly ignores the fact that they are laid only upon unearned increments of urban land. He seems especially alarmed at the undeveloped land tax, which is a tax of only one halfpenny on capital value, when it can be sllown to be increasing on demand for land. Surely there never was a more unjustifiable panic than Lord Rosebery's fright over these small and moderate taxes? * * * _</p>
        <p>The fact is, Lord ft osebery looks at the Budget from the point of view of a country landowner only. Lord Rosebery says that Glasgow is not interested in the land question. That is just where he has gone wrong. The towns are just as much inter- ested in the land question as the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>$ LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>$ LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>- -- Coming Events. Mr. J. Walley, of Manchester, will be the speaker at on open meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society to-morrow (Sunday) afternoon. The harvest festival in connection with the War- rington Women's Circle will be held to-morrow (Sunday) and on Monday. Particulars are advertised. _</p>
        <p>s Harvest festival services will be held at Bewsey- road Wesleyan Church to-morrow (Sunday) when the preachers will be : Morning, Rev. Thomas Gladding ; evening. Rev. E. B. Keeling. In the afternoon at 2.30 a cantata entitled "The Two Harvests" will be given. On Monday at 7.30 p.m. a musical programme will be gone through by the choir.</p>
        <p>The Spiritualists will hold their harvest festival in the Druids' Hall, to-morrow (Sunday). Mrs. Peate will be the speaker and clairvoyant. The first meeting of the season in connection with Orford Barracks Boxing Club will be held at the Gymnasium, Orford Barracks, on Thursday,September 30th. There will lie a ten stone and under novices' competition, open to all civilians in Warrington and a radius of ten miles, two competitions open to the club, and a grand six-round contest. Dr. Collins' Will.</p>
        <p>iThe will of the late Mr. W. J. Collins, M.D.. of Warrington, has been proved at 1,1,263. The Academy Golf Cup.</p>
        <p>Result of fourth round, played at Applelon :—J.W. Bibby beat H. G. Roberts; J. Moore Murray beat W. Greenhouse; W. Richardson w.o. J. Richardson Ecr. C. G. Stone- beat H. H. Evans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Adult School.</head>
        <p>Adult School.</p>
        <p>After the usual Bible lesson at the Adult School on Wednesday evening, a social was held to inaugurate the winter session. Several songs and recitations were contributed by the members of the school, refreshments were provided, and a very pleasant evening was spent. Death of Mr. Joseph Emmett. The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Monday of Mr. Joseph Emmett, of Butteimarket-street, who passed away at the Aikin-street Hospital on Friday last from enteric fever. Mr. Emmett was a wire worker in the employ of Messrs. ,Rylands Bros. Ltd. Church-street, and had been for some time connected with the League of the Cross Band. At the funeral the band heaed the cortege to the Cemetery and played the Dead March in" Saul "as a last mark of respec tto the deceased. There was a large assembly of relatives and friends and the funeral was most impressive. _ _</p>
        <p>Eiening Schools. As will be seen in oiir advertising column, the School of Art, Technical Institute, Commercial Insti- tute, and the Branch Technical Schools have now re-opened for the winter session. A large number of entries has been received at each of the schools, and a successful session is anticipated. To the already extensive syllabus at the School of Mrt, classes in Cabinet Making, Carving and Modelling, Needlework, Embroidery and Leather Work have been added, and teachers qualified to give instruction in these crafts are being appointed. It is hoped that students will show their appreciation of the Education Committee's efforts by turning up in large numbers for these.sub- jects. In these days of strenuous competitions it is necessary that our boys and girls should be " well equipped for the struggle that is before them, and parents who are desirous for their children to further their education should consult the Principals of the Schools or the Director of .Education, who will be only too pleased to advise them as to the course of study their children should follow. GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55.; 28 Bs., and £l2 12s. Others at higher prices. Fully guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,—J. R. WOOD: 47. London rd., Stockton Heath. Advt. Wesleyan Quarterly Meeting. The quarterly meeting of the Warrington Wesleyan Methodist Circuit was held at.Padgate on Thursday. The Rev. E. B. Keeling presided and all the ministers of the circuit were present. The financial statement was again satisfactory. It was reported that there was a slight decrease in memberhip fof the quarter. There was an instructive ConversatiOn on the spiritual state of the circuit,and it was decided to hold a special consectation service at each chapel on the first Sunday in October.—The que=tion of class meetings was discussed, the Rev. T. Miller, Messrs. Withushaw, Smith, Maile and Corbett taking part. Arrangements were made for special ser- vices to be conducted at Bold-street by the Rev. W. D. Walters, of London, in aid of the London Mission. It was decided to invite a special lecturer on temperance work to visit the circuit.—The Rev. F. W. Kernick had been married since the last quarterly meeting and a hearty welcome was accorded to Mrs. Kernick.— The officers of the Worn Out Ministers' Fund were thanked and re-appointed.—An invitation to hold the next meeting at Bold-street on December 16th was ccepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>Primitive Methodist Changes. The Rev. J. B. Buglass will leave' Warrington next year to . take charge of the Rochdale Primitive Med odist Circuit. He will be succeeded by the Rev. NV. Cooper.</p>
        <p>Education Committee. The monthly meeting of the Education Conamitteo will be held on Monday evening in the Town Hall. The business is mostly of a routine character, but on the question of school accommodation the Sites and Buildings Committee has resolved: That subject to the Law Clerk being able to obtain sufficient adjoining land, the site in Winwick-road known as Grace's Pottery be recommended to the Committee as suitable for a new school. That the necessaty notices be issued for the provision there of a school to accommodate 1,260 children. Local Fire.</p>
        <p>About 7.15 yesterday morning a telephone message was received at the Fire Station that a fire had broken out at Mr. T. G. Brooks's joiner's shop in Lexden-street, off Lovely-lane. The fire engine "Captain" and a contingent of men at once turned out in good time, under the direction of Superintendent Turner. On arriving it was found that the joiner's shop, containing a large quantity of timber &amp;c., was well alight. The men at once set to work and with a good supply of water they succeeded in getting the fire under control in an hour and a half. The damage is estimated at between £3OO and £lOO and unfortunately for the owner, it is not covered by insurance. Cycling Accident. William Lawless, a young man residing at 16, Orchard-street, Stockton Heath, met with a somewhat serious accident while out cycling on Sunday after- noon. Mr. Lawless, who was accompanied by two brothers, intended riding to Frodsham, but when pro- ceeding past Daresbury Delph he became dizzy, lost control over his machine and fell over the handle bars. He was picked up by his brothers in an unconscious condition and after being attended to in a house close by, he was taken home in a cab. Dr. Parker dressed his injuries, which consisted of a crushed wrist, a cut above the left eye (in which three stitches were placed) and a badly scared face. _</p>
        <p>PEVERIL " FIRE GRATES Still unsurplssecl, Inspection invited.—J. S. GARLAND, Kitchen Range and Stove Warehouse, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warring- ton.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Railway Accident near Warrington. An accident which caused protracted delay in the traffic occurred on the Cheshire Lines Railway at Sankey Junction about seven o'clock on Tuesday evening. A goods train, laden with copper billets, was preceding in the direction of Warrington when the wheel of.oue of the waggons came off, causing the vehiole to tilt. The damaged waggon was dragged along the permanent way for a considerable distance and other waggons were derailed, the result being that numerous sleepers were damaged and chains broken. Eventually four waggons fell right across the loop line which branches from the Junction to Padgate and another was precipitated across the line leading to the Central Station, Warrington. A break- down gang was telegraphed for and it was soon on the scene, but the confusion was so great that the passenger traffic between Liverpool and Manchester was greatly delayed. During the night all the over- turned waggons were removed from the metals and by about noon on Wednesday the permanent way had been repaired.</p>
        <p>Harvest Festival at Buckley-street. The harvest festival services of the Methodist Free Church, Buckler-street were held on Sunday. Mr. J. W. Cannon, Wesleyan lay missionary, of Haydock, preaching morning aud evening. At the evening service the church was packed, seats hating to be placed in the pulpit. In the afternoon a musical service was presided over by Mr. W. Beswick., Miss Elsie Hewitt and Miss Hilda Spruce sang duets, and Miss Elsie Hewitt rendered a solo very effectively. Mr. Lee was the accompanist. Mr. Cannon gave an address to parents, teachers and scholars. The flowers, fruit, &amp;c., were displayed in a manner that reflected credit on those entrusted with the decorations of the church, and all agreed that the harvest festival had been highly successful.—On Monday evening about 70 sat down to tea, after which Mr. Cannon gave a thrilling lecture on "Remarkable incidents in his life and work." Mr. Thomas Gandy, of Penketlr, presided. The visit of Mr. Cannon will long be remembered. The total proceeds of the festival amounted to £lO 18s. At the Sunday evening services hero were ten conversions. ' The Ragged School.</p>
        <p>The ann—ual prize distribution took place at the Ragged School Mission on Sunday aftenoon. The prizes were presented by Mrs. Charles E. Parker. The services commenced by singing a hymn, followed by prayer by Councillor J. Skelland. Mrs. Parker said that probably they would much rather have their prizes right away and take them home to show to their parents than listen to any address from her, but Seeing that it was the first opportunity that she had had to speak to them, she hoped they would pay attention for a short time while she spoke to them from the words of a text which she hoped they would all remember. The text was "Keep to the right." Mrs. Parker told the children several stories illustrat- ing the text. Having presented the prizes, she said some very kiyd words of encouragement to the workers, complimenting them upon the splendid order maintained. She also spoke of the deep interest 'which both .she and her husband took in the work and stated how very.dear a place it occupied in the heart of her father, the •late Mr. Robert Garnett. Mrs. Parker concluded with prayer.</p>
        <p>"HERALD" KITCHEN RANGES FAVe 50 per cent of fuel.—Agent J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55 Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Messrs. Crosfield and their Bandsmen.</head>
        <p>Messrs. Crosfield and their Bandsmen.</p>
        <p>A case of interest to bandsmen was mentioned to Mr. F. Willis Taylor. at the Liverpool Chancery Vaca- tion Court, on Tuesday, an action had been entered by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., against two of the members of their works band, named Byers and Ryder.—Mr. Eastwood, for the plaintiffs explained that the motion first came on a fortnight ago for an , injunction to restram the defendants, who were mem- bers of the plaintiff's Perfection Soap Works Brass Band, from playing in any other band, and more particularly in the Besses o' th' Barn Brass Band, in breach of their agreement with the plaintiffs. Last week the defendants, represented by counsel, gave an undertaking over Tuesday 'in the terms of the notice of motion. They did not on Tuesday appear by counst 1, but a letter had been received from their solicitor showing that the parties had come to terms. Defendants had paid to the plaintiffs a sum of money, which it was needles to mention, as an agreed stun to cover costs and damages in both cases, and they consented to the injunction asked for being granted until the end of the present month, when the plaintiffs undertook to cancel the agreement.—The order was accordingly granted. Salvation Army Concert. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>A sacred concert was given at the Salvation Army Barracks, Acaftemy-street, on Tuesday evening by Legh-street Primitive Methodist Choir, under the leadership of Mr. A. Spruce. Captain Waring presided in the course of his remirks spoke of the good work accomplished by the Salvation Army, mentioning several places abroad where he had seen them at work. There was one country where he had never seen them and that was Japan but Adjutant Christinass assured him they now had a contingent in that country. The following items were rendered by the choir : Anthems, "Go when the morning shineth " (Lahee), "The Lord is my Shepherd" (Stainer), "God so loved the world" (Sta;ner), "The radiant morn" (IVoodward); solos, "Father Eternal" and "The Gift" by Miss E. Hewitt, "Will there be any stars" and "I shall know Him" by Miss E. Elwell, "Why not to-night" by Mrs. W. Woodland, "My hope is in the Everlasting" by Mr. S. Hamilton, "I have heard of a beautiful city" by Miss B. Brown; duets, "God made all" and "Kind words" by Misses Hewitt and Spruce. The entire programme was rendered in an efficient manner. Mr. A. W. Clatworthy a6companied each item. Adjutant Christmass proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman for so kindly presiding also to the choir for giving them such a splendid service, each item of which was full of the Gospel. The Chairman responding said it had given him much pleasure to be with them that evening and he would further assist • them by sending some produce for their forthcoming harvest festival, which promise was received with acclammation by the audience. Mr. Spruce, who on rising to respond on behalf of the choir was greeted with considerable applause, said that although put io some inconvenience in coming by the pressure of other work in connection with their own church, the choir were delighted with the way their efforts were appreciated and would be pleased to come and help them on some other occasion, tf needed. The proceedings were brought to a close by singing the-Dnxology. TABLE AND HANGING LAMPS, new designs n3w</p>
        <p>showing.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 io 55, Bridge et., Warrington.—AdA.</p>
      </div>
