      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>'focal</head>
        <p>'focal</p>
        <p>will save you, you would soon have a nice little sum to your credit. _Notice that you are not being asked to practise any self-denial in doing this. • The way in which Vi-Cocoa will make you better off is in saving you doctors' bills and money for medicine. It is not only the best food, but by very far the healthiest food, because it is easy to digest—being in fact already digested in the cup, which puts less wear and tear upon the stomach. It keeps out the cold: You do not catch cold and fevers so easily. This is well worth thinking of for medicine and doctors' bills mount up in the course of a year. Even if you belong to a Sick Club, you most likely buy some medicine your- self pretty often. People who use Vi-Cocoa and a packet can be obtained from every grocer for sixpence —do not want medicine. It keeps them well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>GIRL'S THEFTS.</head>
        <p>A GIRL'S THEFTS.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday Annie Bennett, a little girl, daughter of a private stationed at Orford Barracks was charged with stealing various articles of the value of 2s. from the shop 20, Winwick- street the property of James Blakely.—The Chief Constable stated that at a quarter to nine on the above date the girl went into the shop of the prosecutor, accompanied by her mother. While there the girl was seen by a lady assistant to act in a very suspicious manner. Shortly afterwards she was observed to take one or two of the articles and put them in a bag. She was brought to the Police Station and charged with stealing the articles and she at once admitted having done FO. The conduct of the girl's mother in the case was not altogether satisfactory.—The thefts seemed to be systematic.—The Mayor said the bench were not altogether satisfied with the conduct of the mother (who was present in the court)which was very suspicions. The father was ordered to pry 10s.and defendant vas warned that if anything of that kind occurred again she would be sent to a reformatory school. ALLEGED ACCIDENT INSURANCE FRAUD. At the Borough Police Court on Friday, Richard Whittaker of 46, Wilderspool-road was summoned before the Court for obtaining the sum of Ll 10s. by false pretences on the 23rd. August. Whittaker did not appear. Mr. F. G. Taylor, who prosecuted on behalf of the County Insurance Company, said their head offices were at Aberdeen. Whittaker was the local superintendent for this county. He had with him at one time a spare time agent named Bent. Bent was a policy holder, and was entitled to benefit in case of sickness or accident and received the money weekly. Whittaker was the man who paid him the money. In the wrath of August the company received a claim from Emma Bent to the effect that her husband John William Bent had been burned on both hands. Whittaker seat in the claim and 30s. was sent. Whittaker did not pay this claim and the whole thing was a fraud. Bent had nothing to do with the transaction. Whittaker was the only man who bad any correspondence with the company. —John William Bent said he was a gas stove moulder and lived at 40 Garlbaldi-street. He said it was untrue that he was burned on both hands on the oth of August and it was not true that on the 16th of August be went to Dr. Martin's surgery to have his hands attended to. Witness had not received 30s. from the company.— Mr. Taylor asked for a warrant to be taken out against defendant.—The warrant was granted.</p>
        <p>PROSECUTION UNDER THE FACTORY ACTS. At the Borough Police Court on Friday, before the Ddputy Mayor and other magistrates, three cases of contravention of the Factory Acts were inquired into. The defendants were Miss Elizabeth Thotnassen, of Bernard-street, Miss Mary Clifford, 64, St.Mary's-Street, and Miss Elizabeth 11a11,4c,801d-street,all three carrying business as dressmakers, &amp;c. Miss Elizabeth Squire, B.M..nspector of Factories (Manchester) prosecuted. In the case of Elizabeth Thomasson the was charged with, that on the 27th of July, being the occupier of a certain worshop in Bernard-street, she d;d net keep cops or abstract of the rules or laws, affixed on the walls of the workshop and also with not having a prescribed register in use. Miss Squire stated that Miss Pearson, a lady factory inspector, visited the workshop of the defendant and duly looked for the copy of the abstract and could not find it, and asked the defendant to be shown the copy, but she could not pro- duce it. Miss Pearson then asked to be shown the general register. Die defendant was, however, unable to show her the register. She again visited the defendant on August 11th, and found that the abstract had not been affixed, reithe: bad a register been obtained. Defendant bad also been previously cautioned in 1507. She asked the Bench to impose a small penalty as a warning. Defendan said she did not think it was necessary to affix an abstract on the walls and also to have a register in so small a workshop.—The Chairman: How many people do you employ ?—Defendant: Four with myself. —The Chairman said it was a very serious case and the Inspectors were quite in keeping in taking the proceed- ings out.—The bench viewed the case very seriously, but they would attempt to deal leniently with defendant which would serve as a warning. She weuld be fined ss. and costs in each case.</p>
        <p>In the case of Mary Clifford, who occupied a work- shop at 64, St. Mary-street, she was chlrged with neglecting to keep a form of register of medical certification, also with emplo:ng three girls under the age of IS, without registering them. Miss Squire stated that the defendant employed a considerable number of young persons under the age of IS also a number of women. On the 10th of August, Miss Pearson visitel the defen- dant's workshop and found certain )orig persons under 18 years of age employed. She asked to see the general register.On examining it she found it blank and nothing had been entered in it whatever. The register should contain the names and addresses and also a certificate to certify that a surgeon had examined and passed them. Miss Pearson corroborated Miss Squires statements. Defendant was fined 2s. Gd. and Is. costs. The case of Elizabeth Hall, of 4e, Bold-street, was then proceeded with. She was charged with neglecting to have a medical certificate, certifying the fitness of one of her employs —Miss Squire stated that a male inspector visited Miss Hall's workshop and found a airl aged 15 years, who had been employed seven months. Her name was not on the register. The bench would fully appreciate the seriousness of girls beginning work in the workshop without being examined as to tbeir fitness.—Defendant was fined Is, costs in each case making £1 Gs. in all.</p>
