      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>3IONDAY.—Before the Mayo- (in the chair), Aldermri Smethurst, Councillor Wilkinson, and Afessrs. W. Roberts, H. Thornton, W. Furness and G. Pilling. FULL OF ALE AND AGGAAVATION.—John Makin, of SLarp-street, was charged with using obscene language in hearing of Sharp-street on the 16th of August.— Defendant on being asked if he were guilty replied: "Yes, I was full of ale and aggravation." (Laughter). He was fined Gs. ATTEMPTED Sulcibi.l—Margaret Cartwright, a middle-aged woman, of Hamilton-street, was charged with attempting to committee suicide in the River Mersey at Paddington Lock -on August 19th.—The Chief Constable stated that defendant was seen by the lock-keeper at Paddington endeavouring to get into the water. She bad keen cautioned by somebody on the opposite 'side of the river before. Information was</p>
        <p>given to the police and she was removed to the Infirmary where she had been since. There was a sister in court and if she were prepared to look after her he saw no reason why defendant should not be allowed to go. He understand there was some little difference between the defendant and her huaband.—Defendant's sister said she would do all she could for her. Mr. Firth (police court missionary) also promised his help and defendant was given into the custody of her friends. DISORDERLY BEHAVlOCR.—Frederick George Wade, 23, Winwick-streot, was fined 4s. for disorderly behaviour in Horsemarket-street on the Bth of September. PITCH AND Toss.—Edwin and Harry Jackson, two youths of 33, New-road, were charged with playing "pitch and toss" for money in a passage off York-street on the sth of Sept ember.—Police-constable Cox said that about 11.50 a.m. on Sunday, the sth, he was in Knutsford-road w hen he received a complaint that the defendants were playing "pitch and toss" for money in the passage off York-street. Witness proceeded there and caught Harry Jackson and obtained their names arra addresses. They were pitching money up.— Defendants were fined 2s. Gd. each. _ _ •</p>
        <p>—..--.- _ OBSTRUCTION.—Frederick Winstonley and Abraham Dale, carters' employed by Mr. Chamberlain at Common Side Farm, Grappenball, were charged with causing obstruction in Grappenhall-road on the 4th September. P.C.Cox said that at 12.50 p.m.on the above date he saw a lurry with two horsea attached and a cart with one horse attached, standing in Grappenhall-road. Witness waited there ten minutes and began to wonder where the horses-had got to when the two defendants came up. He obtained their. names and addresses and told them they would be .reported.—A fine of 9s. was imposed. WiastouT LICENCES.—The following were fined for keeping dogs without licences, William Rue, of Quay Fold, 145.; Mary Gowan, Mill-street, 55.; William McNeil Sloane, 10s. ; George Thomas Gregson, Elizabeth-street, 55.; Joseph Blease, Dale-street, ss. ; and Frederick Hutchinson, Dale-street, ss. TCrESDAY.—Before Messrs. H. Roberts (in the chair), C. Jackson Holmes, W. Richardson and J. Rymer Young. ALLEGED SOLDIERS' VJOLENCE.—MichaeI Gleeson, 8 private in the King's Liverpool Regiment, stationed at Orford Barracks, was charged with having done grievous bodily harm to Arthur Howell, by striking him in the face with his belt in Horsemarket-street the previous night. —The Chief Constable said that it appeared that at a quarter to nine on the previous night prosecutor went into the big Horse Shoe Inn, Horsemarket-street, with a young woman. While there an altercation took place about two other women, and a number of soldiers, among whom was the prisoner, Fathered round. Drummer Lee and prosecutor went into the street apparently to fight and it was alleged that prisoner took off his belt and struck the prosecutor in the eye with it. Prosecutor was attended to by P.C. Dennett, who took bim to the Infirmary, where five stitches had to be placed in a wound near the eye. P.C. Gibbs thought Lee had done the injury and brought him to the Felice Station. Prisoner also came down and he admitted striking the prosecutor with his fists, but denied that he struck him with the belt. Having regard to the serious injury he asked for a week's remand.—The remand was granted. ,TRAVELLER AND HIS EMPLOYER'S GOODS.—John Griffiths, 162, Knutsford-read, was charged that whilst being bailee he did fraudulently convert to his own use a pair of sheets,a red table cloth and a pair of blankets value £l. 14.9., the property of James Wilcock, 64, Leonard street —Mr. Steel for the prosecutor. He said prosecutor, who was a draper, engaged the defendant who was employed by other people, as a commission agent. Defendant was to receive 50 per cent. on all orders received, but prosecutor soon found this to be impossible, and a fresh arrangement was entered into whereby defendant was to receive 25 per cent. on orders received, and 5 per cent. on the collection of accounts. On the 13th March last defendant called upon a Mrs. Barker, of Regent-street, Earlestown, who gave him an order for a pair of sheets, a red table cloth, and pair of blankets. Defendant told prosecutor that he bad got the order, and the goods were banded to him to deliver, Instead of taking the articles to Mrs. Barker, however defendant took them to a Mrs. Mosey, of New-road who, acting upon the defendant's instructions, pawned them at Mr. Jackson's shop, Winwick-street. In order to make prosecutor believe that Mr. Barker was paying weekly instalments, defendant paid him a -shilling occasionally and altogether paid ss. His (Mr. Steel's) instructions were to ask for an adjournment in order tbat further charges might be broughs against defendant but as the result of a conversation. The prosecutor was prepared to let the case stand as at present.— Mr. Jenkins appeared for the defendant and said he bad advised his client to plead guilty. There was tie doubt defendant intended to pay the money back, and therefore he asked the Bench to be merciful. Defendant was a native of the town. For ten years he had been in the employ of the late Mr. H. N. Houghton. and for nine years be was with the Warrington Slate Company, while for five years he had been travelling as a com- mission agent, up to the time he went to Mr. Wilcocks and nothing of this kind had happened previously. It was possible that that experience would be such a lesson to the defendant that they would never find him there again. Defendants employers bad promised to take him back, and he (Mr. Jenkins) hoped he would become an honourable and useful member of society. If the Bench would deal leniently with him defendant would repay Mr. Wilcock all that was indebted to him. —The Chairman said they were surprised and grieved to see a man like defendant there. They were going to take advantage of the Probationers Act, but at the same time they very much appreciated the manner in which Mr. Steel had conducted the case for the prosecution. Defendant would be bound over in the of sum £l.O, and he would have to pay the cost and the advocate's fee (£2 12s. Gd. altogether). SECOND-HAND DEALER SUMMONED.—Mary Ann Wilcocks, Selby-street, was summoned for having received for sale, a pair of clogs, the property of the Warrington Union, from Mrs. Sarah Morris, Liverpool- road, to whom the clogs had been issued.—Mr. Shearer stated that Mrs. Morris received the clogs, wkich had the Warrington Union stamp inside, from the Guardians in order to send her children to school. Mrs. Morris pawned the clogs with Mrs. Wilcocks, who was a second- hand dealer, for Is.—Mr. Garnett, relieving officer spoke to- having given Mrs. Morris the clogs.—Mr. Rutter said be found out that Mrs Morris had pawned the clogs. —The Bench fined defendant 10s. and costs, eud the Chairman, addressing Mrs. Morris, said her conduct was disgraceful in the extreme. The Guardians were good enough to provide the clogs and she showed her grati- tude by pawning them. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. C. White (chairman), Alder- man Smethurst, and Mr. J. W. Haywood. A CONTEMPTIBLE THEFT.—Elizabeth Hibber+, of no settled abode, was charged with stealing from a grave in the Cemetery the previous day four pieces of ribbon, value Is. The Chief Constable stated that a large num- ber of complaints had been received with respect to flowers &amp;c. having been stolen from graves in the Cemetery.—The registrar (Mr. Young) and his son kept observations the previous evening and about a quarter to six the saw the prisoner at the grave of the late Mr. James Newton and saw her take the ribbons from the wreaths that had been sent. Subsequently Mr. Young's eon went to her and she stated that Mr. Newton was a Conservative and he bad no right to have pink ribbon upon his grave. The police were telephoned for, and Detective Clough took prisoner into custody. She had six previous convictions.—Mr. Young, the register, said he bad received many complaints with regard to these thefts and they had one the previous day.—Prisoner was sent to prison for fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ERMAN'S METAL POLISH.</head>
        <p>ERMAN'S METAL POLISH.</p>
        <p>the very best society that had ever existed. As they said He was the greatest reformer, so they said that the hope of the reformation of the future lay with the Catholic Church. As they said the code of moralty He preached was the very finest,' £0 they acknowledged that that same code of morality was preached by the Catholic Church to-day. As they spoke of His patience and goodness, of all those human virtues which it was impossible for anyone to deny to Jesus Christ, so they gave to the Catholic Church those same virtues, and just as they denied to Him that which was the very heart of His being— his divinity—so they denied to the Catholic Church that which was her very heart and centre—her divinity. Tbey acknowledgea Him as a human person, they acknowledged her as a human society. They denied Him as a divine person, they denied her as a divine society. They said polite things about her. They said the Catholic Church was the best police force that bad ever been invented, that they cot ld rule men by love when they could not by force. They said she was the greatest benefit society that had ever existed and they denied that which was to her the very heart of her being—that she was Body of Christ and the one divine society which Jesus Christ instituted: They denied His uniqueness, and they denied hers. They Catholics, if they witnessed to the fact that Jesus Christ was the same yesterday, to-day. and for ever, had to live in a certain attitude towards that society which was His Body. They bad to give to her detisions not merely the grudging obedience of one who thought she was a little wiser than himself. They must not n orely shrug their shoulders and obey because it was the simplest thins to do. They had to stand in their whole attitude towards her as the disciples stood in their attitude towards Jesus Christ namely that she was divine, unique and supreme. She was not merely a club they might join, criticise and leave. She was that society concerning which they had to answer a parallel to that question which our Lord put to His disciples "Whom do men say that I am?' In conclusion Father Benson said they would not convert England by sermons, books, or speeches. The priesthood, he believed, could do very little to concert England but the laity could do almost every- thing. If by their lives the people of England saw them witnessing to Jesus Christ, that they lived as those who trv'y and really believed the Incarnation and that that Incarnation was extended unto the present, that the Sicraments were indeed life-giving fonntlins of grace, if they saw them living as those who believed that the Catholic Church was the divine society, and not merely the best human society that had ever lived, if they saw them living with their faces towards eternity and bright with the light that came from the face of Him who inhabited eternity, then the conversion of England would not be-se far away. _</p>
        <p>Ai: the close of the service there was a procession round the Church, the Abbot bestowing his blessing upon the kneeling congregation. In the evening there was the Service of the Sacred Heart and Pont'fical Benediction. Father Benson preached on "The mark of Holiness."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SHOOTING MATCH.</head>
        <p>SHOOTING MATCH.</p>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB v. NATIONAL SERVICE Saturday last was the occasion or the return match between the Appleton Rifle Club and the National Service League. The weather was fine but the light was not as good as might be wished for. The conditions were as follows :—Each team to consist of ten men, the best eight scores on each side to count. Any rifle, any sights bar telescopic. Distance 160 yards. Seven shots at each of two targets, six inch and 3 inch bull. Highest possible score 70 per man, or an aggregate for each team of 560. When each team had fired on the 6in. bull (that. is, when the match was half over) there was some excitement on finding that the scoring was level, tut the completion of the match showed a victory for the Appleton Club, who made a score of 461 against 454 by the National Service League, thus winning by a majority of 7 points. The following scores were made. ArPLETON RIFLE CLUB. Cinch. 3inch. Total. H. Potts, sen. B. Potts, jun. C. W. Bowyer 3O 28 59 C. P. Day 33 24 57 ' F. C. Bennion 3l 24 55 A. Pitt .3. Sankey 3O 25 55 A. Shepherd 32 ,22 54</p>
        <p>Other scores : E. Pollard J. Towers 29 20 40 NATIONAL SEIIVICE LEAGUE. Capt. H. Owen C. Liblaitor 33 27 60 T. Buttery R. Dope J. wilcock A. Adams 3l 24 ri T. Taylor 3l 24 55 P. Bancroft 3O 22 52 ..--- 454</p>
        <p>Other Scores J. Taylor 2B 20 4R F. Malts</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>The weekly meetings of the Be-wsey Wesley Guild were continued on Tuesday evening when a devotional service xvas held. There was a good attendance. Miss Healey rend a paper on" Prayer "and slid that Christ set a grand example to His followers in respect to prayer. Christ had no sins to confess, no weaknesses to deplore and if He had the need of prayer, how much more had we.</p>
        <p>. . . . . . . . .... ___:-.... ..........._ -.17..."7.. -'''',f,..i...---, S • :•:;.% . Out c. -:-. A•• '-11,-;‹, .. ,:' ' ' 1,:-Rtifi eft - h ,6 ~.,,.. ~ ..i,..„ lute e , . ... ,_.... .....„........,.._-‘, _,.......,..„-.1.:..</p>
