      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a fair attendance at the meeting of this society on Sunday. The secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) pre- sided. Mr. McEachern, Mr. Joseph Owen and the Secretary conducted the meeting in the absence of the appointed speaker who could not be present. Mr. T. Wallington presided at the organ: Five new members received a very hearty welcome. The Chairman expressed the thanks of Mr. Gresty to the brethren for their vote of sympathy and good wishes for his rczovery. The Chairman slid he hoped all the brethi en would do their best to make a very large attendance next Sunday at the open meeting. St. Alban's Church. Solemn Re-Opening--Visit of Father Benson.</p>
        <p>Sunday was a memorable day in the history of St. Alban's Roman Catholic Church, for it witnessed the solemn re-opening of the church, after extensive structural alterations. A description of the work has already appeared in our columns, and all that need be added is that the reconstruction of the front of the edifice and the introduction of large stained glass windows at the west end considerably enhance the dignity of the church, the interior arrangements of which are exceptionally beautiful. In the morning Pontifical High Mass was celebrated with all that splendour of rated which is such a striking feature of the services of the Roman Catholic Church. The high altar was exquisitely embellished with choice flowerer. The Mass was sung by the Abbot of Ampleforth ; the deacons of the throne were the Rev. Fathers Primavesi and Dolan, the assistant priest Rev. Father Disimpel ; and the deacon and sub-deacon of the Mass, Rev. Father Huggins and Brother Marron respectively. Mr. Fishwick, of Liverpool, acted as master of cere- monies. Gounod's "Angel Guardian" Mass was impressively sung by the choir, and Sir Charles Santley's "Ave Maria" was given at the offertory. Mr. Caldwell presided at the orgtn and Mr. Johnson conducted. The services were rendered especially interesting by the fact that the preacher for the day was the Rev. Father R. Hugh Benson, the gifted son of a late -Archbishop of Canterbury. Singularly youthful in appearance, be speaks with an earnestness which com- mands attention. There is no mincing of words, and the fearlessness and directness of his utterances are evidt ntl7 inspired by conviction. Without the aid of manuscript or Rotes, he pours forth a torrent of eloqut ace, and whether the hearer agrees with the views expounded or not the magnetic personality of the speaker compels him to listen. Taking for his text the words "Ye shall be witnesses unto nip," Father Benson said the story of Northern Catholicism was one of the finest stories in the world. When they looked back on the last three hundred years, on the lives of their forefathers, and saw how in spite of every difficulty and every pressure brought to bear, they had kept the faith they ought indeed to thank God. In Lancashire, above all other counties, and in Yorlcsbire, the faith had been preserved when the south of England had lost it, and they who were of Northern blood and he who also, thank God, was of Northern blood, ought to thank God for the strength and generosity of the grace that He gave in order that the faith might be kept. But to keep the faith was not the whole duty of the Christian. Our Blessed Lord, when He sent His Apostles into the world, did not send them merely to remember all that. He had taught them, merely to observe the observances He had laid upon them, merely to remain faithful to Hini in their own lives, but He sent them to do an even greater thing than to keep .the faith, which was to give the faith. Referring to the work of the Church as a witness to God, the preacher said the Sacraments pointed to Jesus Christ as their source and author. They only bad value so far as they came from Him. They were worth very little merely as symbols: they were indeed life-giving fountains of grace made active and energetic by Him who stood behind and admirostered them. The very heart and centre of the creed was Jews Christ. Their devotion to the Saints had value SO far as those Saints stood in relation to Jesus Christ. The august and unique dignity of His blessed mother arose from the fact that she WaS His mother. Every article of the Ca'holic faith and every devotion which it practised and enjoined upon them all pointed to Him and all witnessed to Him, thus felfilling the Commission He gave to His Church at the beginning "Ye shall be witnesses unto Me." It seemed to them sometimes as they looked around the world at the present day, as if in a very short while the only body calling itself Christian which would be perfectly clear and explicit on the doctrine of the incarnation would be the Catholic Church. Three hundred years ago it was not so. When that great catastrophe fell upon this country which was called the Reformation, it was not on the doctrine of the Incarnation that the divergence arose. It was not, in fact, until compara- tively recently that Protestant bodies spoke with a faltering voice about that which was the very foundae tion and centre of the Christian need. It was only Comparatively recently that there had arisen bodies claiming the name of Christian and practising to a very large extent the Christian virtues, reverencing our Lord Jesus Christ as the very greatest reformer and the greatest martyr who had ever lived, but denying to Him that which was the one essential fundamental doctrine of the Catholic Church, concerning Him, namely that He was God of God, Very God of Very God. There was a great deal of flattery of our Lord at the present day amongst those who called themselves Christians. Many were willing to acclaim him as the greatest teacher who bad ever lived, and to confess that the code of morality which He promulgated was the very highest code of morality that had ever been promulgated, People who did not even claim the name of Christian taunted Christian; with not living up to that supreme code of merality which He promulgated upon the Mount of Beatitudes. They said that if Chris- tians only lived out the Sermon on the Mount the whole world would very soon become Christian. The very people who were willing to acknowledge that the life and teaching of our Lord had transformed the world, that the whole civilization as we had it to-day had arisen from them, that Christ was the greatest prophet, teacher, refornler and martyr, item silent %hen we approached that which was ther fundamental doctrine of the Catholic Church concern- ing Him—that be was true God incarrate. He (ihe nreacber) believed tl at this doubt concerning the incarnation had spread very., much further than we were sometimes inclined to think.. He thought there were many persons, members of Christian bodies, who con- fessed with their lips the Incarnation but who really in their hearts denied it. He believed it would not be Ion" before the Catholic Church would be the one Christian body which explicitly and clearly taught that which was taught in the Creed— that lie was of one substance with the Father that he was indeed true God incarnate for us men and for our salvation. Father Benson next emphasised the point that if they believed -the Incarnation unhesitatingly their lives ought to be utterly different from those of people who were in the leist doubtful about it. The man who believed the Incarnation might seem outwardly like the man who did not, but the whole of his heart, and his outlook upon time and eternity, were different. Alluding to a recent book in which the lives of Christians were unfavourably contrasted with the life of a good Pagan, Father Benson said one could not help feeling that the life of the Catholic ought aotteibe able to be put for one instant beside that of the very best Pagon who ever lived. It was not enough for him that a Catholic was no worse than a Pagan, or that he was rather better than other people; he ought to be inraitely better. The Catholic employer and the Catholic servant, the Catholic in every rank of life ought to be distinguished not merely by the outward practices of his religion; not merely by the words upon his lips, hut by every action he performed, by his whole lile as lived before the world. They ought to have that supreme pride which was the grace and glory of the Catholic that they as Catholics were not the same as other people, but different and a great deal Letter. They ought to bear about with them that supernatural atmosphere which was so unmistakable. But merely believing that a certain thing took place two thousand years ago would not produce that atmosphere. Unless there were some means by which that past fact was made present, by which the enormous benefits liberated by tle Incarnation were still extended and spread through the world, they would never hate the supernatural atmosphere which they needed. And they Catholics knew that by the Catholic Church and the Sacraments that Incarnation was extended. Not only did the Sacraments extend the Incarnation but the very Catholic Church was an extension of it. As our Lord lived upon earth two thousand veers ago after wonder- ful fashion, so in this mystical. body he still lived upon earth. Just as at the present day there was a great deal of flattery of Jesus Christ, so there was also a great deal of flattery of the Catholic Church. Just as men spoke of Him merely as the very hest man who had ever lived, so they spoke of the Catholic Church as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WARRINGTON ANGLERS' ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ANGLERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Annual Shield Competition.</p>
        <p>The chief event of the year in connection with the Warrington Anglers' Association—the shield, cup and medal competition—wps decided on Saturday in the Bridgewater Canal at Statham, Lymm. It was open to all members of the Association, anglers from Warring- ton, Lymm, Broadheath, Manchester,etc., being engaged. The conditiors were favomable and moderate sport was enjoyed. Forty-one teams of 6 each, together with 31 individuals (total 277) took part. The result was somewhat of a surprise, the first prize consisting of the silver shield (holders of) and gold medals, being won by the team resaresentipg the Manchester Tradesmen A.S. with the following Catch :</p>
        <p>lb. oz. dr. A. Chambers 0 8 2 W. Thompson C. R. Meachen 0 9 12 J. Robinson J. Newton, sea.</p>
        <p>J. Newton:jun.</p>
        <p>Totpl catch</p>
        <p>The second prize consisting of silver cup (holders of) and medals went to Perseverance No. 1 team (head- quarters, Cattle Market lan,Market-street,Warrington): lb. oz. dr. T. Chadwick 0 4 11 J. Burrows B. Owen 0 512 F. Chads ick 0 0 6 NV. Owen J. Simister 0 1 0</p>
        <p>Total catch .. 2 1 7</p>
        <p>For the individual merit competition there were 20 prizes value £lO and gold medal (the gift of Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), carrying with it the title of champion of the Association. The result was as follows : lb. oz. des. 1 W. Thompson (Manchester) 2 H. Wright (Broadheath) 3 W. Ur.sworth (Warrington) 4 W. Owen ) (Warrington . . 0 13 8</p>
        <p>5 13radbury (Warriniton) 0 12 4 6 W. Millington (Warrington) etc. to 20 prizes. The arrangements, which worked out admirably were conducted by Messrs. P. Knight (general secretary), F. J. Emson (assistant secretary), H. Kent, H. Taylor, A. Cook, S. R. Lodge, J. Watson, G. Johnson and J. Orford members of the Comntittee), F. Singleton and P. Wolfenden. The competition was a decided success. The contest for the Juvenile members of the Associa- tion takes place to-day (Saturday) at Grappenball.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>DO YOU WEIGH TOO MUCH.</head>
        <p>DO YOU WEIGH TOO MUCH.</p>
        <p>THE REAL REMEDY AND THE RECIPE.</p>
        <p>There are some stout peopte whom the above question does not seem to trouble very much. They are, how- ever, wrong to be so careless, fOr obesity is a com- plaint which brings manyevils inits train. There are others who have sought long and earnestly for a really reliable remedy for obesity, and to these the following prescription will be of absorbing interest becau.73 it presents—in a simple and harmless form and sure relief —a remedy which is rapidly gaining a world-wide reputation. The reader may make up the prescripLion for himself (or herself) on getting the ingredients from the chemist— 3r the latter would do so willingly. I.lls is the full recipe :—One half-ounce of Marmots, one ounce of fluid extract of Glycyrrhizs 8.P., one ornce of pure Glycerine B P., and Peppermint Water, to make six ounces in all. Take a dose of two teaspoonfuls after each meal and at bedtime. This admirable and quite harmless remedy will soon take off two pounds of the offending fatty excess; and as, day by day, the weight decreases strength and vigour win return in equal ratio; for be it observed, there is no fasting or exercising required. Rest, good food, regular doses—these are rl.l that is necessary to reduce weight to normal, increase the supply of pure blood, clear the skin, beautify the complexion. and restore energy and cxce"ent spirits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Quite a furore has been caused by the singing of the Alms " L'incognita " at the Hippodrome this week. We should think she is fully entitled to the descaption of" the greatest soprano ever heard on the Vaudeville stage. 'Whoever or whatever she may be she can sing in a style comparable by only a few artistes. Her voice is a pure soprano of very sweet tone and really remarkable range. The highest notes are reached without any effort and are flute-like in their purity. Only in the lower registers does the singer seem to fa'l at all, and here the fault—if fault it be— is solely one of lightness. For ourselves we were wholly delighted with the masked lady's accomplishments. A singer of ber quality has not been heard in War- rington for a long t;me. There is a very good bass singer also on the bill th:s week in the person of Mr. W. C. Webber, who positively revels in "Down in the deep" "In cellar cool," etc. We like the timbre of his voice, but do not admire his somewhat staccato enunciat:on. Halma, the Scottish juggler, is unexcelled in his particular line and some of the feats of legerdemain which he pei Tonna are enthusiastically applauded. One of Halma's exceptional qualities is his speed. The tricks are all carried through at a very rapid rate, which adds to the merit of them. Azella Wilson earns warm applause for her graceful feats on the trapeze, and Cyril Jephson, • light comedian, sings his songs in pleasant style. Harley and Melton, comedy artistes, might with advantage develop some originality and dispense with the vulgarity which occasionally crops up in their per • formance. A smai t, dancing and dialogue turn is given by two" deputies." The Palace pictures are very good, and Mr. Widdoes orchestra does excellent service, particularly in the accompar:ments to " L'in'cognita."</p>
      </div>
