      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WOMEN WHO NAB.</head>
        <p>WOMEN WHO NAB.</p>
        <p>Not long since a physician said he believed that, nine times out ,of ten, when a woman "nags," she is tired.. Tired, mark you, not because of too much work, but too littte strength; the physical and mental- tire, the fagged-out feeling which is born of indiges- tion. That makes women shrews, men•angry brutes. Indigestion is a sort of starvation;and starving people are seldom pleasant company. The cure is food food eaten with a relish and digested perfectly, just as a perfect mill grinds grain. When your stomach is out of order it cannot do perfect work. Stagnant food ferments and gives off poisons, instend of the rich nourishment that goes tc: built up and sustain good blood, muscle, nerve anu bone. This poison first taints the blood and is thus carried by it all through the body, dulling the braill and producing biliousness, constipation, dizziness, wind and -pains in the stomach, loss of appetite. 31" - Maria Peacock, of Flamstead, near Dunstable, wrote not long ago, saying : " his now between 17 and and 18 years since I first used Mother Seigel's Syrup. My complaint Iv" indigestion. My appetite was poor, the slightest exertion fatigued me, and I was always drowsy particularly after eating. Sometimes I was so dizzY that I was in danger of falling. However, a few bottles of Mother Seigel's Syrup thoroughly freed rue of these ailments. I have ne,ver since been troubled with Indigestion, but I have often given the Syrup t° my children for their ordinary ailments and always with good results." Mother Seigel's Syrup is made of the curativo extracts of certain roots, barks and leaves. It cures in_ the healthy, natural way, by toning and strengthen- ing the organs of digestion. It makes food nourish you and thus builds health on good digestion. Th° 2s. Od. bottle contains three times as much as the Is. lld. size.</p>
      </div>
