      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MESSRS. PEARSON AND KNOWLES. --- Directors' Annual Report.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. PEARSON AND KNOWLES. --- Directors' Annual Report.</p>
        <p>IE2O4OIOIETM.W. efiliz MCIONT,</p>
        <p>The 36th ordinary general meeting of the Pearson and Knowles Coal ,and Iron Co. Ltd. will be held et the company's offices in ‘tiarrington on Monday, the 27th %a., at-12 iio3ll.—The following report will " submitted by the directors :—The profits for the year ending June 30th, 1909, amount to £61,890 2s. 4d., aq the balance brought forward from last year is £12,057 2s. id., together £73,977 4s. sd. An interim dividend bt the rate of 6 per cent. per annum on the preference shares end 3 per cent. on the ordinary shares was pal,d in March last for the half-year ending December 316't 1908, amounting to £15,817 10s. The directors DV' recommend that a dividend he paid for the past 119 year at the rate of 6 per cent. per annual on LB° preference shares and of 6 per cent. Peif annum on the ordinary shares, making wit' the interim dividend 4.1 per cent, for the year, and a total distribution of £10,220. Tho directors further recommeni that £lO,OOO be added t° the reserve fund, raising it to £18(,000; that the sum £9,240, which represents the expenditure on 03051 account for the year be written off, and that tbe balance of £14,517 4.4. sd. be carried forward. Tbe work of repairing the damage done by the explosion et• the Maypole Pit of the Moss Hall Coal Co.,Ltd.,referrel to in the last report has not yet been completed and some considerable eme must elapse before the t,ollierY can be in working order. The trade of the year hag been carried on wider circumstances of general depres: sion, and almost all departments of the company's bus'- ness have suffered in consequence. Orders have bee° scarce end prices low, and it has been impossible to maintain the full output of either collieries or iron' works. The present pcsition is still unsatisfactorY• In compliance with the a:ticles of association, Mr. J. Harmood Banner. M.P., retires from the Board and eligible for re-election. The auditors,Messrs. Blease err. Sons, also retire, and are eligible for re-election. S. Hartatoon Banner, Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BOWLING. WARRINGTON WORKS ASSOCIATION. Annual Hand!cap.</head>
        <p>BOWLING. WARRINGTON WORKS ASSOCIATION. Annual Hand!cap.</p>
        <p>The annual handicap in connection with the Warring- ton Works Association, which has this year proved a conspicuous success, was brought to a close on Satur- day afternoon when some interesting games were decided on the Bowling Green, Bank Quay. The ful- lowing are the details :</p>
        <p>FIFTH' ROUND. Ike Barlow l5 J. Warburton R. Brobbin W. Norgrove l5 J. Gilbert W. Thomason 35 W. Roughley W. Bibby l5 M. Clare H. Lindley C. North l5 T. Heir by lO H. Wright Ted Gleave l5 W. Barton (Fletchers) 9 C. Millington ' Jack Gleave G. Monk W. Lawton l5 T. Jackson l4 E. Willsams R. Looker J. Nock</p>
        <p>SIXTH ROUND. W. Norgrove Jack Gleave lke Barlow l5 Ted Gleave .1. Nock W. Thomason l5 C. North R. Brobbin R. Looker W. Lawton</p>
        <p>SEVENTH ROUND. - R. Looker l5 J. Nock 4 Jack Cleave 35 Ike Barlow lO W. Thomason 35 R. Brobbin l3 W. Lawton</p>
        <p>SEMI- FINA R. Looker.. l5 W. Lawton W. Thomqaon l5 Jack Gleave (holder) 7 FINAL.</p>
        <p>W.Thoroason,Monks H. 15--h. Looker, Ropery ... 7 Looker led 4-3, but after peeling at fire Thomason led throughout. the card reading: 7-5. 9-5, 11-6, 11-6, 13-6, 13-6, 14-6, 14-7, and 15-7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>PRESENTING THE CUP.</head>
        <p>PRESENTING THE CUP.</p>
        <p>At the close of the handicap, Lady GREENALL, the donor of the cup, presented it to the %inner. Mr. WILLETT (chairman of the committee) said they had reason to be thankful that not one of the season's matches had been interfered with by hod weather. It was always a pleasure to the committee to see men from the various works there every Saturdey. They got better acquainted with their fellow-men and there was not that ill-feeling between the workers of one firm and another which used to exist. They played in friendly rivalry and knew how to take defeat. Mr. ViJ tett conclude I by expressing indebtedness to the donors of prizce. Mr. WEBSTER, a vice-president of the Association said they were delighted to see Lady Greenall encouraging the skilful game of bowls, in which the workingmen took such a deep interest, and she would eventually lecome quite an expert at the game. The works competition and the individual merit handicap had each established a record this season, there being 274 entries for the letter event. The prize money amounted to between £3O and £4O. There was a great deal of labour entailed in organizing the competitions, but it was a labour of love. (Applause.) Lady GREENALL then handed the cup to the winner. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, M.P., in proposing a vote of thanks to Lady Greenall, said they all appreciated very highly the distinction which Lady Greenall had won as a sportswoman in the hunting field. (Applause.) It was t berefo:e peculiarly appropriate that she should present tbe first prize at a gathering of Warrington sports- men like that. It was not only in bowls that Warrington had been to the fore, but in marry other of the outdoor sir arts and rec-eations of old England. Local exponents had made their mark in cricket, football, athletics and Iswimming—and he must not forget that his old friend Mr. Charles Hutchings established a record in the golf world by winning the championship when be was fifty- three and a grandfather—(laughter), beating all the youog bloods and securing a record which he (Mr. Crosfield) ventured to say neither amateurs nor professionals acre going to break in a hurry. Certainly in the time-honoured game of bowls Warrington:ans had every reason to be on good terms with themselves because this year the Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club had carried off the first place in the Lancashire and Cheshire County Bowling Association—(applause)—and the wonderful success of the Works Bowlirg Association was also one of the healthiest and best features in the outdoor recreative life of the town. He was sure that Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall, like himself, hoped that the association would prosper and increase in numbers in the Wm-v. (Applause.) Councillor BROADHURST, in seconding, congratulated Mr. Thomason on his victory which would be regarded as an honour by his fellow-workers of Messrs. Monks, Hail and Co.</p>
        <p>The vote wrs heartily passed Lady GREENTALL, in responding, said it was the greatest pleasure to come down thete and Lee such a gathering of sportsmen—and when she said sportsmen she meant that that term embraced everything that was good and straight. England would not be the nation it was to-day—the envy and admiration of every other nation in the world—if it were not for the sportsmen and the workingmen who were the backbone of the countt y. (Applause). The .prizes are to he presented by the President (Captain G. Crosfield) in the Club Room, Bowling Green Inn, on Monday, Septsmber 27th.</p>
        <p>YORKSHIRE v. LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE</p>
        <p>There was a battle royal for the Crosfield Cup and medals on Saturday, when these counties, having tied in the competition,played off. The event is decided on the aggregate of the two pames. One portion of the decid- ing match was played at Halifax cu Saturday. The green at Thrum Hall was in good condition but there was a drizzling rain during the afternoon. Mr. J. C. Cartwright WtiS the referee, and Messrs. IT Taylor (Yorkshire), and G. Leek (Lancashire) were the measurers. Lancachire and Cheshire obtained a lead early and finished this section of the match 43 points ahead of Yorkshire. Scores : YORKSHIRE. LANCASHIRE &amp; CHESIIIRE. J. Hirst, Spelt Victoria 21 A Gleave,WWton B.H. 18 F. Eastwood, Brighouse 16 11.11arrison,L'ghRlw'y 21 %V. Spencer, !Oland F.Savoury,Cre.dock&amp;Co 8 S.Sackville,S'nenLib'l 21 H. Squire, (cant.) Clifton 20 HS. Connolly,Roebuck 21 W. Ratcliffe, Halifax J. Stead, Spen Victoria 2l T. Ellison, W'g't'n Cern 9 W. Walker, Holbeck A. Cropper, Dodworth F.Wilson,BrighouseAlb'n 17 T.B.Burgess,Win gt'nEl 21 W. 11. Wilson, Cleckh'en 21 C. W. Smith, Roebuck 15 J.T. Mitchell, Horb'y Old 6 11. Arnold Didsbut y... 21</p>
        <p>184 227 The games at Frotton were keenly contested through- out, and in the finish the combined counties finished up 13 points in front. Deta'ls : LANCASHIRE .454 CHESRLAIP. YORKSHIRE:.</p>
        <p>_ J. W. Coom hes (capt.) 21 B. Eastwood, Paddock 4 W. Smith, Belmont J. Fairhurst, Chorlton l5 G. E. Thewlis (capt) ... 21 G. Ball, jun. Wr-rington 21 E. Dyson, Sheepbridge 13 J. Westhead, Illrpurhey 19 W. Lodge Waterloo... 21 G. NV. Corner. Roebuck 21 H.Hcaton,Lockwood C. 12 A. Scott, Warrington H. Clarke. George 2l F.Heaton,Lockwocd C. 19 G. Burnell, Unsworth lB W. Oldham, Faylor H. 21 T. Dutton, Bowdon 2l G. Taylor, Punch Bowl 19</p>
      </div>
