        <p>The grass growing experiments of Mr. Ernest Parke, of Kineton, Warwickshire, car led out under the advice of Dr. Dyer, have now reached their eighth year, and the crop recently gathered again shows the remarkable results obtainable by literal treatmtnt of land which although possessed of inherent capacity for heavy cropping, bas fallen ',uto a condition of poverty through long neglect. Certain portions of two fields on Mr. Parke's farm (ion remarkable for its poverty when he took it over) continue to le treated as they bad been trcatrd for years, namely, mown in the summer and unmanured except for the natural manuring due to ,annual autumn grazing. These areas have this year yielded respectively only 14 and 15 cwt.of hay per acre, desrite the moist season. In one field the yield has been increased to 43cwt. of bay per acre by the use of 3cwt. of superphosphate and I kwt. of nitrate of soda per acre, and in the other to 46cwt. by the use of sowt. of basic slag 'and 11cwt. of nitrate. The increase—in both cases,about 11 ton of hay per acre—bas herr' produced at aniiverage cost of well under 30s. per acre, or less than .£1 a ton for the extra hay, plus, of course; the cost of handling the iccreased lulk. There are also plots receiving phos- phat, s and nitrate separately, and plots on which potash silts (not ouch needed, apparently, on this particular land) are included in the dressings; but the most satisfactory results, taking into account quality of herbage as well as quantity, are those quoted.</p>
        <p>We are standing on ground thrice historic. Its commanding position made the hill a place of strength as far back as a millenium ago, and here Ethelfreda, that fine, kindly yet heroic and strong-minded woman, the sister of King Alfred reigned as Protectoress and uncrowned Queen. She is said to have founded a strong tower here, but no trace remains to interest the antiquary. There are however many remnants of the stern old days of our remote ancestors. .Big defen- sive ditches surround the hill and the earth works of the Saxons and Romans may still be traced. In this big enclosure stood the fort and gave refuge at various times to Saxon and Roman,and as we look down on the Roman Road which runs over the shoulder of the hill we can picture, if imagination be good enough, the soldiers and pack horses that travelled from Deva to Manchu Hu m—the Chester and Manchester of long ago. If the pilgrim cares to push yet further back into the dim recesses of history he can look on the small" lows," or -hillocks, at his feet and see there the traces of earlier races still. These mounds shelter the remains of neolilhic man and the British Museum has some earthenware dug from them to show what kind of domestic vessels were used before the dawn of history. Descending from the hill the traveller may take his choice-of ways. If he be a botanist he will seek the shores of Oakmere and revel among its grasses and plants. If he be a geologist he will add his speculation to the rest as to whether these great inland lakes—Pickmere, Budworth Mere, Oak Mere, Hatch Mere— are due to the mining operations below or are the remains of a great sea that once covered Cheshire.</p>
        <p>For ourselves we choose to see Vale Royal. Should the pilgrim be on foot he will here have the advantage of the cyclist and may take a pleasant bye-path which wanders through woods until it reaches the shores of a fine lake that lies in the middle of the old park of Vale Royal.</p>
        <p>S _ , Y 4, ... . , • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>gaithissi , ~ ' ' ', – . _, ..,, :</head>
        <p>gaithissi , ~ ' ' ', - . _, ..,, :</p>
        <p>MeN•d\,ow".M."."%e+o\ o'N. #\.f.d'\,eo%. SUMMER PILGRIMAGES. DELAMERE AND YALE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>A fine day in autumn and a sojourn under the forest trees (let the owner of jaded nerves take note) is a panacea for most of the ills the modern mind is heir to. Whether we take headaches, or forebodings, we are pretty sure to leave them and bring back in their place new hopes and interests. We offer no advice as to the pilgrim's plan for getting there. For ourselves we again enrich the railway company and make our, beginning from Acton Bridge. Picking our way through Crowley and Norton we descend to Hatchmere and from thence enter upon what is surely one of the lovliest rides in England, two miles through the unspoiled glades of the forest of Delamere. It is a place to let sentiment run free and one could recall many stories and memories connected with the lonely roads orkhe forest. But our rest under the trees must be brief and the stories must wait, for we have much journeying to do to-day. _ _</p>
        <p>Deldmeie Forest is n-ot what it was. It is not the "forest primeval" of which Longfellow sang. The marsh and bog have been drained and good potatoes are grown on the rich soil that is left, but as the cyclist travels the romantic roads he is covering the ground that from the dawn of history has been forest land.</p>
        <p>Until the last century vast stretches of bog and morass were the terror of the traveller; to-day the forest with its growth of fine young firs is an altogether delight- some thing. Here and there arel.patches of oak and beech that tell of antiquity, but for the most part the forest is young—young as forests ,are reckoned--and no one could desire to have it different.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>EDDISBURY HILL.</head>
        <p>EDDISBURY HILL.</p>
        <p>Our first objective shall be Eddisbury Hill. To reach it we skirt the forest on our way to Kelsall and joining the Roman" Waping Street " travel east for a couple of miles. For the pilgrim who comes on his first journey no better beginning could be made than this, for though Eddisbury is only 580 feet above the sea it lifts us high enough above the level of Cheshire to give a fine panorama. On the day that we climb it the air is clear and the country lies at our feet like a many- coloured map. To the south Beeston and Peckforton Castles rise up sharp above the sea of foliage. To the north Halton Castle rises with almost equal clearness. At our feet is Oakmere and the fine Roman thorough- fare Watling Street. To the west the river Dee glimmers in the sun and we strain our eyes expecting to catch a glimpse of the sea beyond.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TIIE HOME OF ETHELFREDA.</head>
        <p>TIIE HOME OF ETHELFREDA.</p>
        <p>VALE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>This lordly old house is the abode of the ancient family of Cholmondeley. If the pilgrim is writing the name he may use his own judgment as to the spelling, for an antiquarian friend of ours, who has time for such things, says he has found it spelled in 23 different ways in the family's own docu- ments. But if be be pronouncing it he deviates from all the 23 versions and says "Chumley." (An untutored caller at Vale Royal once asked if Lord Chol-mon-de-ley were at home. "No"said the butler with scorn "neither aint any of his pe-o-ple." Let the pilgrim beware.) The hall is a medley of a house and tells little of the past. The front door is the old door of the Abbey, of that there can be little doubt, and it has stood the centuries well.</p>
        <p>Little is left of the old Abbey, and the expectations we have formed after reading up our history are doomed to disappoint- ment. A curious four-sided cross on a column stands on a raised bit of ground, and is called the Nun's grave. Other little links with the past are the white cows which have been kept for long, long years. The legend says that when all was stolen a white cow with red ears came and sustained the starving brethren of the Abbey with her milk. And there are also eagles—real eagles—kept in cages and fed on rabbits, cats and rats. But before the visitor can appreciate these things he must take a copious draft of history; and this we must now proceed to adminster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>STORY OF VALE ROYAL ABBEY.</head>
        <p>THE STORY OF VALE ROYAL ABBEY.</p>
        <p>When Henry 111. died, Edward, his son and heir was returning from one of the Crusades in Palestine. He hastened home to take the throne, but when on the water was overtaken by a terrific storm. They gave up hope of saving the ship, and it seemed as though Edward would never live to be hailed as Edward 1., but in his extremity he prayed a prayer and vowed a vow. The priests of course were within ear-shot,and one of them thus recorded the prayer:</p>
        <p>Virgin Mother! Mary blest! Thou alone can'st give us rest! Should we 'England see again I will raise to thee a lane Where till time shall be no more Priests in chorus shall adore Holy name of Mary good And King Edward's gratitude Thus shall to the world proclaim Virgin Mary's blessed name Who to hithlielp in clanger came.</p>
        <p>Whether he said this or not we don't profess to know. The poetry sounds too good for a drowning man. But this is what they told him he said, and that was good enough. The clergy chose the spot and they chose well. They were to have this rich and beautiful stretch of country as their very own and the King was to build them a fine Abbey to match.</p>
        <p>And so it came to pass that in August, 1277, Edward himself was in this beauteous place to lay the foundation stone.</p>
        <p>THE PRIESTS AS LANDLORDS.</p>
        <p>One would have thought that in getting Abbey and lands they would have been content. But to satisfy an ecclesiastic is a difficult-thing, and to our modest reading of history has never been done. They modestly asked for powers not only over the land but over all the dwellers and cattle thereon. They were to be judges, police, magistrates, and everything else, and all the fines and penalties went into their exchequer. When a poor native died the abbot was entitled to his best beasts and part of his stock, the widow and children being graciously allowed the leavings. A man could not permit his child to be married without " feeing " the abbot to a respectable amount, and the abbot fixed the price. These dear spiritual fathers were thought- ful men and did not overlook trifles, for they stipulated that they were to have power to buy fowls and ducks from their tenants at twopence each—and have their pick. When they opened the abbey they had— no, not a service—a big feast and ate por- poises. And we are gravely assured—that is to say, they assured other people—that they were so holy that when souls groaned in purgatory one benediction(from the abbot would shoot them straight into heaven. But this did not last. The people became restless. They shot the abbot's horse (the shooter getting hanged, of course) and when they got chance they played football with a monk's head. The Civil powers put them down and things dragged on until a later critical day. At the time of the Reformation the Abbot happened to say that" the Kyng's grace was not lawfully wed." He was a plucky man to say that of Henry, for had not Henry become the head of the Church? He paid for his courage at the end of a rope. Then came the day when the King decided to plunder the monasteries for his own ends, and we can imagine the joy with which these poor starved wretches, who had lived in dread of the Church all their days, rushed in to help the soldiery to destroy the buildings and carry away the spoil. The Abbey has gone but there is some fine old glass that belonged to it and a richly carved old chest made of thick planks of oak. THE KING'S SHIPWRECK AND "HIS OTHER MAJESTY."</p>
        <p>One of the windows we must not fail to describe. It commemorates the founding of the Abbey, and is placed above the front door. At the top the Deity in heavewsur- rounded by companies of angels looks down upon Edward I. in his dire calamity of ship-' wreck. The king is holding up in his hand a model of the Abbey he is promising to build, and beneath him is his endangered</p>
        <p>ship. All around are demons who blow the waves and winds, and, worst of all, at the bottom is the much-dreaded Lucifer himself tugging hard at a rope to drag the ship down. As you look at it the odds all seem in favour of the old man at the bottom, and he is. by the by, the real frightful old. person- age that the monks believed in without a trace of the respectability that has attached to him in modern days. The beholder will not recognise in this horned and tailed and clumsy gentleman the one who drafts prospectuses and presides at the Board meetings of Limited Companies. But he is far easier to understand. When we came home after this surfeit of Abbots and Kings we took down a book which carries us back to pre-Reforma- tion days. Did the gentle pilgrim ever read" The Cloister and the Hearth " ? The historians dwell among Kings and Abbots. Charles Reade bids us dwell among the common people. Let the reader learn the sorrows of the poor wretches who had for their masters the nobles on the one side and the priests on the other, and he will never- more talk nonsense about " Merrie England" and" the good old days."</p>
        <p>A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>Photo</p>
        <p>TIIE LEN'</p>
        <p>There was considerable excitement in Warrington shortly before seven o'clock on Saturday evening when an airship was seen gracefully sailing over the town. This is the first time that Warrington has been visited by one of these new vehicles of flight and eager stectators soon filled, the streets. " Have you seen the airship? " was the question of the evening, and the most sceptical had to • •admit that the conquest of the air is no longer confined to the realm of imagination, but bids fair soon to become an accouiplished fact. The airship came from Manchester and, aided by an easterly wind, was-travelling at a speed of about twenty miles an hour on reaching Warrington. The most prominent feature of, the contrivance was an elongated balloon, SOft, in length and egg-shaped in appearance. Depend- ing from the balloon was a light framework, in the centre of which was a large square basket, in which three men were distinctly visible. After passing over the town, the airship descended in the stackyard of Mr. Baxter Bennett, of the Grange Farm, Great Sankey, where the balloon was quickly deflated. In a remarkably short time a large crowd had appeared en the scene with the object of making a closer inspec- tion of the airship, and they watched with int( rest the</p>
      </div>
