        <p>Mak 48</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>ING TIIE warE CITY ON ITS JOUUNEY</p>
        <p>work of detaching the framework, and packing up the machine. for the return journey to Manchester by train.</p>
        <p>The airship is the Spencer No. 3, and was in charge of Mr. Henry Spencer, of Spencer Bros., London,-who have already achieved considerable success in aerial navigation. The gas vessel is 80 feet long, 30 feet in diameter, and contains when filled 40,000 cubic feet of gas. The machine is driven by a 6 h.p. petrol motor, affixed to a bamboo framework. The frame- work is divided into three sections, the fore part con- taining the motor and the centre portion the basket in which the aeronauts are accommodated. It is interesting to note that the machine is the same in which the suffragettes made a circuit round the Houses of Parliament earlier in the year. Mr. Spencer expressed -himSelt as being highly pleased with the success of his flight. He ascended from the White City in Manchester at five minutes to six on Saturday evening, in company with Mr. Frank Goodden (his assistant) and the secretary of the life- boat demonstration.iihich was being held there. He toured round the White City for a short time and thm took a cross-country fight in the direction of War- rington,crossing the Manchester Ship Canal and various</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>flu MiSCELLANY.</head>
        <p>flu MiSCELLANY.</p>
        <p>Tuesday last was the centenary of the death of the famous Warrington printer, William Eyres. His reputation as a printer extended far beyond his native town, and he took a place in the front rank of the printers of the eighteenth century. His best work was unsurpassed by any of his contem- poraries. It was the reputation of Eyres' Press that drew John Howard, Thomas Pennant and many others to Warrington. We hope soon to pay homage to Eyres, .and give some account of his work. ** * *</p>
        <p>Another centenary just celebrated is that of the birth of Oliver Wendell Holmes, the brightest and cheeriest of American writers. The author of the Autocrat "sneaked in," as he used to say, along with Gladstone,Tenny- son, Darwin and the other famous ones who were born in 1809.</p>
        <p>The celebration has produced, as might be expected, a crop of stories about the genial author, and the American papers have crowded their columns with notes upon his sayings and doings. We venture to quote a few which have not appeared on this side of the Atlantic.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Holmes contributed to a certain bazaar a letter for" the post-office." Placing a one- dollar bank-note between the leaves of a sheet of paper, he wrote on the first page : " Dear lady, whoso'er thou art, Turn this poor page with trembling care ; But hush, oh, hush thy beating heart, The one thou lov'st will be there."</p>
        <p>Turning the page in obedience to this injunction, the one-dollar "greenback" was disclosed, and to prove the truth of his</p>
        <p>assertion, the Doctor had written the follow- ing appeal on the opposite page :</p>
        <p>"Fair lady, lift thine eyes_ and tell</p>
        <p>if this cs-not a truthitaletter ? This is the one (I) thou lovest well, And naught (0) would make thee lore it better (10). ** * *</p>
        <p>He was often beset by visitors who merely called out of curiosity to see the literary Hog. He was not only lionise 4 but as he said "positively elephantised." The poet was always courteous, and, indeed, lib never seemed averse to praise. Once, after an especially large inroad of these complimen- tary callers, the Doctor was asked whether he did not enjoy it. "Enjoy it!" he exclaimed, "Why, I felt like the small elephant at the Zoo, with a cheap excursion party on his back ! "</p>
        <p>* * *, *</p>
        <p>Holmes's wit overflowed in his briefest notes to friends and to mere acquaintances. Here, for example, is the way he announced to his sister the birth of his eldest son, now Justice Holmes of the United States Supreme Court:</p>
        <p>"March 9, 1841.</p>
        <p>"My dear Ann,—Last evening between eight and nine there appeared at No.B Mont- gomery Place a little individual who may be hereafter addressed as.</p>
        <p>Holmes, Esq., or</p>
        <p>The Hon. His Excellency</p>
        <p>Holmes, M. C. ..Holmes, Presi-</p>
        <p>dent, etc., etc. - but who for the present is content with scratching his face and sucking his right forefinger."</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>On resigning his professorship in the medical college Dr. Holmes gave a sly " dig " at the modern craze for a big staff: "There were six of us who taught the medical graduates of Harvard College during a considerable part of the time when I was professor there. , There. are now seventy. How much better they are taught I do not know. I presume they are taught well. But a wicked thought came into my head just now—it is not every animal that has the most legs who crawls the fastest."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>TO WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>Manchester Gun rdian.</p>
        <p>railway lines with which he was unfamilar. Reaching Warrington he thought it advisable to descend, as darkness was coming on. The open fields on the westerly,side of the town seemed the most desirable for a successful descent, and be threw out the trail rope immediately after crossing the Liverpool and St. Helens 6anal. His approach to the Grange Farm, Great Sankey, was witnessed by Mrs. Bennett and a friend, and they were rather startled to hear a voice exclaim : "Now girls, get hold of this guide. rope." Notwithstanding the unusual nature of the request, the rope was seized, and with the assistance of bin Bennett, the airship was guided into the stack- yard, where the descent was safely completed without the slightest difficulty. It was about five minutes to seven o'clock when the descent Was n ade, .so that the flight had occupied an hour andthe distance covered was twenty miles. The highest altitude attained on the flight was 1,500 feet. '1 he airship was conveyed to the Central Station on Saturday night, on a lurry provided by Mr. Bennett and was subsequently sent on to Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WHOSE HAND?</head>
        <p>WHOSE HAND?</p>
        <p>EY ANNIE 0. TIBBITS.</p>
        <p>Author of "The Love that Won," "Hunted Down," "Hidden Hands," "The Finger of Fate," "Robes of Shame," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XX (Continued). "What is it?" asked Mrs. Cathcart, quickly. Ethel was r,taring wildly at the photograph, and Dick, Ivith a sudden nervousness, crossed the room towards her. As he did so she started up and held it out to him. "It's</p>
        <p>" Dick ! " she said, breathlessly. Olga ! "</p>
        <p>He took it in silence. Mrs. Cathcart looked from one to the other uneasily. Dick was staring at the face in the silver frame like one who looked at the face of the dead, and Ethel had grown desperately white. "Is anything the matter?" Mrs. Cathcart asked at last. "Is there—anything wrong ? " No one made any reply for a moment. Then suddenly Dick turned, and, with a, shaking hand, gave Ethel back the photo- graph. " It is a mistake," he said. —look again." Something in his eyes, some strange tone In his voice, made her obey in silence. "It is—it is strapgely like," he added, in thu same queer voice and with his eyes never leaving her face. "But it is not the same. You will see it if you look again, Ethel." With an effort she tore her eyes away front his and bent her head, then she looked up again wildly, imploringly. It was Olga's face, Olga's face undoubtedly, tnmistakably—Olga as they had seen her in the flesh only a few hours before. She under- stood suddenly his meaning in denying it. He was thinking of her brother's happiness. NVell, she could obey him now, there, beforo Mrs. Cathcart, who loved Olga, but the truth would have, to come out later. She rose to her feet and held out the photograph to Mrs. Cathcart. "It is the most extraordinary likeness I ever saw," she said, "and we thought for a moment it—it was someone else. We—we didn't know Olga Henderson, but we made inquiries about her for—for certain reasons which we cannot tell you at present. Thank you very much for seeing us." She held out her hand. Mrs. Cathcart caught it in hers. "There has no Maui come to Olga Henderson, has there?" she cried.</p>
        <p>"Look again</p>
        <p>Ethel shook her lead. "Will you tell me if you get news of her?" Mrs. Cathcart added.</p>
        <p>"I will put you in communication with her Itt_once—directly I can," Ethel answered. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cathcart' looked at her in 'a puzzled ray, as if loth to let her go. "I hope no harm has come to her," she said, slowly. " was afraid. She was so alone' and there was a man named Perry, Sam Perry, whom • I distrusted, and I—think she was afraid of him. He was always calling, and his wife, too, and they were bad people; in fact, I be- lieve the wife is in prison now for something she has done. They used to live near here, and I was always afraid that in some way Olga would come to grief through them, though I could never tell how." Ethel drew a sharp breath and then turned to go. "We will let you know—if any harm should have come to her," she repeated, dully, and then Mrs. -Cathcart and Dick followed her from the room, Mrs. Cathcart Jetting them out, and standing on her door- step to watch them cross the road.</p>
        <p>As soon as they were out of sight Ethel turned to Dick. "It was Olga," she said. "Oh. what does it mean? What can it mean?" a "It means," Dick said, slowly, "that Olga Murray was the wife of Stephen Henderson, and that it will break Geoffrey's heart." • Ethel stood still. All that it meant she could not at that moment realise, but it was enough to ruin all their lives, she thought. Olga a dead man's wife and a murderess by her own confession? She dared not think. She caught Dick's arm. "Come along," sho cried, huskily. "It will drive me mad if I stop to think now. I think I will go and do What shopping mother wants. I must have some excuse for to-day. I think I will go now, Dick. I don't think I can stay any longer, I must think of something else." Dick agreed, and in a sort of nightmare they drove to Regent-street, and there, with those hideous things beating in her brain, she bought her mother's silks and her own clothes.</p>
        <p>She sent Dick away; a man was worried by shopping, and he had other things to do. "I will be at the station ready for the five o'clock train," she said, and at five o'clock she was there, waiting for him to appear, when .a messenger-boy came up to her. "Are you Miss Verriker? " he asked. "The gent said I was to look for a lady such as you, and there's a letter for you, miss." Ethel snatched it from him and read: "I have stumbled upon another clue, which -I must follow .up. Don't wait: I will be down by the next train if possible.—llwx."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CHAPTER XXI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI.</p>
        <p>All day Olga had paced restlessly up and down her room. It was only when someone came in to see her that she sat still. Directly they had gone she began again—pa - ing like a wild thing in a cage, feeling like a rower drifting with the stream, and waking suddenly to find that she was being sucked along by a current that she could not resist. It was dragging her swiftly now, dragging her to destruction.</p>
        <p>She could hear the roar of the rapids in her ears growing nearer everF hour. Ques- tions throbbed in her brain—ugly questions that threatened to drive her mad. Why were DIA and Ethel gone to London? . Was it only shopping, or was that an excuse for something else? Dick hoped to prov. 3 his innocence. Would he try to-day? Would he find out anything to-day? She felt breathless as she walked up and down, breathless with suspense and excite- ment.</p>
        <p>Suddenly a sound brought her to a stand- still before her open window. She bent and looked out. Someone was in the gardens beyond.</p>
        <p>She waited for them tip come into sight and then her face changed suddenly. On the grass in front of the terrace two or three people, were walking. She recognised them with a start. Mrs. Dunston and Mrs. \Ter- riker. were ahead, and Julia and Geoffrey following, and as she looked they were over- taken by Edward. A sudden fear swept over her. Her heart stood still, and she leant, gripping with both hands at the window-sill.</p>
        <p>. Then, after a minute, she turned and began brushing her hair. She would go down. She WAS Afraid of Julia—afraid even to Fee her at Geoffrey's side,- and as• she saw her now a sudden rage at her own folly for not explain- ing the " accident " of the other night seized her. Why had she not spoken? Why had she not denounced Julia and -Edward there and. then Why had she kept silence ? She dressed .rapidly, and in ten minutes was walking downstairs. A housemaid who met her stared at her stupidly for a moment. "Why, miss, I didn't kiio.w you were com- ing down," she said. "Shall I help you?" Olga shook her head, resting heavily on the bannisters. "No—no, thank you, but please instead go And tell Sir Geoffrey that I am coming down. Tell him I will come and have tea with them in the garden," Olga said. The girl obeyed, and when Olga reached the terra ct Geoffrey was there to meet her. His face had brightened eagerly. "How glad I am to see you down," he cried, "but do you really feel well enough to come, darling Has the headache gone ? " She nodded, and then put her arm through his. Together they went down to the lawn where the rest were seated. Mrs. Dunston put up a pair of lorgnettes and looked at her curiously. Me had not seen her since the "accident," and for a moment Olga fancied that Julia looked uncomfortable. The next she was lauchinc loudly at some joke which</p>
        <p>Edward had made, and was laconically shak- ing hatuls with her while Edward stood at her side. _</p>
        <p>---;We only came to see how you were," Mrs. Dunston said, "and now we. will bc</p>
        <p>going." "Nonsense," cried Geoffrey; • "don't go yet, it is early." He looked at his watch. I say, mother, get them all to stay to dinner. The last party hadn't a happy ending, but let's try again. Mrs. Dunsten; do agree: Will you stay and dine to-night?" Mrs. Dunston threw out her hands with a deprecating gesture. "How can we?" - she cried. "If we were dressed-"_</p>
        <p>"Oh, bother dress," cried Geoffrey. "Olga isn't well enough ..to go and change, and mother won't mind for once, will you mother? And Ethel will only get -back at the last minute and won't have time. So stay, Mrs. Dunston—please do. We'll have an in- formal party. Edward doesn't want to dress, do you? And we'll just be free and easy, and after dinner we'll have the fountains lighted, ifithout fear of any of you catching cold if you come out to look at them."' He settled it all gaily, and they all fell in. Olga saw, with a qualm, that Julia gave Edward a curious look while Geoffrey was speakino.° to Mrs. Dunston, and the restless feeling took possession of her again. That Julia and. Edward would make another attempt on Geoffrey's life she felt certain, and yet witnt would they do? What could they do? Once or twice she fancied Julia looked at her with distrust. Did she suspect that she had overheard her conversation with Edward? Did she guess how it was that she and not Geoffrey came to fall through the trap-door in Illuebeard's room?</p>
        <p>o doubt she did, Olga thought, and for that reason later on she went up to her and Edward when they *ere standing together. " Ethel said," she began, slowly; " that you knew that there was some mystery about the room with the shaft in. I wish I had heard you talking about it, but I was out of the room at the time. If I had beard you speaking perhaps I should not have gone in to look."</p>
        <p>Edward's face had grown white. Julia looked. at her with hard, suspicious eyes.. . .</p>
        <p>"What made you go hi?" she asked, sharply. Olga turned to her, controlling her features and keeping calm in spite of the wave of heat that went over her. She was acting a part for Geoffrey's sake, pretending a lie for him to lay Julia's suspicions in the hope that she might watch and be prepared in case they should make an attempt of the sort again. .. "I saw the door open," she said, coolly, "and wondered where -it led to."</p>
        <p>Julia drew a deep breath, and Olga little knew how nearly she had slipped. If instead of saying the door was open she had said she had tried it to see, Julia would have known at once that she had heard something to make her suspect. "I had never seen the room before," Olga added, "and I was curious. I wish I had not been so curious." She smiled a little. and Julia seemed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>"There was poison it in</head>
        <p>"There was poison it in</p>
        <p>satishect "Does anyone know how the room came to be open? "" she asked.. __ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Olga shook' her head. "Geoffrey asked all the servants next day, and no one knew anything about it, and he *thinks that one of them must have unlocked it, and was per- haps startled by my appearance in the corridor and ran away, forgetting to shut it again. Anyhow, that seems the only ex- planation." - " Yes," echoed Julia, •" that seems the only explanation."_</p>
        <p>It was not Olga's imagination that she gave Edward a quick look and drew a breath of relief.</p>
        <p>She turned away. At least they did not suspect her now, and perhaps if they meant another " accident " she might see and save again the life of the man she loved.</p>
        <p>-But apparently Julia and Edward were in- nocent of all such intention. Once Olga stole up behind them only to hear them talking .commonplaces. Whatever they might do in future, surely they did not mean to do any- thing to-night? She was almost satisfied, almost at ease when dinner-time arrived.. They were all in the drawing-room, and as a clock struck Geoffrey looked at his watch. „</p>
        <p>"Shall we wait for Ethel?" he asked. "Has anyone seen her? Has she got back yet, mother?."</p>
        <p>- Mrs. Verriker shook her head. Ethel had come quietly, almost as if she was afraid, but the butler who came to announce dinner stepped up to Mrs. Verriker as she did so. "Miss Verriker is here," he said, "and she says will you please not wait. She will not be long." But the soup had gone, and the fish was on the point of going, too, when Ethel came in at last. She looked white and almost frightened as she went up and kissed her mother.</p>
        <p>"Where is Sterry? " Geoffrey asked. "Why don't you bring him in to dinner?" Ethel looked in his direction, but her eyes travelled above his head. "lie was kept in town," she said, "and I came back alone. He—he will come in if he is not too late, I think."</p>
        <p>She greeted Julia and Mrs. Dunston, and then stumbled into her seat, with a bare nod at Olga. Geoffrey frowned a little. "You look tired—and cross," he said. " Was it hot in town? " "Yes, very," said Ethel. She was con- scious only of one figure and one face in the whole of that great room. She Saw nothing and no one else—only Olga, Olga almost opposite, with her white face and great dark eyes that seemed to grow darker and more strained as they watched her; Olga, with her quivering lips and trembling hands; Olga, white, and faint, and ill, who knew as surely as though someone had shouted it aloud the suspicion that was in Ethel's mind.</p>
        <p>She had found out had heard something. And she had known she Would.</p>
        <p>From the very moment when they had told her that Ethel and Dick Sterry had gone to town she was afraid—afraid of she knew not what, for was it likely that they would traco hex? Was it likely, even if they did, that they</p>
        <p>would get at the secret of the Cireatbrulge murder of six -years ago? , But suppose they did—suppose they did? Her hand grew cold and her fork rattled to her plate as she saw Ethers .face. It looked as white as her own, as desperate, and Olga saw clearly enough that she was only playing with her food.</p>
        <p>She was scarcely attempting to eat her own, but after Ethel's appearance it grew tasteless altogether. When dessert came she pushed her plate away with relief and looked round wildly. They would not sit there much longer now. In a few .moments Mrs. Verriker would give the signal, and they would leave Geoffrey and Edward together. Anything was better than sitting still, oven the walk to the drawing-room, which would briug the end so much nearer—which would bring her almost to her last hour.</p>
        <p>She had gone on drifting, . letting herself go, shutting her eyes to what might or would happen until now. She had refused to -face the end—refused to see what her course would be, hoping madly, hoping against hope. as drowning men and gamblers do, that something would occur to avert the end, that something would come—death, perhaps, and even that would be welcome in place of the steps which she might be forced to take. But nothing had happened except a grittdual tightening of the net about her. Nothing had happened except the approach of what .she had refused to see. She was a -murderess ! Excuse there had been in plenty. People might pity her, they might say that Stephen Henderson had deserved his death a thousan( times, but that would make no difference. She had killed him. Perhaps, as Dr. Daere had hinted, a jury would tall it manslaughter and find it justified—perhaps, au, perhaps! But it would make no difference.</p>
        <p>Through that wild and desperate act six years ago she brought upon herself a punish- ment more bitter than she could bear. She hesitated as she left the robin ane• looked into Geoffrey's face. "Don't stay," she whiznered as he held open the door. " Don't silly with him." _ _</p>
        <p>He gave her a look and a smile that nearly broke her heart.</p>
        <p>She found herself in a low seat in the drawing-room without knowing how she , got there. The night was warm, but she war shivering. Somehow she knew that there would be no other night for her after this. somehow she knew! _ _</p>
        <p>_ She looked round presently wh.m she casild steady herself, searching the dimly-lighted room for the familiar figures. She sat almost directly behind Julia, who was slimly upright in her chair in an attitude which reminded Olga, with a cold thrill, of her attitude 6 night or two ago, when she and Edward had Waited. for their opportunity of killing Geoffrey. She sat up, too, a feeling of excitement upon •her, with a sudden intuition that some- thing unexpected would happen to-night. Some minutes elapsed. Mrs. Verriker looked across the room at Ethel, who wa. lying in her 'chair as though she was tired out: _ _</p>
        <p>1-‘-tirA VOU nQ tiroil no all „flint. child?" she</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 18, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 18, 1909.</p>
        <p>smile, but Geoffrey saw only in her face a look which startled him, a look that went to his heart.</p>
        <p>She held out her hand. She fancied that there was a sudden awful silence in the room. Then instantly she became aware that Mrs. Verriker was saying something in her ordinary voice, and that Ethel, too, was speaking. But she did not hezr what she said, she did not know even that it was to her that Ethel spoke. She looked into Geoffrey's. face —looked desperately, . hungrily, wildly—for the last time.</p>
        <p>"I think I'll have some coffee, after all," she said in a whisper.- "Will you give me that? "</p>
        <p>She fancied that there was a sudden, startled movement in . the' •direet;on of the 'table, and with strange haste she snatched the cup from Geoffrey, and, lifting it to - her lips, drank off the hot coffee at a draught. Then she flung the. cup to the floor. There was a cry from Mrs. Verriker, sudflen start from everyone in . the 'room. Julia, in the act of draining her own cup, put it down sharply, but Olga was holding out her hands to Geoffrey,- looking still into his face.</p>
        <p>" Geoffrey !" he cried. in a oncer, wean- ing voice. " Oh, Geoffrev, will you hold me? "</p>
        <p>His arms were round.. her instantly. " What is the matter, darling? " he cried. " Are you ill? Are you faint? What is it? "</p>
        <p>She made no reply for a moment. • She was waiting. • Ethel and Mrs. Verrihor had sprung to their feet. Edward , had come forward a few steps ; Julia, with a white, strained, frightened face, sat still at her coffee-table. Olga looked- round slowly until her eyc3 met hers. Geoffrey was bending to -look in her face, holding het still in strong arn,s. "I have saved your life, Geoffrey," she whispered. "Twice I have saved your life, hut I have done it now for ,the last time," She put ,ont her finger and pointed it at Julia, who had fallen back and sat staring- at her dazedly.</p>
        <p>" have drunk your ea:cc, and there was poison in it! I saw her put it in—l saw her!"</p>
        <p>" Poison !" cried Geoffrey, wildly. rthel and Mrs. Verriker stared dumbly with while faces. 'Poison! Olga darling, you are dreaming—imagining—" " Nono," she wrenched herself free, and stood facing him with flashing eyes and twitching lips. "No—no, it is no imagina- tion," she gasped breathlessly. "I saw. I know. I—l heard them plotting your death —I heard them some days ago—l don't -know how long it was, but it was the night I foll down the shaft in the -Blpebeard's room. It was done on purpose. It was Edward who un- locked the - door. lie—he went in to get things ready—to unbolt the trap-door—ready for you to fall into it. I went instead—l went to undo - his work, and I fell: To-night—to- night they meant to poison you, and they have poisoned me instead." "Is this true?" Geoffrey cried, hoarsely. "For heaven's sake is this true?"</p>
        <p>No one made reply. Olga stood rigid. "If it is, for heaven's sake ring, mothei., and send for a doctor at once."</p>
        <p>But Mrs. Verriker was already at the bell. "No—no." Olga started forward. "No—no, don't try to save me—don't let the doctor come. Let me die."</p>
        <p>"Let you die ! " cried Geoffrey, aghast, motioning to his mother to ring again. "My darling, my poor darling, I'd rather shoot myself."</p>
        <p>He had her in his arms again and was look- ing uneasily into her face. Ethel and Mrs. Verriker came forward anxiously. Only Mrs. Dunston and Edward stood apart. Julia had not moved from her chair. She sat rigid, with dull, unseeing, unbelieving eyes, with her hand to her throat, with her lips moving without uttering any sound. Edward roused himself with an effort. "It Is uot true," he said, in a hoarse, Unsteady voice. "There was no poison." - He stopped, checked by the opening of the door. The doctor—fetch the doctor quickly ! " Geoffrey cried to the butler. Don't waste a moment. It is urgent—a question of life and death, tell him." The man disappeared. Olga had sat down. "I don't know how long it will be .before the poison acts," she said, "hut there is some- thing I must say before I die. Geoffrey, I must tell: you the story of my. must confess. Oh, try not to think badly of mo. Whatever I have been, whatever I have done, I have loved you. Oh, T have loved you better than my life. Geoffrey, have pity on me." Geoffrey knelt a' her side. Mrs. Verriker was outside the door giving orders for water, sal volatile, brandy-and-milk—all the, anti- dotes to poisons that she could think ot Etiiel was leaning forward, with sudden pity on her face, and with her hands stretched out to the girl whom she had that day discovered to be Stephen Henderson's wife! The pain on Olga's face had dissolved the last doubt and distrust, and she remembered only now Chat Mrs. Cathcart and poor old feeble Mrs. Crawford had loved her—and women do not love bad women. She, too, knelt at her side.</p>
        <p>Geoffrey's strong hands held Olga's as they lay in her lap. His grey face, rigid with horror, and fear, and anxiety, was watching hers.</p>
        <p>--7'44-'orgive, my darling? There is nothing I wduld not forgive you. Why, my little girl— my little girl, you .can't realise how much you are to me." _ _ _</p>
        <p>A sob choked him. There was a moment's silence. Mrs. -Verriker bustled back with a housemaid behind her carrying the thing S she had ordered, and ,Ethel rose suddenly to her feet to help. Only Mrs. Dunston and Edward stood silent and helpless, and Julia had not moved in her chair. Hei eyes, wide set and glassy, stared at Olga 's face. Her lips still moved, and Edward, standing close beside her, fancied he heard her mutter over and over again: " Failed ! Failed! Failed!" But he was too unnerved, too horribly fascinated by this terrible development to look round. And Mrs. Dunston, too, grey of face, daunted, appalled, could only b: t dumbly watching. Olga's eyes were on Geoffrey's when Ethel held a glass to her lips.• "What can we do, Geoffrey? We do not know what poison it is? '! "Ask them," Geoffrey cried, hoarsely. "Ask Edward—Miss Dunston, and, 'oh! for heaven's sake give her something quickly." Ethel forced the glass to her lips and Olga, twisting her head, thrust it ,away. " No—no," she cried. "let me die! It will be better for me to die. Oh, if I live I shell harm you all. Geoffrey, I shall only be a curse to you if I live." She stopped, frightened by the pain in his eyes, and she suddenly put her hands on his shoulders and looked down at him.</p>
        <p>"Geoffrey, let me tell you everything while I can, while I have time," she said. He moved his lips, but for a moment no sound came from them. "Tell me what?" he asked, hoarsely. Before she had time to speak there was a knock at the door, and in the sudden silence that followed everyone heard the housemaid's voice.</p>
        <p>' "A gentleman downstairs to see Min Murray," she said. "Mr. Sam Perry." Olga started up. "Let him come—let him come," she cried, wildly. (To be continued.) •</p>
        <p>• COMMERCIAL FAlLVRES.—According to "Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday,Septenaber 10th, was 117, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 67 ; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 50. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 63; Deeds of Arrange- ment, 57—Total 120; being a decreaseof 3. Tile total number of Commercial Failures recorded during the 37 weeks of the present year is 5,529 ; the total number recorded in the corresponding 37 weeks of last year was 5,872, showing a decrease of 313.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>BLACKBERRIES AS A FARMER'S CROP.</head>
        <p>BLACKBERRIES AS A FARMER'S CROP.</p>
        <p>An auxiliary crop that is very valuable at this season is the common bramble of the hedgerow, and as it, costs nothing to grow it is one that the farmer never loses upon, no matter how adverse the season. It is, says "Farm and Home," a hardy. native plant, and r, s the fruit always sells readily it is well worth the trouble of gathering and marketing where at all thick upon the ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>k The Braganza Wedding,—Although • . the Austrian Court is one of tho most oxcltmive in Europe, it freely opens its doors to brides who are not of royal birth. When the Countess Sophie Chotek married the Arch- duke Francis Ferdinand, nephew and heir of the Austrian Emperor, not only did Francis Joseph give his consent to the union, hut conferred on the bride the title of Princess of Hobenberg. Ho has also extended a similar recognition to Miss Anita Stewart, the American heiress, who was this week married at Dingwall, in Rosshire, to Prince Mignel of Braganza. The Prince is the son of Dom Mignel, the Portuguese Pretender' a nephew of the late Empress Elizabeth of Austria. Previous to the wedding at Dingwall this week, the Emperor Francis Joseph had made Miss Stewart a Princess in her own right, so that she will stand on a level with her husbands' relatives at the Austrian Court.</p>
        <p>Another Peerage Claim.—The claim to the peerage of Lord Macdonald, otherwise known as the Lord of the Isles," which is shortly to come before the Scottish Courts, appears to depend for its success or otherwise upon a very simple issue. In the early part of last- century, the third Lord Macdonald made a Gretna Green marriage, and had two children, a son and daughter. Mien wards Lady Macthinald had scruples concerning her runaway marriage, and sbo and Lord Macdonald were re-married in a church in England. Subsequently,m, second son was born, and for some or other reason it seems to have been supposed that the Gretna Green marriage was nullified by the second marriage, and as a consequence the first' son was not born in wedlock. Ho then took the name of Bosville, and settled in Yorkshire, whilst the second son, Godfrey, alto was born in 1809, succeeded to the title and estates in Sk3e. The present Lord Macdonald is a son of this Godfrey, whilst the claimant—Mr. Alf-xander Bosville—of Thorpe Hall, Bridlington, Yorkshire—is the grandson of the disinherited son mho settled in Yolkshire. He contends that Lord Macdonald's marriage did not invalidate the previous one, and that his grandfather was therefore the eldest legitimate eon of the third Lord Macdonald. • All that seems in ques- tion is consequently whether the Gretna Green marriage mai legal, and whether it was rendered null by Lord Macdonald's re-rtarciage in church.</p>
        <p>The " Lord of the hlcs.—The prercnt Lord Macdonald, married iss Louisa Boss—daughter of the late Colonel Ross, of Crotnarty, and has a family of three sons and one daughter. One of his sisters is Lilian Lady Cromortie, mother of the Countess of Cron artie—who remarried and became the wife of Mr. Reginald Cazenove. The still youthful Countess of Crornartie who married Major Blunt in 1899, is a pecress in her own right, and one of the largest women landowners in the three kingdoms.</p>
        <p>The Motor-Car Danger.—The notice "Please Drive Slowly," now exhibited in many villages, and will, it is to be hoped, do something to check the reckless driving of mot ot-cars, as to which there are many com- plaints from dwellers in roadside houses. The Road Unions' petition to the Queen on the subject of dangerous motor traffic, is being signed by thew-ands of village women, who say "Our children are always in danger, our things sre ruined by the dust., We cannot open our windows, and our rest is spoiled by the noise all night." Everyone knows and recognises that the grievance of these people is a very re at one, and although we fail to see what the Queen esn do, possibly this appeal to her Majesty may induce some offending motorists to pay a little more respect to the rights of the users of the highway. A Patron Saint for Avistors.—Like the motorists, who have adopted St.- Christopher, the aviators are looking round for a suitable patron se,int and protector. Whether ot not safety lies in that direction, it is abun- dantly clear that flying is a very risky game, 'rind it seems that the Life Insurance Companies will not pay on policies to the families of those who lose their lives at it. Some of the Accident Insurance Companies are offering to cover the risk of ballooning and flying at special rates. One, however, notices that in all the s, whether relating to fire, life, burgulary, &amp;c, there is a long slring of clauses in minute print, which collectively sr em to go to show that the insured could not in any circumstances recover a claim, and in the aviator claim of one policy it is stated : The insurance commences in the person entering the ballon basket or car, and wil cease as soon as the insured has loft the basket or car." That condition recalls she story of the lady who remarked that she was "not in it" as she fell out of the balloon, and in her case this' policy would have become inoperative just when it might be sup- posed to have become useful. The Ride-Astride llabit.—The ride-astride habit is not a- graceful costume for horsewomen, but it is i coming to ho worn more generally by ladies n the field, and makes its first appearance en the stage in "The Whip," the new Drury-lane drama. It is worn by Miss Bateman in the first, and also in the hunt breakfast scene, together with a silk hat, with a very wide brim and deep band. Fashions, no doubt, are mostly dictated by self-interested fashionable shopkeepers, but the, stage is not without's large influence in this direction' and in this instance it is likely to increase the vogue of the ride-astride habit among horsewomen. Tight Lacing.—Women and girls who torture them- selves in divers ways in order to obtain a sixteen or resenteen inch waist, will note with surprise, and no doubt great disdain and disgust, that a twenty-six inch waist is really correct and healthy for a woman of average size. This information comes from a well- known authority on the subject, and without doubt if we want to enjoy good health, and forget we have such things as nerves, we should avoid tight-lacing as we Would the plague. Moreover, so rutrz,h can be done now-a-days by corset-makers, to apparently reduce the size of the waist, without undue pressure anywhere, and we have so many styles available that help the " chubby " on towards gracefulness, that there is no possible excuse for the girl or woman who errs in the matter of tight lacing. -</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Matierials.—Plain cloths, which the tailors Lave "boomed" so long, am not to reign supremo this coming winter. Itich silks will be well to the fore, in tailor-land, also a new material which really is velvet, yet looks like crepon, and is As soft and supple as the petals of a rose. Another addition to the list of tailor fabrics, which bears the somewhat peculiar name of " !Aline," is thickly corded, with a dull and somewhat lustreless surface like poplin. In serges, the variety closely resembling the "oatmeal cloth" of by.oune days, is claiming considerable atten- tion, and will 'be much employed for coat and skirt costumes, generall classed as "useful." Striped velvets in two distinct colours, Such as green and grey, and brown and purple, are being much worn by, smart Parisiennes, but so far our tailors have shown a decided preferencs for the plain varieties of velvet which they deem in better taste.</p>
        <p>New Belts.—Any girl or woman who crochet can quickly and inexpensively make hessolf a fashionable belt, Coarse twine, and a pair of very neat clasps, are all that is necessary to attain this end, the twine being simply crocheted to the desired width and length, and sown to the clasps. These twine belts look particu- larly well with ti earl and serge gowns, and keep in condition very much longer than those built of leather. Berlin Wool Work.—A decided novelty at the milliners is the velvet hat; boasting a" puffed crown, with a four inch band about it, worked of white giant Berlin wool, two huge wings, fashioned in a similar way, standing out at one side. The effect is excellent though no doubt there is nothing very lasting about it. The wool bands and *ings will very quickly get out of order, and soil, still it ought not to be very difficult task to copy them, exactly, and renew them from time to time.</p>
        <p>MADGE'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>I saw Ler pat it in 1"</head>
        <p>I saw Ler pat it in 1"</p>
        <p>asked. "'Well, I suppose it was a long nay, and you will tell me all about everything to- morrow. But you are generally such a chatterbox." _ . . . _ _</p>
        <p>Ethel passed her hand across her face. :" I think the crowds gave me a headache," she said. "London seernea horribly noisy."</p>
        <p>At that moment the butler stepped • across to Mrs. Verriker, and placed a silver coffee- tray on a table before her. s</p>
        <p>'Sir Geoffrey says that they will come tip nere for coffee, if they may, m'rn." Mrs. Verriker put down her work. "That will be nice," she said. "But we won't wait. Miss Dunston, you are closest to me. Will you pour out the coffee to-night as Ethel is so tired?." _</p>
        <p>_ The butler instantly • removed the table, and again the feeling of cold ran through Olga as the doer opened and Edward went straight towards 'Julia - and stood before her ready ,to take the cups.</p>
        <p>Olga watched her like one fascinated. She saw Julia's white hands move about the Silver and the delicate china cups with a breathless feeling of anxiety. Would any- thing happen? Or was she living in a night- mare of unrealities? Had - she really over- heard anything, or had she imagined it? Had her fall through .the shaft really ben only an accident, after all? Such things surely only happened in books, and she tried to shake the feeling from her. But it clung in spite of her- self, and her eyes never moved from Julia's stiff, unchanging back. _ _</p>
        <p>Only for nioment, when Edward brought her coffee, did she turn away, and then she shook her head and looked back immediately, "I won't have coffee to-night, thank you," she said.</p>
        <p>Edward took away fife cup, and Olga noticed suddenly that instead of offering it to Geoffrey he replaced it on the table. "1,11 have mine black, please, Miss Dunston," said Geoffrey, "and two lumps," He had stopped to speak to Ethel, who. was sitting a little more upright. But Olga did not turn her head, and then it was that she saw something which made her heart stand still.</p>
        <p>Edward was bonding over the tray, his back to the room, to Geoffrey and Ethel, to Mr • Verriker and Mrs. Dunston, and then it was that Olga's quick eyes- saw something drop from his fingers to the tray: Inure_ diately Julia overturned a cup, and Olf.ta fancied, rather than saw, the small white hands fumble in the tray and then linger for a moment, poised above the cup. She got up abruptly, and Julia turned with a start and a little cry. She saw both of them looking at her, and Edward swiftly took up a cup. " How you startled me," said Julia. "I didn't know you were behind in that corner." "I am sorry," said Olga. She passed Julia and the tray, and went , towards Geoffrey. Edo --d turned, too,and handed him the cup of C.Ol lee.</p>
        <p>Olga saw Geoffrey take it in his hands. Her heart was stifling her. Her eyes, fixed hungrily on Geoffrey's face, saw nothing else —saw only that one face, as she would see it always while life lasted. She was trying to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SPARROWS IN GARDENS.</head>
        <p>SPARROWS IN GARDENS.</p>
        <p>Taken altogether, the sparrow is the most abused of all our native birds, yet in many parts of London it is practically the only member of the feathered tribe to be met with, and its note, though not musical, has a certain amount of cheerfulness about it. The sparrow hss also another redeeming feature, and one that has both interested and gratified me of late. ln common with 'rang others situated within the London area, my Rose-bushes were badly attacked by aphides. Before I hnd times to syringe them, I noticed that the bushes were being overhauled by a number of sparrows, who, setting to work in a systematic manner, cleared off all the insect pests from the young shoots, without injuring even the most succulent of them. This desirable trait of the sparrow is certainly worthy of record, for as a rule nothing but abuse is bestomed upon it, though I have seen its aphis-destroying propensities recently mentioned in a gardening contemporary.—" Gardening Illustrated."</p>
      </div>
