      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Croft.</head>
        <p>Croft.</p>
        <p>SCARLET FEVER.—Owing to an outbreak of scarlet fever in the village the Medical Officer (Dr. Sephtcn) s decided to close the Council schools. At present 6re are eight known cases, and as they are of a very aught nature it is anticipated the schools will be "'opened in the course of a fortnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Burtonwoed.</head>
        <p>Burtonwoed.</p>
        <p>nsth LANG LTAGE.—At the Warrington Petty Sessicns °ll Wednesday, James Harratty of Burtonwood was largei w:th using indecent language on the highway 11 the 2Sth last month.—P.C. McCormick stated that " was on duty in Chapel-lane and saw defendant who "s shouting and swelling ar.d his language was dis- "sting. He was fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Orford.</head>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>A SCHOOL BOY'S FUNERAL.— Touching scenes witnessed at the funeral on Sunday of Philip ileY, the fifteen-year-old son of Mr. John Brailey, 9f Orford Cottages, who died after a very short illness lu the Warrington Infirmary where he had undergone an ul?eration for peritonitis. Deceased was very popular Ivith his old school fellows, six of whom acted as bearers at the interment on Sunday afternoon. A service, con- ducted by the Vicar, was held in Orford Church, from Zbeace the cortege preceded to the Cemetery, followed Y the Sunday school children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>IfILL CLIFFE _BAPTIST CHURCH.—There were large toaeregations at this Churela on Sunday when the autumnal sermons were preached by the Rev. A. Spurgeon 11°11hagshead, of Bradford. The rev, gentleman also gave an address at the Children's Service held in the °flaing. Special h3'lnns and anthems were rendered tithe choir in a very creditable manner. The collec- r°as wheel, were in aid of the chureh funds amounted o gi,..N,EGLECT OF CHILDREN.—At the above Court, John a labourer, late of Stockton Heath, nas -"altnoned for neglecting his children in such a manner ,11 to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to rilth.—Mr. Browne, who appeared on behalf ot the 40-.P.C.C:, said that since an adjournment of the case, V endant s conduct had, he was pleased to say, 'LaProved, and he accordingly asked that the charge he Withdraw n.—The case was withdraen. 110 LIGHT.—KNOCKED A POLICEMAN DOWN.—At the a Court, Frank Brown, Arley Hall Garden, was fined Qr riding a bicycle without a lighted lamp attached. icting•Sergehnt Simms said be was on duty in Zundon•road when he saw the defendant coning down e brow from the Bridge on his bicycle without his ;`I43P lighted. Witness shouted to him to stop, but endant dashed into him and caught him with his `4,4Pclle tar, and they both crashed to the ground. I,:itness' hand was cut severelv,and had to be attended to Y •Dr Parks r, who had sent a bill in for lls.—Defend- a.„.. was ordered to ray the doctor's bill and 2s. 6d. costs, --aaing 13s. ad. in all. u. FOOTPATH OBSTRUCTION. A MAGISTRATE'S •;:4RNINO.—At the Daresbury Petty Sessions on esday, James Dsniels, Charles Kirkland, John books, and Thomas Bostock, youths living in Stockton Wreath were charged with having obstructed the free use etktbe pavement of London-road on Sunday, September e.—Acting Sergeant Simms said all the defendants a ale standing on the footpath near St. Thomas's Church I,llPeaple had to get off the pavement to pass.— at e!Yr all had pea blowers and were shooting " haigs " tte tram drivers and conductors as they passed on 4;4e trams.—The Chairman (Mr.Berder) : We quite agree :let this conduct must be stopped, but we will give you, other chance, as we don't want to fine you, as your Poret nts will have to pay. If you come again before the 1%111 we,will request your parents to give you a thrash- az, which yon deserve.) ,k-r\EPENDENT METHODIST CHURCIL—A bazaar in entection with the Independent Methodist Church was dltlened in Mr. Mason's warehouse, Fearnhead, on Thurs. b,Y by Mr. J. Dodds, of Penketh.—The object of the „faar is to raise £250 to enable the congregation to Frataence the building of a new church and school at tearchead, Mr. Edward Gandy, of Fearnhead, having ttrously presented them with a plot of land. A very earge number of people were present at the opening th:etnolaY. Mr. William Bennett occupied the chair in to° !absence of Dr. Bennett, who was hindered from 11:11'ng by important business engsgements.—Mr. Wm. nuett read a letter from the Doctor apologising for absence and enclosing a cheque for £2 2s.—The airman said he was sorry the Doctor was not present :at be would endeavour to be a good substitute. He a very pleased to he among the Risley people who verked very hard for their cause, and he hoped they Ould be very auccessful in their endeavour at that r.z.ar. Mr. •J. Dodds and he were very old ifteuds and he had great pleasure in introduc- v4.ll Arr. J. Dodds to them.—Mr. J. Dodds said he was Or pleased to be with them. He understood Risley aZt, been associated with Methodism for over 100 years, te be admired the pioneers of the cause very much. a Ugh Bourne and Peter Philips were men of sterling 111,114 enthusiastic character, men worthy of resemblance, bB was very pleased to be associated with a cause Web had such auspicious- pioneers. Some of his laloends said he should not support bazaars,but when he eat.l!ed round and saw the labour and energy keetbited in the bazaar, it made him feel that ed„ could not help but be in sympathy (4,,.a them, and he wishen them every success. 4151ause).—Mrs. Dodds said Mr. W. Bennett bad been Ile:chine the Risley people'in their entiring efforts and eh' \lle very pleased to be there that day. From what t: had heard -Risley had established itself and she hoped Pp. 3Qe them very soon in their new church at errahead.—'Mr. W. Hughes proposed a vote " thanks in which he expressed the welcome teeistile church to the different ladies and gentle- lea', Present for their kind help and support.—This TO seconded by Mr. C. H. Harrison and carried. efollowing is a list of the stallholders :—Drapery 4,11 : 'yrs. C. H. Harrison, Mrs. J. Bate Mrs. R.Bold, 'l` A. A• Wcrral/. Drapery Stall: Mrs. .I.Higham,Mrs. Vti• Worrell Mrs. P. Worrell, Mrs. J. Dilliway, St-asll M. 'Smith, Miss E. Flatman. Farmers' !' . Mrs Worrell, Mrs. William Hughes, Mr. A-orrall. Mr. A. Worrell, Mr. P, Worrell. 4t.oeery stall : Miss A. Smith, Miss M. E. Ashton, 4atalisis R. Stringer Mrs. Peter Lythgoe. Refreshment Mrs. Houghton, Mrs. England, Mrs. Jackson, a.ra• T. Hughes, Mrs. Dumbell, Mrs. Mom, Mrs. ,111. llonks, Mrs. P. Stringer Mrs. W. J. Bate, tit 43 B. Bate Miss E. Z. Bate, Miss B. A. el-e, is Ida Bate, Mrs. James Bate, Mrs.J. Mte, Mrs. lar."`agbes, Mrs. Wm. Hughes. Rummage stall : Mrs. J. v°rrall, Mr. W. J. Bate, Mr. J. Worrell, Mr. J. Barton, I.lllllag Men's Stall: Mr. R. Bold. Mr. J. Quaine, Mr. T. ila,:'ll3fison, Mr. J. Higham, Mr. Fred Hughes. Ice 'aal Stall : Master J. Houghton, A. Jackson, Fred te Shooting Range: Master W. Jackscn, A. it 14'1:311, Jonathan Bate. Weighing Machine: Master S.CAlRbatn. Cloak Room : Mrs. S. .Bate, Mrs. P. Rer. Bran Tub: Miss A. Houghton, Miss E. Mr. William Hughes is the President of the Voaaittee and Mr. C. H. Harrison the committee. kaetkbazaar was re-opened yesterday by Mr. George " r Fearnhead, and it will be continued to-day. IP</p>
