      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>:,:-.f.:' ' , AA. _ . _</head>
        <p>:,:-.f.:' ' , AA. _ . _</p>
        <p>Grappenhall</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OF CHICKENS: A REMARKABLE CASE.—At the Daresbury Sessions on Tuesday. Peter Hodgkinson, a turner, formerly of Widnes, was charged with stealing between the 30th and 31st of July at: Grappenhall, five chickens of the value of 10s., the pro- perty of Emma Watson, of Cedar Terrace, Latchford Without.-3lr. A. Browne, who represented defendant, pleaded not guilty.—Mrs. Watson said she counted her chickens before the robbery, and on discovering it gave information to the police. That. Was on the</p>
        <p>6th August. She saw them at Mr. F. Dutton's house at •Thelwall and picked them out of about 300 others.—By Mr. Browne: She had not paid 2s. 6d. for some chickens which had been stolen from another farm, nor had she paid 2s. 6d. towards her share of what her son and other boys had stolen. The chickens were then examined by witness and she was confident they were hers. -She could tell them by their colour and marks.—Frederick Dutton of West-lane, Thelwall, said that on Saturday, July 31st, defendant went to his house and brought five chickens. Witness brought them from him. Defendant said he had made an exchange for a babies cot with Mrs. Watson. Witness was present when Mrs. Watson went to his house to inquire about the chickens. Witness had known defendant for a number of years.—P.C. Wood stated that he arrested defendant and charged him with the offence, to which he made no reply!-In answer to Mr. BrOwne, witness said that Mrs. Murrey had said that no more would be heard of the robbery it 2s. 6d. each was paid by the parents of the boys who committed the robbery.— Mr. Browne in his speech for the defence, said that all the bother arose out .of some boys. The defendant's boy, Watson's boy, and others went for a walk for some chick weed, and on seeing the chickens on the rCadside they stole them and took them to the defendants premises where they were left for a while a cote having been purposely erected.—On defendant's return home at night, he remonstrated with his son for stealing the chickens and asked him to put them out. The boy did not do .so and defendant to save his bay from getting into trouble took the chickens to Mr. Dutton, and sold them, which was a silly and foolish act to do. This act was the quintessence of idiocy for which one could hardly believe a sane man •to be responsible. Norman Hodgkinson son of the defendant gave evidence as to his stealing the fowlea.—Defendant gave evidence and said his object of selling the chickens was to screen his lad. He was fined 40s. and cost and the chickens were handed over to Mr. Watsons. ' OPEN-AIR BUDGET MEETING.</p>
        <p>An open-air meeting in favour of the • Budget was held at Grappenhall on Saturday evening. There was a good attendance. Mr. Robert Lewis, of Manchester, was the principal speaker, and Mr. C. Jockson Holmes the chairman. The Chairman said that once more we were in the midst of the most exciting times. The papers were full, just now, of far-reaching events—the Budget and the Polar expedition. That meeting was arranged to "give correct information of the mest powerful Liberal Government that had ever been known in our time. (Hear, hear.) Of course, they were subject to opposi- tion of all kinds—straightforward opposition and that of the noble lords and graceful dukes %Ito were not able to continue their subscriptions—small paltry sub- scriptions—of one guinea to fcotball clubs and other recreation societies. (Applause.) He then moved a resolution in favour of the Budget. Mr. Lewis who spoke at length and raised consider- able enthusiasm said that of all the measures and suggestions made during the term of office of the present Government none had commended so much support and none had been demonstrated to be fairer than the Budget. The Government were engaged in a very sericus task and the couLtry would before very long—it seemed to him—be called upon to express their opinion on a question of the greatest. possible importance. We were actualty threatened with a revolution—not of our making. It was a revolution proposed by the landlord classes of this country in order that they might defeat the proposals of the Government for meeting the expenses of the current year. Nothing had commended the Government—under the consideration of fair minded people—more than the fact that they were not at all desirous of levying leans to pay national expenditure. They were dasirous of meeting the annual cost of maintenance of the State out of the taxes to be levied during the year upon the general population of the country. They had been asked, and it had been suggested that instead of raising the sum of 13 millions by the impasition of additional taxation that they might get over the difficulty of providing for national defence and old age pensions by a loan that the people of this country would have to pay sooner or later. The Government in their-judgment had said that they did net consider that was honest finance and that they ought to pay for such things out of the revenue. Con- tinuing he said that we had had the dukes declaring that it WAS utterly impossible for them to meet the claims that would be made upon them by the Budget. On the other hand we had the marquises, the earls and the barons who had also declared that the burden of taxation by this Budget was so great that it would be impossible for them to meet it. We had on Friday the marvellous spectacle of a past Liberal Premier—(hear, hear)—making a speech in Glasgow in which he con- demned the Budget hip and thigh. (Voice: "For selfishness.") He (the speaker) had every respect for Lord Rcsehery. A man who had occupied the position of Prime Minister of England was worthy of enybody's respect, yet his criticism of the Budget might have easily been given by a Conservative member at a Budget Protest League meeting ; for from beginning to end there was no real argument against it. (Hear, hear.) It was simple denunciation in his ow a charming and delightful phraseology. He said it was socialistic, as though nuking use of that term were all that was necessary. He said that it was "revolutionary." Then he said it would create unemployment and that the arm of righteousness would certainly came down upon the community if it became law. What were the proposals that called forth such strenuous denunciation upon the party of that good man? Coming to the land taxes and the Budget, the speaker said it was a lamentable thing for Lord Rosebery at last to take up his place among the Conservatives. Of course he had been going wrong for years. (Laughter.) We remembered his attitude on the Armenian atrocities and the Boer War. (Hear, bear.) Now when it came to a question of populer right we found bun in the same place as before —on the side of big monopolists and bondholders, on the side of the rich and not of the poor. And yet that was the man who, on the day when he became Prime Minister of England, made use of some remarkable statements with regard to the taxation of land. He said:— Besides these meetings the County Council has made some great Parlimentery exertion,. It has laid down some principles which will not be allowed to die until they are carried into effect. The first of these is the taxation of ground values. That is a principle which .is becoming universally estab- lished, because I think at the last election when the other party held their meeting in this very Hall, the judgment of that meeting acknowledged that that was a sound and just principle. It is not a very easy principle to carry simply into effect, but I have little doubt that with brains now devoted to- the opplication of it we shall soon arrive at a working result." Continuing, Mr. Lewis said that that was the very man who on Friday declared that the Government 'pro- posals so far as the increment value of the land was ' concerned were revolutionary.spoiliation and robbery, (Laughter). Concluding, Mr. Lewis said that there was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 18, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 18, 1909.</p>
        <p>a good time coming, whereas the good time and the Tariff Reformer was taxation upon the necessities of life— a tax upon the industry of the people, the necessities and upon commerce. (Applause). Mr. Will Ford, the Liberal agent for the Enutsford Division, also spoke. He said they did not fear the fight which was said to be approaching. They were well prepared and looked forward to it with the greatest pleasure—confident that they would rep Eat the victory of 1906. (Applause). The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried, only six voting against.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Parish Council was held on Tuesday evening. Present :—Mr. J. Charlton Parr (in the chair), and Messrs. Lister-Kaye; N. Greening, Bacon, Ashton, Collins, Wareing, and Charles H. Hoult (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>FOOTPATHS.</head>
        <p>FOOTPATHS.</p>
        <p>This questicn came UP- again for discussion, and Mr. Wareing stated that certain improvements in Cartridge- lane had not yet been made. The Clerk : I have written to Mr. Mclntyre, but received no answer.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins : Will this matter come before the annual meeting ? -</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Yes. The question mas adjourned until the next meeting.</p>
        <p>DUST LAYING.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a re-tier -Ir-o-in—the County Council staling that a special process for dust laying would cost 215 per mile during the season only. The Chairman : In view of Parliamentary considera- tion of this question, I would suggest that the matter lie on the table for the present. This was agreed to. SPEED LIMIT FOR MOTORS. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Ashton: I think something should be done to check the terrific speed of motors between the Dog and Dart, and the Latchford swing bridge. It is most dangerous to the public. Mr. Wareing: Could we not get police assistance ? The Chairman : I suggest that we send a resolution to the Chief Constable for the County. This was agried to, and a resolution moved by Mr. Ashton, seconded by Mr. Bacon, to the effect that Col. Hammersley's attention be drawn to the fact, and that steps be taken to reduce the speed of motors on the afore-mentioned distance of Knutsford-road.</p>
        <p>POSTAL ALTERATIONS.</p>
        <p>Tbe Clerk re;cl—COr-re-Siondence from the Postal authorities referring to the Grappenhall sub-office suggesting the following alterations :—that the time for opening he altered from 7 a.m. to 8 a.m. and that the office be closed on Fridays from 1 p.m. so 7 p.m. so as to afford a half holiday. As a consequence telegrams for Grappenhall would be delivered from Latchford, also that the office be closed on Bank blonde, from 12 o'clock noon. Telegramsxould be sent from iNarrington General. _ -</p>
        <p>This matter was carefully d scussed and considered from all points. Mr. L. Greening moved and Mr. Lister Kaye sec3nded that the Council approve of the suggested alterations. This was carried Imanimonsly. DANGER 9 US DITCH. _ _</p>
        <p>The Clerk said ditch Knutsford-road near Mr. liankeys farm had how been fenced round by the County Council.</p>
        <p>SEWERAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins a-sied-iiie—C-h-air-nTair if he was in possession of the fact that already a scheme was on foot which might, cost the ratepayers some thoussnds of pounds. He, Mr. Jenkins, read a local report, stating that a special committee had been appointed to confer with the surveyor on the spot. He also stated that an engineer had been appointed to go on with the work. All this aas done without the consent of the Council and in contravention of the law. The Chairman: I have had no official communication Mr. Jenkins.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins: ilocr did the scheme originate. The chairman: Ido not know.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Lister-Kaye: An engineer hex been appointed and already a sum of from £6O to £7O has been spent. I think we ought to be in possession of more definite information.</p>
        <p>A resolution was carried to the effect that this Council ascertain hem Mr. Ashton (clerk of the Rim- corn Rural Council) what has already ba done in the matter. And why in accordance with the act they had not been notified.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Thelwall</head>
        <p>Thelwall</p>
        <p>MOTOR WARNINGS.-At a meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday, the Secretary of the Royal Automobile Club wrote with regard to the erec- tion of motor car warning signals at a dangerous corner in the village of Tbelwall, stating that the Club restricted the use of its own signs to places of an acutely dangerous character. So far as he was informed the danger at Thelwell-road would appear sufficient to warrant danger signals, and he suggested that the ordinary redtriangular sign of the Council would be sufficient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Winwick.</head>
        <p>Winwick.</p>
        <p>FREE CHuncir.--In atd of the Trust Funds or the above church a sale of work and cake and apron sale were held on Saturday and proved a great success. The Rev. J. B. Bugloss of Warrington, in opening the pro- ceedings, referred to the difficulties which confronted Nonconformity in rural districts. At the conclusion of the sale tea was provided. In the evening an entertain- ment was held, Mr. Bridge of Park-lane presidine. The programme included songs by'M iss Lawton, of Orford, and Miss E. Smethurst of Bolton, violin and piano duets by the Misses Lawton, of Orford, end Lancashire sketches by Mrs. Barlow of Warrington.</p>
        <p>Lymm. II MISHAP AT MESSRS.E.BROWN'S &amp; EONS NEW PREMISES. —On Wednesday afternoon whilst putting the new plate glass windows in the new shop of Messrs. E. Brown's &amp; Sons, Booths Hill, can of the sindows was smashed. This will be a very serious loss to the builder or the shop fitter. LOCAL WILL—Mrs. Cordelia Ridgway (90), of Ash- field, Victoria-road, Manchester, widow of Mr. Thomas Ridgway, of Elm Lodge, Towcester, whose estate has been proved at £18,307 left £lOO for a memorial tablet in Higher-lane Baptist Chapel, Lymm, in memory of her late husband, and £2OO to her servant Charlotte Ann Garaway, together witk the income from £2,000. LYMM CHORAL SOCIETY.—This society held its first rehearsal on Tuesday evening at the Council room. The season promises to be most popular yet held. Under the conductorship of Mr. T. AL Eastham the society is sure to go, and we would recommend all those with musical talent. The public are expecting great things from the society during the Tear. The treasurer is Mr. J. D. B. Smith, Bryn-anon, Lymm, and secretary Mr. Frank Brown, Higher-lane, Lymm. GRAMMAR SCHOOL SCHOLARSHIP.S.—Tbe following cmdidates, who are placed in order of merit have been elected to free scholarships at the Grammar School under the Board of Education regulations: Walter Jones, Oughtrington School ; Lilian Burrows. Warburton School : Herten Atkinson, High Leigh School ;Herbert Roebuck, Thelwall School ; Dorothy Twiss, Oughtrington School; Ruth Willan, Lymm School, and Sydney Rouse, Lymm School.</p>
        <p>-NAGS HEAD BOWLING CLUB SUPPER AND CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The above club held their annual supper and concert on Wednesday evening last, at the Nags Head Hotel. About 40 members and friends partook of the repast laid before them in a most satisfactory way by the proprietor, Mr. David Mcßride. After sipper, they adjourned to the concert room whore songs wore con- tributed by the following gentlemen. Messrs. J. Hatton, W. H. Mosten, T. Heaven, D. Mcßride, F. Wilson, Booth, and H. Dix ; a bone duet was given by Mr. H. Dix and Mr.A. Eaton. The accompanist for the evening was Mr. Dobson of Warrington. Afterwards a vote of thanks was passed to tte singc-rs and also to the chair-. man or the evcning, Mr. John Hatton.</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO THE 3IILLINGTON ACCIDENT. LICENSING PROSECUTION.</p>
        <p>At the,Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday, a sequel to the fatal hansom cab accident on August 31st at Millington, near Altrincharn' was heard, Joseph Thomas Lea, licensee of the Boat House Inn, Agden, being summoned for permitting drunkenness on his premises. The two men in the hansom at the time of the accident —Martin Cndclihy, son of the licensee of the Jolly Thresher, Ly mrn, and Ernest Leah, groundsman of the High Legh Cricket Club—were charged with being drunk. Cuddihy and Leah hired the cab to drive them from Altrincham to Lymm, but the cabman lost his way and the cab Wt s upset in a lane. Ihe driver was hurt, but he told the two men, alter they had helped him to his feet, that he was "all right.' They then set out to walk home, but the driver was found dead the next morning near his cab. Mr. Caldecutt, who prosecuted for the police, said that Cuddihy and Leah went to Altrinctam at noon cn August 31 and had drinks at two public-houses. At seven in the evening they obtained the cab to drive them home, and witnesses alleged that the two men and the cabmen were under the influence of drink. Before going home they again asked for drink, but the landlord of the bin refused to serve any of them They then went to an inn at Bollington, but the landlord's daughter, concluding that they were drunk, ordered the cabman to drive on. They went to the Boat House, Agden, and remained there from a quarter t• nine to ten minutes to eleven. Cuddihy stood drinks to customers, Borne of whom would fay that he and Leah were under the influence of drink. The landlord twice ordered them out, but they refused. Later, at Millington, where the accident happened, the two men and the cabman were considered to bo drunk.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Cabe, for the defence, argued that the surrounding circumstances bad nothing to do with the case. No Ovid( nce was given ttat Cuddihy and Leah were served with intcxicating liquor at the Boat House. All that the witnesses could say was they bad had some drink. Evidence of people in the house was that they were not chunk hut sober. The defendant asked them to go because the horse had keen standing outside such a length of time. The defendant was fined £2 and costs, £lO fis. altogether. Cuddiby and Leah were fined Hs and costs for teirg drunk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SOLICITOR AND POLICEMAN'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>At Frodshara Sessions on Wednesday, H. N. Masker, solicitor, Runcorn, was charged with having a dt g at large after 'sunset. Police-constable Roberts said Mr. Linaker admitted the dog was his, and took it into the house. Mr. Linaker declared that this was absolute purjury. He was in bed. The constable persisted in his statement. Mr. Linaker said it was his brother Fred, who is an engineer on a Leyland liner, now on a voyage to Savannah. He applied for an adjournment sine die which was granted. SLEEPING UNDER A VITRIOL TANK.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Monday, before the Mayor. and Alderman Ovens, three "out of works," named Robert Lyons, John Stone and John Smith, were charged with sleeping out in Messrs. Golding, Davis's works. All pleaded guilty. Police-Constable Jackson, the works constable, said the manager complainei bitterly of those men sleeping out in the works, often in very dangerous situations. These men were found sleeping under a vitriol tank, and they ran great risk of being suffocated. There were also grave complaints from the workmen of having their suppers stolen.—Prisoners were sent to gaol for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>THEFT OF A.HANDCART.</head>
        <p>THEFT OF A.HANDCART.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Monday, John Meeney, alias Mu! crow, of St. Helens, was charged on a warrant with stealing a handcart, value .£2, whilst bailee, the property of Thomas Houghton, marine store dealer, of Farrant-street, Widnes. Prisoner borrowed the handcart for the 23rd of August for the purpose of rag-gathering. He did not return it, and a warrant was taken out for his arrest. The St. Helena police gave him into the custody of Sergeant Davidson on Friday. The handcart Lad been left at the Marine store in Liverpool, and had not yet been recovered. The bench sent him to gaol for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held in the Duttcus Workhouse, on Tuesday. Mr. T. Wright presided and there were also present :—Messrs. T. Wright, J. Spruce, J. Woodward, Admiral -Jones- Parry, Morris Davies, 0. Pilling, C. E. Linaker, jun., J. Bolsbaw, d. Egerton-Warburton, P. Booth, L. Bolton, E. Bretherton G. Watson, P. Collins, A. R. Norman, .1. P. Chadwick, J. Swinton (surveyor), G. F. Ashton (clerk), H. B. Hough (assistant clork).—The Clerk 'sub- mitted from Messrs. Baker, Blaker snd Haines, of London, a draft of the provisional order granted by the Board of Trade under the Electric Lighting Act 1882 and 1888 to the Cast-net Kelner Alkali Co. Ltd., in respect of the urban district of Runcorn and the town- ship and parish of Weston within the rural district of Runcorn. The schedules showed that the whole of the urban district of Runcorn and the township of Weston is the area of supply. Power is asked to break up certain roads and streets and the private tramway of the Runcorn and Helsby Red Sandstone Co. The strPets and parts of streets throughout which the undertakers are to lay down suitable and sufficient distributing mains for the purposes of general supply within the period of two years after the commencement of the order are South-road, Weston Point, part of Sandy-lane, Weston-road from Sandy-lane to Highlands-road, Greenway-road and part of Sandy-lane. The following Committee was appointed to consider the matter, the Chairman, Vice-Chairman Mr. Pilling and Mr. Antwis. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday.—Mr. T. Baxter pre- sided and were also present : Messrs. S. Wright., J. Spruce, S. Woodward, Admiral Jones-Parry, J. Hallows, W. M. Morrir, J. H. Davies, G. Pilling, C. E. Linaker (Inn.), J. Balshaw, G. Egerton-Warburton, P. Booth, L. Bolton, E. Bretherton, G. Watson, P. Collins, A. R. Norman, J. B. Chadwick,T.tooke, W. R. DePrez, and J. Pearson (relieving officers), G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk) and T. D. Robinson (master).</p>
        <p>THE NEW MASTER AND MATRON. The Clerk read a letter from the newly appointed master and matron (Mr. and Mrs. Persons, of Sheffield,) intimating that they will take up their new duties on October 11th.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Well Mr. &amp;Mason stay until then? Mr. Rollinson : Yes.</p>
        <p>NUMBER IN THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruee said that there were 161. inmates in the houses during the last R eek as compared with 170, the corresponding week last year, vagrants relieved were 68 as prepared as compared with 71.</p>
        <p>MR. LINAKER JUN., THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward with respect to the new gas plant installed in the house at the instigation of Mr. C. Linaker, jun., said it would be a lamentable thing if Mr. Linnker were not thanked fer the services he had rendered in that capacity. He had also done a great many services for the Board and also the Rural Condon and he moved that the thanks be noted on the minutes of the Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. Morris in seconding, said he was one of the obstreperous members with regard to the new plant. but he had now chgnged his opinion. The firm who had put the plant in had done that work better than he had seen any before. Mr. Spruce supported. Mr. Linaker in reply said it had given him great pleasure to do the Board a service. He bad had a great deal of experience with respect to the lighting plant, and thought it would be beneficial to them. It had, however, not been very much trouble to him, as Mr. Rollerson had taken all the work off his shoulders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MOTOR ROADS.</head>
        <p>MOTOR ROADS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Chester Rural Council wrote stating that at the last meeting of the authority a resolution was passed that the proposed expenditure out of the Government Development Grant to make motor roads would only be a waste of money which could be better spent on improving existing road,. The resolution was approved by the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>BATHING COSTUMES BARRED.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens magistrates were somewhat puzzled on Monday by the statement cf the manager of a local skating rink, who was applying for an extension of hours, that it was proposEd to hold a "seaside fancy dress" carnival.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: What, in Warrington New-road.? (Laughter). "What are seaside costumes?" asked the Mayor iu a puzzled sort of tone. The applicant said he lsupposed they included yacht- ing, motoring, golfing, and cricket costumes. "Bathing costumes ? " queried Mr. T. Jenkinson, suspiciously, amidst laughter. " No, bathing costumes are barred," was the reply. KILLED BY A STRAW.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse held the inquiry at Sutton Police Station on Monday on Samuel Owen, aged twenty-eight of 19, Eliza-street, who died on Friday morning last under remarkable circumstances.—Mrs. Owen, the widow, said her husband was in perfectly gcod health when he went to work on Friday week,when he received an injury to his nose. He worked on the Saturday, but his nose rapidly got into a very bad state.—Charles Heggett, 79, Junction-lane, said that Owen worked for his uncle, Mr, John Willis, farmer, of St. Helens Junction. On Friday, 3rd inst., Owen was thatching a stack of hay with wheat straw. He was by himself. Witness was in the washhouse at the farmhouse about three o'clock in the afternoon, when Owen came in and said he had a stab in the nose with a straw. They both afterwards went to milk the cows, but deceased said nothing more.— Inspector Jackson said that Owen called at Mr. Willis's house that afternoon before he went out wit h the milk and told him about the occurrence.—Dr. O'Keeffe said that on Monday last week Owen was seen by his assist- ant, Dr. Ferries, and witness saw him on the Wednesday. The cause of death was septic pneumonia. There was a wound in deceased left nostril anderysipelas had set in, and it was the direct cause of the pneumonia which caused death. He had known the man for a good many years, and he was a perfectly healthy. sound man.—The Coroner: I suppose some people are more subject to erysipelas than others?—Dr. O'Keeffe : I would rather say that some people are more fortunate in not getting the microbes that produce the disease introduced into the wound.—A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>" TERRIER " AND HIS RIFLE.</head>
        <p>A " TERRIER " AND HIS RIFLE.</p>
        <p>'William Harrison (18), of 18, Barker-street. was charged at St. Helens on Monday with attempting to commit suicide with a rifle the previous day. Harrison belonged to the Territorials, and he had been shooting on the Saturday, and bad taken his rifle Lome with him. On Sunday aftetnocn he and his mother had a slight quarrel about his sweetheart. Ile went upstairs, and they soon afterwards beard a rifle shot. The police were informed, when it was found that the young man had discharged the rifle and that the bullet had gone through the bedroom wall of the adjoining house. The man said he only did it to frighten his mother. ills mother gave him a very good character, and said he waa a very good lad, except that he was a Lit hasty tempered.—The magistrates, after seriously warning him, bound him over in £5 for twelve months and ordered him to pay costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>LANCASHIRE'S NEW CHIEF CONSTABLE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE'S NEW CHIEF CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>Captain Charles Villiers Ibbetson, formerly of the 4th Royal Irish Dragoon Guards, was on Thursday, at a meeting of the Lancashire Standing Joint Committee, appointed Chief Constable of the County Palatine, at a salary commencing at £l,OOO per year. Captain Ibbetson joined the county constabulary as assistant Chief Constable in July, 1984, at a salary of .£OOO per year, and since the retirement of Colonel Moorsom three months ago he has officiated as acting Chief Constable,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>ELLERS smc3lE J _ ~</head>
        <p>ELLERS smc3lE J _ ~</p>
        <p>CLARKE'S BLOOD MIXTURE</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere In boxes. price 111 i (56 pills) I 4 219 (168 pills).</p>
      </div>
