      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Land Taxes.</head>
        <p>The Land Taxes.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The letter of Mentor in the last week's issue of the "Warrington Guardian "confuses the question through failing to discriminate between site values of land and land used for agriculture. The Budget does not concern itself with land, values of the latter character. It is impossible for Mentor or anyone else to say exactly what the rise in site values has been during the last thirty years but there is no doubt that it has been very great. Rough calculations have been made by various authorities, and taking an average of these it is reckoned that ,in London one-third of the gross assessment is land rent, that is rent for a value which is in no way, the result of any effort of the owner but is socially created. Londoners pay over fifteen millions per annum to the owners of the land for the right to build upon and occupy this land. Portions of this land may ,have changed hands again and again and it would be unjust to take from present owners past increase. In the Budget this is recog- nized, and what landowners are asked to do is when future increase is realized to hand back to society one-fifth of the value which society creates. We may talk all round this subject as we like, but it will be difficult to convince anyone of ordinary commonsense that. there is anything unjust in this or the like proposals. The "Statist" a few months ago plied a case in which a plot of vacant land containing 1,600 square feet (about as much as would be required for a work- man's cottage in the country) sold by auction for £3,000 per annum which calculated at twenty-five years purchase represents £75,000 or about £47 per square foot for the freehold of the vacant land. Cases have also been given in which land in London has realized up to £7O per square foot.lf this socially created value is not a fitting subject for taxation it is difficult to say what is</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER Penketh, September 14th, 1900.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>"A REAL LIVE MAN." Mr. Crosfield in the House.</head>
        <p>"A REAL LIVE MAN." Mr. Crosfield in the House.</p>
        <p>We have had, the privilege of seeing a letter written by a gentleman in London, who frequently visits the House of Commons, to a friend in Warrington. The letter touches upon the early debates on the Budget, and incidentally makes complimentary reference to Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. The writer says :—" I wish the Warring- ton people in general could see what a real live man they have in Mr. Crosfield (he seems to be a general favourite). He flits about from side to side of the House, first speaking to one and then another, and there is hardly an hour passes but what you see him in conversation with one of the Ministers. Last Thursday afternoon he came in with the Prime Minister. A short time after I noticed him on the Irish benches sitting between Messrs. John and Willie Redmond. A little later he was with T. P. O'Connor, and half-an-hour after with Keir Hardie, and then on the front Bench between John Burns and Winston Churchill. He might be one of the Whips. His proper place is between two very fine Liberals, Sir William Collins and Mr. Simon, K.C. So, for a new man he means business."</p>
        <p>LOCAL ASSESSMENTS. More Important Appeals. Several important appeals were heard at the meeting of the Warrington Assessment Committee which was held at the Workhouse yesterday, Mr. J. Billinge presiding. There were also present :—Messrs. G. Mather, A. Brooks, It. Bolton, W. Furness, E. L. Bent, J. Evans, R. Forshaw, A. Bottomley (clerk), R. R. Meacock (valuer), W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer for Warrington), Fairclough (Bcrtonwood), C. Cole (Newton), Dickenson (Haydock). MESSRS. ARMITAGE AND RIGBY, LTD. Mr. S. R. Armitage appealed on behalf of Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, Ltd., against the assessment of Cockhedge Mills at gross £5,575, rateable £3,575. Mr. Meacock said he would suggest a reduction of the assessment to gross £5,185, rateable £3,225. That would be a £502 net increase on the old assessment. The main reason of this reduction was that a portion of the works which had hitherto been profitably let was let no longer. Mr. Armitage thanked Mr. Meacock for the extremely courteous way in which on two occasions he had given him the opportunity of expressing his views without harrassment. At the same time he thought the figures could be reduced somewhat. He could not help feeling, with all due respect to Mr. Meacock's extended know- ledge of mill properties, that he had somewhat over- estimated the commercial value of Cockhedge Mills. Reference was made on the last occasion when he came before the Committee to the growing value of land and it was contended that their property had been enhanced in value. Mr. Armitage gave figures showing how cottage property in the neighbourhood had greatly decreased in value and he also pointed out that several works in the vicinity had been closed. He therefore considered it a fair inference that instead of increasing in value, the mill had depreciated so fares yardage pace was concerned.He suggested that £3,075 rateable would he a fair assessment. The cloud of bad trade had not entirely lifted, and they were only working partial time. There did not seem to be very much hope of speedily getting on to full time but the change might come any day and they hoped it would. The committee decided to recluce the assessment to gross £5,185' rateable value £3,125. , ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Artl;u'r.lir-o;;;43 appealed on behalf of Mr. Joseph Beecham against the assessment of the Royal Court Theatre at £5OO gross £42.5 rateable. He explained that certain alterations that were in progress were not really extensions but readjustments of the intetior, including the provision of a new tar and offices, which would add to the convenience of the patrons and management. On the recommendation of the valuer, the assessment was reduced to £425 gross, £375 rateable. LONGFORD WIRE CO., LTD.</p>
        <p>Mr. 0. Lofthouse appealed on behalf of this company against the assessment of their works at £2,140 gross, £1,390 rateable. He explained that certain parts of the works were not in use for the purposes for which they were built. The Valuer said he had been afforded every faci!ity in his inspection of the works' and he would recommend a reduction of the assessment to £l,BOO gross, £1,189 rateable. This was agreed to. HENRY KENYON Sc CO.</p>
        <p>This company was represented by Alderman Tinnion, who appealed against the assessment of £209 gross, £166 rateable. On the recommendation of the valuer, the assessment was reduced to .€2OO gross, £l2B rateable. FAIRCLOUGH &amp; SONS, LTD. Mr. John Fairclough represented this company, whose assessment stood at £1,425 gross, £1,601 rateable. After hearing a report from the valuer, the committee reduced the assessment to the old figures of £ll6O gross £BB6 rateable. RICHARD EVANS AND CO. LTD. Mr. J. Robinson appealed on behalf of this company against the assessment of deir premises as follows : Coal mines Scc., at Newton £5,500 gross, £2,500 rateable; coal mines at Barlonwood, £2,500 gross, £1,200 rateable ; land and buildings, plant and coal mines at Haydock, £32,000 gross; £14,227 rateable. The committee fired the assessment provisionally at £13,898 rateable ; pending further conference between the valuer and the firm. COLLINS GREEN COLLIERY CO.</p>
        <p>Mr. lit'Cri:iFarrclo—u-g-ii--iepre:e—n-ted--th-is -company who appealed against the assessment of their land, buildings. mines, water, etc. at Collins Green at £26,350 gross, £12,797 103. rateable. Having heard a report from the valuer the committee reduced the assessment to £12,942 gross £5,772 10s. rateable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>VULCAN FOUNDRY LTD,</head>
        <p>VULCAN FOUNDRY LTD,</p>
        <p>Mr. Dogget appfaled on behalf of this company against the assessment of their works and land at £5073 10s. gross, £3,573 10s. rateable. On the recommendation of the valuer, the committee reduced the figures to £4,793 102. gross, £3,373 10s. rateable.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.— WHITE &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw at., opposite top of Bold et., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. CHARLES RODDY.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. CHARLES RODDY.</p>
        <p>Many readers of the " Examiner " will regret to hear of the death of Mr. Charles Roddy, landlord of the Royal Arms, Bank-street, which occurred with painful suddenness on Monday morning last. It appears that Mr. Roddy left home apparently in his usual health, at about 9.30 for Arpley Station, intending to entrain for Lymm. Councillor Lord overtook him in Atpley- road and they walked slowly to the station,and engaged in conversation. They had reached the station entrance when, without any previous warning, Mr. Roddy collapsed. He staggered forward and Councillor Lord caught hold of him and prevented him from falling. Dr. Joseph, who happened to , be in the vicinity was quickly summoned to the scene but on examining Mr. Roddy he found life was exdinct,. The police ambulance .was sent for and the body was conveyed to deceased's home. Mr. Roddy had been treated for the past twelve months for heart trouble by Dr. Liston. He was born in the little town of Cloonlimney, Ireland, 54 years ago and at the age of 13 years came to this country and commenced work 'on a farm at Moss Side, Cheshire. Later he came to Warrington and was employed by the London and North-Western Railway Co. For 21 years he was licensee of the ,okl Bull's Head, which adjoined the Seven Stars in Bridge-street and then he held the licence of the Royal Arms for the past 12 years. In 18S6 he was in the neighbourhood of Bridge Foot and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>LATE MR. CHAS. RUDDY.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. CHAS. RUDDY.</p>
        <p>heard cries from the Mersey. He ran down and saw a little girl in the water and without divesting himself of his clothes jumped in and rescued her, His gallantly was rewarded by the silver medal and certificate of the Shipwreck Humane Society. Mr. Roddy was a member of the Council of the Warrington branch of the United Irish League and was a Past High Chief Ranger of the Irish National Foresters of which he was secretary for about 20 years. He was a member of the Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society, representing East Mayo and his keen Irish wit, earnestness and winning personality earned the respect of the members. He was a staunch Roman Catholic and was connected with St. Mary's Church. A strong believer in religious education he gave vent during the recent controversy to his conviction that a child should be taught the religion of its parents. It was a source of pride to him that he had been able to raise a con- siderable amount on behalf of Roman Catholic schools by means of pence subscribed by members of his fellow countrymen who visited his house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the body was taken to St. Mary's Church at 8.30 next morning a Requiem Mass was celebrated. Part of the funeral service was conducted at the church at 3 p.m.by the Rev. Father Basil Primavesi aud then the remains were conveyed to the Warrington Cemetery, where a large crowd had gathered to pay their tribute of respect. ne chief mourners were : John, Patrick, Foincis, Wilfred (sons), Mary, Annie, Nellie, Maggie ad Rosy (daughters), Mrs. Patrick Roddy (daughter-in-law), and Mies Rosy Roddy (grand- daughter). Representatives of various organisations were present. The Foresters were represented amongsl others by Messrs. W. Eagan, M. Devany, P. Mannion, T. Kelly, J. Morrissey, L'. Stanton, M. Rogerson, M. Morgan, P. Walsh and J. Jeffers. The Young Men's Catholic Society were represented by Messrs. M Cunningham, J. J. Black (also Parliamentary Debating Societ3), W. Boyle,T. Hughes and E. llession ; United It WI League, Dr. Liston and Edward Kelly ; Altrincham Irish National Foresters Friendly Society, Mr. P. Flannery. The hearers were Messrs. W. 14:agan, P. Mannion, P. Stanton, J. Drury, T. Kelly, A. Hansbury, M. Devany and M. Rogerson.</p>
        <p>REFERENCES. On Thursday at the usual weekly meeting of the War- rington Branch of The United Irish League, the chair- man, Mr. P. Conway,on behalf of the members expressed their sorrow at the loss they bad sustained through the death of Mr. Roddy. Ile had been a most enthusiastic and consistent Nationalist, and was a prominent mem- ber of the branch. A vote of condolence was extended to Mrs. Roddy and family in their sad loss Dr. Liston, Messrs. Jas. Fay and A. M. Crowe also referred to the death of their respected colleague in a feeling manner We have received the following : - At a special meet- ing of the National Union of Life Assurance Agents, a vote of condolence was unanimously passed. Mr. Roddy had been a member for some years, and was most highly respected by his fellow members. Although be held no office he was a most useful member. He faced all subjects with calmness, and his judgment was always well weighed. Mr. Roddy, we know for some time past has not enjoyed good health, but little did we think that we should have to part with him so soon. As recently as Friday week last he attended the branch meeting end took a very active part in the discussion. His place will he a difficult one to fill in our branch. His name will, however, he long remembered as a thorough Trades Unionist. To those whom he has left sorrowing we tender our heart- felt sympathies in their most sad bereavement.—Edwin Plinston. President ; George A. Leach, Vice-President; John Moorfleld, Secretary ; Harry Melbourne. Treasurer Warrir gtcn Branch N.U.L.A.A. .</p>
        <p>Owing to the delith of Mr. Roddy Councillor Toole's lecture to the United Irish League, which MIS to have bten delivered cn Sunday, has been postponed indefinitely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>HOWL EY WARD VACANCY. Mr. Howard Returned.</head>
        <p>HOWL EY WARD VACANCY. Mr. Howard Returned.</p>
        <p>The( vacancy in the representation of the Howley Ward in the Warrington Town Council caused through the lamented death of Councillor W.B.Roberts, was filled on Monday evening when Alderman James Evans announced to a large crowd assembled at the Town Hall that Mr. George Christopher Howard (independent), had a majority of 45 over Mr. Charles Dutton Parkinson (Conservative), the figures being 486 and 441 respectively. The by-election created much more than usual interest and a keen fight was witnessed. Mr. Howard had the earnest support of the Liberals and Mr. Parkinson had all the leading lights of the Tory party to assist him. A circular was addressed to the candidates by the Association of Foundation Managers of the Church of England Schools, Warrington, which asked : (1) "Are you prepared to carry out the Act of 1902 according to the purpose of its framers—namely, equality of treatment for all scheols—Voluntary and Council. (2) Are you in favour of carrying out the trust deeds as regards religious instruction according to the Act of 1902? We understand that both candi- dates answered these questions in the affirmative. During the day Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenallt Mr. and Mrs. Robert Pierpoint put in an appearance in the Ward.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the counting of the votes, Councillor HOWARD proposed a vote of thanks to the presiding officer for the manner in which he had con- ducted the election, and also to the Town Clerk and his staff for the courtesy he had received at their bands. He also thanked the Chief Constable and his staff for the work they had done, and he further wished to thank his opponent for his courtesy. Mr. Parkinson was a sportsman and a brick. Mr. PARKINSON had great pleasure in seconding the resolution.</p>
        <p>Alderman EVANS remarlied that there was really nothing to thank him for, because the work was done by the officials. Councillor HOWARD, speaking from the Town Hall steps, briefly thanked the electors of the Howley Ward for the honour they had coVerred upon him. RESULT ANNOUNCEb AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph HOWES announced the result of the con- test at the great Budget meeting in the Parr Hall. He said they bad had great magnates of the Tory party riding through some of the worst parts of the town, fraternising with the people in a manner which was not usual with them. They declared at the commencement of the contest that they must win the seat, as it was as important as a general election. One of the candidates was a Tory, and the other was not, and the gentleman who vas not a Tory was offered by the leading Tories a very favourable consideration if he would retire, but he refused. The result of the contest was that the Tory was beaten and Mr. Howard had been elected. (Applause)</p>
        <p>Photo</p>
        <p>H. Booth</p>
