      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>NOTED YACHT AT FIDDLER'S FERRY,</head>
        <p>A NOTED YACHT AT FIDDLER'S FERRY,</p>
        <p>The picture shows the yacht " Mimosa " locking out of the canal into the river. It was her first visit to the Ferry, and she is the largest cutter to navigate the upper reaches of the Mersey, being of 21 tons register. In the midnight race from Liverpool to the Isle of Man she finished fourth. The owners are Messrs. Roliertson and Tordoff, two Manchester gentlemen.</p>
        <p>_ -.-. • .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Shaw—Wilson.</p>
        <p>The first marriage to be solemnized in the new church of St. Margaret's and All Hallows, Orford, took place on Saturday, the bride being Miss Martha Ann Wilson, daughter of Mr. A. Wilson, of Orford, and the bridegroom Mr. Arthur James Shaw, of Orford-lane, Warrington. Although owing to the recent death of the bride's sister the ceremony was very quiet, great interest was taken in the event by the villagers, and a large congregation assembled. The bride is a teacher in the Sunday school and is much esteemed in the village. The Vicar (Rev. (I. Vale Owen) conducted the service. The bride, who was • given away by her father, was attired in a cream serge costume with bat to match. The bridesmaid, Miss Ida Wilson (sister of the bride) wore a white needlework dress of mercerized lawn, trimmed with insertion, and her ornament was a gold signet ring (the gift of the bridegroom). Mr. J. Mather acted as best man. Later in the day the happy couple left for Scarborough, where i the honey- moon s being spent. The many handsome presents included a handsomely bound book, "The life of Christ," from the Sunday school; a large family Bible from the Vicar. (Rev. G. Vale Owen) ; _an oak biscuit barrel from the teachers of the Sunday school, and a marble clock and bronzes 'from Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co. (tube department).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Sumner —Wells.</head>
        <p>Sumner —Wells.</p>
        <p>A wedding of considerable &lt;local interest was solemnized on Thursday at the Parish Church, when Mr. Wa'ter Sumner, of the Town Clerk's -department, son of Mr. and Mrs. Robert Sumner, Orford-lane; was married to Miss Emily Wells, daughter of Mil. and Mrs. Henry Wells, Clegg-street. The officiating clergyman was the Rev..A. J. Bratt. The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in an ivory silk poplin de chine dress, and wore a veil surmounted with orange blossom She also carried a shower'bouquet of lilies, tulips and roses. The brides- maids were Misses Lily and Polly Wells (sisters of the bride) and Misses Cissie and Polly Sumner (sisters of the bridegroom.) Their dresses were of old rose, silk eolienne, and they wore black picture hats. They carried bouquets of sweet peas and carnations;- and each of them had gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr, Arthur Sumner (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man. The reception was held in St. George's Mission rooms, a large number of guests attending. Later in the day the happy couple left for London and the South Coast, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was of green Scotch tweed with hat to match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>COUNTY REVISION COURTS.</head>
        <p>COUNTY REVISION COURTS.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clement Elphinstone Lloyd, Revising Barrister, held a Revision Court in the large Hall, grappenhall, on Thursday, to revise the voter's list of the townships of Grappenball and Latchford Without. Mr. Will Ford represented the Liberals and Messrs. G.Duckworth and J. H. Dean the Conservatives. The Assistant overseers present were Messrs. C. H. Hoult (Grappenhall) and Joseph Smith (Litchford Without). Nothlng of public interest took place. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clement Elphinstone Lloyd, Revising Barrister, held a Revi....ion Court in the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, on Thursday, for the townships of Stock ton Heath, Warrington, Welton Inferior, Walton Superior, and Appleton and Stretton. Mr. W. Ford represented the Liberals and Messrs. G. Duckworth and J. H. Dean the Conservatives. The assistant overseers present were Messrs. Ellison, (Stretton and Appleton), E. Bradshaw (Stockton Heath), Molesdale (Walton Inferior), Joseph Whitfield (Walton Inferior), Lawrence on behalf of Mr. W. H. Woodcock (Warrington). _ .</p>
        <p>No business of public- interest transpired. At the close of the proceedings the Revising Barrister highly complimented Mr. Bradshaw on the excellence of the Stockton Heath list, add his remarks were endorsad by the political agents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. JAMES NEWTON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JAMES NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will regret to liear of the death of Mr. James Newton, Orford-lane, who passed away, somewhat suddenly on Saturday after several weeks illness. His cheery presence will be much missed in the town and also in the surrounding district, where he was well known and respected. Mr. Newton, who was fifty years of age, was a native of Warring- ton, and rather curious to state, he died in the house in which he was born, and in which he had lived practically all his life. For the past thirty years he had carried cn the business of a farming contractor and was the proprietor of the Twiggeiy. He leaves a widow and a geown-up family of sons and daughters. The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Tuesday in the presence of a large gather- ing of relatives and friends. The Rev. B. Percival, Vicar of St. Peter's, officiated. The chief mourners were Messrs. Joseph, James and George Newton (sons), Mr. George Newton (brother), Messrs. Steel Mannion, Owen and Mullineaux (brothers-in-law), and Mr. Berry (father- in-law). The bearers were Messrs. J. Littlefair, J. Hamblett, Edward Caldwell, T. Jackson, T. Cooke and S. Ellison. Floral tributes were sent by the fol- lowing :—The widow and family, Mr. Short, Mr. and Mrs: Lawton, "Jim, Lizzie and Clara Miller," Mr. and Mrs. Holland, Mr. and Mrs. Davies (Penketh), Mr. Joseph Sudlow, "Mary Ann and George" (brother and sister), employes Hutchings' Tanyard, Mr. Barnard, Mr. and Mrs. John Hamblett, Mr. and Mrs. Daintith, Mr. and Mrs. Dan Cooper, Mr and Mrs. Allison (Cemetery Hotel), Mr. Caldwell and family, "Emma and Tom" (sister and brother), Mr. and Mrs, Mainwaring, Mr. S. Wakefield. Mr. E. L. Eaton, Mr. S. Hewitt, Mr. and Mrs. R. C. Shaw, Mr. John Sudlow, employees of Gartons, Ltd., Mr. J. Drummond, Mr. W. G. Scragg and family, Mr. James Grounds and family, Mr. and Mrs. James Robinson (Rixton Hall), Mrs. Massey and Mrs. •Birch, Mr. and Mrs. James Blundell (Rixton), Mr. and Mrs. Hughes, Mr. and Mrs, Daintith (Martinscroft), Mr. and Mrs. Sutton, tenants of Orford Avenue, Mr. and Mrs. Cooper (Blue Bell Inn), Mr. and Mrs. Sheldon,- Mr. and Mrs. W. C. Brooks, Old Twiggery Farm, "Lizzie and George (niece and nephew). "Polly, John and Jim Sudlow."</p>
        <p>WIDNES, ST. HELENS AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. WARRINGTON THURSDAY V. Sr. HELENS TRADERS This match was played on Thursday on Padgate-lane ground before a fair number of spectators, being the opening match of the season for Warrington. It was a keenly contested match all through butwith no score at half-time. The St. Helens team were credited with a very doubtful goal in the latter part of the game which ended with the score, St. Helens Traders, 1 goal; Warrington Thursday, nil. Next Thursday, Warrington Thursday v. St. Helens Tramways. Walkers' Ground, Padgate-lane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>READY FOR THE FIGHT.</head>
        <p>READY FOR THE FIGHT.</p>
        <p>Premier says Commons must Rule, --- - AUDIENCES OF 13,000.</p>
        <p>TRAIN LOAD OF PEERS AND M.P. S ATTEND.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the thoughts all politicians were directed towards Birmingham, where the Prime Minister, flanked by a host of his supporters in Parlia- ment, delivered the counterblast to Lord Rosbery's speech at Glasgow. An extraordinary amount of interest was aroused over the Premier's visit to the ctfpital of the Midlands, and one has to go hack in memory a longtime to recall an occasion when the preparations for a public meeting were on a scale of completeness and lavishness comparable with the arrangements made for the Bingley Hall and Curzon Hall functions. In the first-named building there was a gathering of no less than 10.000 people. The Prime Minister himself had something in the nature of a triumphal progress from London. He travelled in a luxurious special train to Birmingham. attended by a company of Liberal peers and M.P.'s numbering close upon 100.</p>
        <p>The chief meeting in the Bingley Hall was presided over by that well-known Birmingham Liberal, Mr. Arthur Chamberlain. Mr. Asquith was accompanied on the platform by Mrs. Asquith, Miss Asquith and Mr. Herbert Asquith.</p>
        <p>• [BIRMINGHAM, FRIDAY. MR. ASQUITH, addressing a vast audience, said that that large representative audience had come to say that in settling both the measure and incidence of the Budget the representatives of the people should have the final and determining voice. (" Down with the Lords !") Lord Rosebery had contented himself with unfertile generalities. They must turn to another quarter for the answer to the practical question: What was the alternative? Neither nations nor individ- uals could live for long on a diet of blood and thunder. They could not raise revenue out of a string of perorations. The time had gone by for Mr. Balfour's ambiguities and reservations.</p>
        <p>The question was in everybody's mind: what was going to happen to the Budget? Whether the lords mutilated or rejected it mattered not. If it put aside the provision which the House of Commons made for the financial interests of the State that would in- deed be a revolution the most formidable since the days of the Long Parliament. There was no rule more deeply ingrained in our constitution, more solemnly hallowed by precedent, more plainly sanctioned by trad- ing and present requirements than that in matters of finance the Commons as repre- sentatives of the people should have an abso- lute, unquestionable, decisive voice. (cheers). That right was recognised even by Lord Salisbury and Mr. Balfour. Was that right to be put for the first time for three hundred years in jeopardy, and the machinery of the country to be brought to profound and far- reaching chaos? That way lay revolution. If it was seriously threatened it would in- volve issues wider and more far-reaching than the right of the House of Lords to meddle with finance. "I say for myself and for the Liberal party we are ready" (applause) "we are not only ready but anxious, not only anxious but eager to take up the challenge." (Great cheering).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>MAYORALTY, Alderman Forsiiv7r—Again Accepts</head>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY, Alderman Forsiiv7r—Again Accepts</p>
        <p>A caucus meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held at the Town Hall on Thursday evening to consider the selection of Mayor for the ensuing year, There (WilS an attendance of nineteen and Alderman Smethurst (deputy mayor) was voted to the chair. Alderman Tinnion proposed and Alderman Burton seconded that Alderman Forshaw, the retiring Mayor, be asked to accept office for another year. There was considerable discussion, several members re-iterating their grievanee with regard to the Mayor's attitude on questions arising out of the -Royal visit. Eventually the motion was carried by 10 votes to 7. A com- mittee consisting of the Deputy-Mayor, Aldermen Tinnion and Burton, and Councillor Roberts was appointed to acquaint the Mayor with the decision of the meeting and to invite him to accept office again. His Worship accepted the invitation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>ROLLER SKATING. The New Rink at Warrington.</head>
        <p>ROLLER SKATING. The New Rink at Warrington.</p>
        <p>L?</p>
        <p>The Empire abating rink, which is in course of con- struction near Trinity schools, Warrington, will be opened at an early date. It will be a magnificent build- ing with an ideal floor and a well-furnished lounge, and it will be conducted on up-to-date lines. Carnivals, gymktnas. hockey matches, &amp;c., will be held at intervals. There will be excellent accommodation for non-skaters and it is claimed that the rink will be the finest and best equipped out of London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PALATINE RINK.</head>
        <p>THE PALATINE RINK.</p>
        <p>The proprietors of the Palatine Rink, Wilderspool- road, intend to still further improve the accommoda- tion for their numerous patrons by adding 60 feet to the floor, making 230 feet of skating length. The rink will then be the longest in the north of England. Important reductions.in prices, commencing on Monday next, are announced. A .Recreation and Hockey Club is being forded, and special concessions and reduced prices will be made to patrons. Next week there will be an 8-lap race each evening. Full particulars appear in our advertising columns.</p>
        <p>Rev M. Ryan Leaving Warrington. The Rev. M. Ryan, priest-in-charge of the Church of the Sacred Heart, Warrington, is about to leave the town, having been appointed to the church of Our Lady Star of the Sea, St, Anne's-on-Sea. Father Ryan has carried on a quiet but very successful work at the Church of the Sacred Heart ever since its estab- lishment as a small mission about seventeen years ago. As an outcome of his labours the present handsome church and the adjoining schools were erected and much progress has been made in the various sections of the church's activities. Father Ryan is a useful member of the Board of Guardians and his departure from Warrington will be re.gretted not only by his own flock, by whom he is held m great affection, but by members of other religious ' bodies who esteem him for his kindly and genial manner and his untiring work in the Bank Quay district. Father Ryan will be succeeded by the Rev. W. Roddiffe, of St. Marie's, Widnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>BUDGET IN THE VILLAGES.</head>
        <p>THE BUDGET IN THE VILLAGES.</p>
        <p>MR. R. L OUTHWAITE'S EXPERIENCES IN CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>[By R. L. Outhwaitel</p>
        <p>The village open-air meeting is tiva prime test as to whether any political question has gripped the nation. Men are not drawn to them by the magic of a name, for the great man does not speak upon the green, and if they come it is becauw they are attracted by the subject to be discussed. I have just been at a series of village meetings in Cheshire, and what I have seen and heard •has afforded convincing, proof that the people upon the soil have instinctively seized upon the salient fact that in the land clauses of the •Budget there..is at least a germ of hope of emancipation for themselves.</p>
        <p>Never at the hotly-contested by-elections before the last General Election have I seen such attendances, such enthusiasm. Taking six meetings, attended by at least 1,200 people, mostly labourers and small farmers, only one dissentient declared himself on the vote. The ,fact of the dukes and the great territorial magnates having cursed the land clauses of the Budget has</p>
        <p>LIT THE FLAME OF REVOLT</p>
        <p>upon the countryside, and on opportunity is now pre- sented to the Government to deal with the Lords and the land that may not come again for generations, if it be not grasped. These villagers need no instruction on the importance of making land accessible to the people, and it is a cause for regret that• the Budget does not provide a tax to force locked-up rural acres into use, as it does one to so affect urban lands.</p>
        <p>This rural division of Cheshire provides lessons in the land question which, as I found them of value to myself, I think may interest reade• s of "The Daily News." I have described on many occasions</p>
        <p>TILE DESOLATION OF TILE COUNTRYSIDE where the great monopolist has debarred the labourers from obtaining a foothold on the soil, and for the first time I have seen a district where a considerable num- ber of small holders have managed to establish them- selves. Nct thanks to the policy of the landowners, but because when they seized upon thousands of acres of . commons, certain rights of squatters seem to have led to their acquiring small areas in lease or freehold, so driving along a country road with a farmer of wide experience as guide and informant, I saw an unwonted sight. For miles we passed small holding after small holding, and the signs of contentment and prosperity were self- evident. There were trim cottages set in gardens blazing with flowers; surrounding them I saw more fruit trees in an hour's drive than I saw when driving for days over half a million, ducal acres. Oat crops that would yield 70 bushels to the acre; heavy crops of cabbage that have followed on the early potato crops, small fields Of pasture of luxuriant growth, and not a rood of ground that did not betoken having been called upon to produce with loving care, and I asked my farmer friend, who knew them, how these men maintained themselves on their holdings, averaging about 15 to 20 acres. •</p>
        <p>A MAN WHO HAS 42.IADE 'A FORTUNE. HIMSELF</p>
        <p>from the soil, he knew how these smaller brethren had fared. " Passing one holding of about 20 acres, as well equipped as many a farm I have seen of a thou- sand acres, he remarked: "The man who rented that holding for years died lately worth £1,400. It is wonderful what these men save. I know several who hive left substantial sums up to £7OO apiece." He told me of the manner in which these small men earn their livelihood. In addition to the crops, there is the butter from a few cows to sell, pigs for the butcher, and some buy a few young calves in May for £1 10s. a head and sell them next May at from £6 to £B, when prices are good, thus making their rents. In many different ways, the intelligent man, when he has a foothold on the soil, can make money by buying and selling, and this is what these small holders do. This district, so human and prosperous, was as</p>
        <p>A REVELATION OF WHAT ENGLAND WILL BE</p>
        <p>when the monopolist gives place to the man with the hoe. Then suddenly we came upon a waste covered with bracken. What is this ? " I asked my. friend He replied : "That is the game preserve of a lord who is trying tc make money by acquiring land in Africa, instead of giving people a chance to make money for him on his land at home." Then he chuckled and said: "Do you see that rising ground? Business men from Manchester would like to get that land to build residences upon it. Will it come under the halfpenny tax? and what will his lordship do then ?" Probably say that agriculture is ruined because his games preserve is taxed. Then I put a final question to my informant. I asked him how the existence of small holdings affected wages. He told me that the Email holders mostly worked for them- selves, or co-operatively, helping one another. When they worked for farmers they proved themselves to be exceptionally intelligent workers, and got high wages. The average wage of an agricultural labourer in the district is from 15s. to £1 a week, with privileges Something better, this, than the 12s. t 9 14s. a week, I found ruling on the 1,000-acre farms on Earl Pembroke's 40,000-acre holding in Wiltshire, with the privilege of being kicked out of the cottages rented at 2s. a week at a week's notice. And I have no doubt that</p>
        <p>TIIE EXISTENCE OF SMALL HOLDINGS</p>
        <p>has contributenn this district to the existence of the higher wage rate. ' And it is these small holders and the agricultural labourers who have been attending Budget meetings, eager for a conflict with the Lords on the question of the land, and who are taking to. themselves the words of Mr. Lloyd George: " With your help we can brush the Lords like chaff before us."—" Daily News."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Building 'Scheme.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, the Rev. T. R. Matthews presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Simpson, Dr. Murray, Messrs.G. Mather, J. Billinge9 A. Brooks, R. Bolton, W. C. Brooks, W. Furness, 11. Lee, J. A. Rushworth, W. Flannagan, M.W. Setou, T. Ritchie, J. Evans, R. Forshaw, A. Bottornley (clerk), R. W. Peacock (assistant clerk), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate Cottage Homes).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>INDISPOSED PIG.</head>
        <p>INDISPOSED PIG.</p>
        <p>The reading of the following report on the House Committee's minutes evoked considerable amusement. The veterinary surgeon had to te called in on Sunday, to a pig which was previously ill. It is not out of danger yet. (Laughter). Will the Board authorise the payment of the fee? The necessary authority was given. THE NEW BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote transmitting copies of an Order authorising the Guardians to erect a.building at the Workhouse for the accommodation of the aged and infirm inmates and to provide furniture for the building, at a cost not exceeding £6,657. The Order also authorised the Guardians to borrow the amount and to repay the loan within a period not exceeding thirty years. The CLERK said they could now go on with the pro- posed building. He had informed the architect and builder, so that no time would be lost. The matter was referred to the Building Comm ittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-18_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>LABOUR FARM.</head>
        <p>A LABOUR FARM.</p>
        <p>The CLEnx said he had received a letter from the Christian Social Service Union asking if the Board wished to send any men to their labour farm at Marple. The Clerk said the object of the Union was to train and reclaim "unemployable men and it was reported that they had been very successful in the work. The cost of maintenance at the farm was 12i. each per week.</p>
        <p>Mr. EVANS asked what was the outcome of tbe farm training.</p>
        <p>The CLERK: They teach the men farming and when they think they are able to be trusted they send them out to Carrida. When they get to Canada they are free.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. BROOKS: How long wcu!cl we have to pay 12s. a week?</p>
        <p>The CLERK: Until such time as they think the man is fit to start for himself.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROOMS: Have they given any statistics of the time generally taken? The CLERK: I cannot say that they have, but I know of the case of a young man who went to one of the farms and who was chargeable to the Union between two or three years. Mr. EVANS said he could quite conceive that there might be something in the scheme which would con- siderably relieve the Guardians of the country generally. If they had a person who was likely to be a trouble to them all the days of his life and they could send him to the farm and from there to Canada where he would be placed on his own resources and made to get his living, there was something worth consideration in the proposal. Mr. MATHER moved that a reply be sent that the Board did not consider they had any suitable cases for the farm. • Mr. W. C. BROOKS seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. FurtsEss said tbat if there were inmates who were fit to be taught farming and who could work, they ought not to be harboured in that institntien. Ultimately it was decided, by 11 vbtes to 5, that the letter be referred to the House Committee.</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
